\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Ohio: How to Vote, Where to Vote and What's on the Ballot\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alyce McFadden \par \uc2 474 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 On Election Day, voters in Ohio will decide on two ballot measures: one on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and one on the recreational use of marijuana.\par \par \uc2 Ohio voters will decide Tuesday on ballot measures that would add the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the state's constitution and legalize the recreational use of marijuana.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters have been on a winning streak with ballot measures across the country, but their battle in Ohio may be particularly tough.\par \par \uc2 Here's what else to know:\par \par \uc2 How to vote\par \par \uc2 In-person voting in Ohio will take place from 6:30 a.m. to 7:30 p.m. Eastern time on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Voters must bring a valid form of identification, like a driver's license or state identification card, to their polling sites. A law that went into effect this year changed what kinds of identification may be used to vote in Ohio, requiring voters to show photo IDs at the polls. Forms of identification like utility bills or pay stubs are no longer eligible.\par \par \uc2 The secretary of state's website maintains a full list of acceptable types of ID.\par \par \uc2 Only Ohio residents who have already registered to vote may cast a ballot; the deadline for new voters to register for this election has passed. Voters who are not sure if they are registered can check by entering their name into Ohio's Voter Search tool.\par \par \uc2 Where to vote\par \par \uc2 Voters can find their polling place selecting their county and home address on the secretary of state's website.\par \par \uc2 It's too late to return an absentee ballot by mail, but registered voters who received one but did not mail it before the Monday deadline may still deliver it by hand to their local board of elections.\par \par \uc2 What's on the ballot\par \par \uc2 Ohio voters will weigh in on two ballot initiatives Tuesday. The first, State Issue 1, would amend the state constitution to protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state. The amendment does not place a particular limit on when in a pregnancy an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may occur, though it would allow lawmakers to restrict abortions after viability in separate legislation after the initiative is passed.\par \par \uc2 The other measure on the ballot, State Issue 2, would legalize the sale and use of marijuana by Ohioans who are 21 or older. The drug is legal for medical use in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Voters can find a sample ballot for their county, complete with local elections, on the Ohio secretary of state's website.\par \par \uc2 A voter in Ohio during a special election in August. Voters in the state will now decide whether to amend the state constitution to protect access to \uc2 .  (PHOTOGRAPH BY Maddie McGarvey for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231107ejb70004r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
