\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Is on the Ballot For Elections on Tuesday\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer and Shane Goldmacher \par \uc2 1,805 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Elections in Kentucky, Ohio and Virginia will give an early preview of how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will shape the political landscape in 2024, and the effectiveness of both parties' approaches.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has emerged as a defining fault line of this year's elections, with consequential contests in several states on Tuesday offering fresh tests of the issue's political potency nearly 18 months after the Supreme Court ended a federal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The decision overturning Roe v. Wade scrambled American politics in 2022, transforming a longstanding social conflict into an electoral battering ram that helped drive Democrats to critical victories in the midterm races. Now, as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and bans in red states have become reality, the issue is again on the ballot, both explicitly and implicitly, in races across the country.\par \par \uc2 In Kentucky, Democrats are testing whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can provide a political advantage even in a red state, as Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , a Democrat, has used the state's near-total ban on abortions -- which was triggered by the fall of Roe -- to bludgeon his Republican opponent as an extremist. In Ohio, a socially conservative state, a ballot question that would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the State Constitution will measure the extent of the country's political pivot toward \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 And in Virginia, the only Southern state without an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, Gov. Glenn Youngkin, a Republican, is trying to flip the script in the state's legislative elections, casting Democrats as ''extreme'' and saying his party supports a ''common-sense position'' -- a 15-week ban.\par \par \uc2 The contests give an early preview of how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will shape the political landscape in next year's presidential and congressional elections -- and the effectiveness of both parties' approaches.\par \par \uc2 Strategists across the political spectrum agree that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains highly energizing for the Democratic coalition, particularly in states where Republicans could pass further restrictions. In Pennsylvania, where the parties are battling over a State Supreme Court seat, even a gun control advocacy group began ads backing the Democratic candidate by raising alarms about the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights -- a tacit nod to the issue's resonance.\par \par \uc2 ''It's still a very, very powerful issue to folks, both in terms of motivating Democrats to vote and as a very fruitful persuasion issue for swing voters,'' said Angela Kuefler, a longtime Democratic pollster working on the proposed Ohio amendment.\par \par \uc2 What remains less clear is how far into conservative areas Democrats' arguments will be effective and whether Republicans can deflect some of the attacks.\par \par \uc2 That's what Republicans are trying to do in Virginia, where G.O.P. candidates like State Senator Siobhan Dunnavant, an OB-GYN running in one of the state's most hotly contested races for a newly redrawn seat, have aired numerous ads on the issue.\par \par \uc2 In one ad, she says, ''I don't support an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban,'' even though she supports a 15-week ban on the procedure with exceptions for rape, incest, the woman's health and cases of several fetal anomalies. She argues that a 15-week restriction is not a ban but rather ''legislation that reflects compassionate common sense.''\par \par \uc2 ''Every Republican in a swing district knows the Democrat playbook that's going to be run against them,'' said Liesl Hickey, a Republican strategist and ad maker working on the race. ''The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue can either define you, or you can define it in your campaign.''\par \par \uc2 Since Roe was overturned, Democrats have prevailed in six out of six ballot measures that put the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  straight to voters. This year, national groups backing both sides have poured tens of millions of dollars into the Ohio contest, transforming an off-year ballot measure into one of the most important races this fall.\par \par \uc2 A victory in Ohio would provide further fuel for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights efforts next year. That will be especially true in pivotal battleground states where campaigns are already underway, including Arizona, Florida and Missouri, said Amy Natoce, a spokeswoman for Protect Women Ohio, a group founded by national anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups to oppose the amendment.\par \par \uc2 ''We know that all eyes are on Ohio right now,'' she said. ''States that are considering similar constitutional amendments are looking to us.''\par \par \uc2 In Kentucky, Mr. Beshear is further testing the limits of where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can mobilize a Democratic coalition. Since Roe ended, the state has become engulfed in a political battle over how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be regulated. A trigger law that took effect immediately after the decision banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in nearly all circumstances, except to save the life of the woman or prevent severe injury. Efforts by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to block the ban in court were denied.\par \par \uc2 Last fall, voters rejected a ballot measure that would have amended the state's Constitution to ensure that no right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the document.\par \par \uc2 In his campaign ads, Mr. Beshear has focused on how his Republican opponent, \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2 , supports a near-total ban.\par \par \uc2 The Beshear campaign has aired some of the cycle's most searing spots, including a straight-to-camera testimonial from a woman who was raped as a child by her stepfather. She says in the ad that Mr. Cameron would force child victims to carry the babies of their rapists.\par \par \uc2 ''We have the most extreme law in the country, where victims of rape and incest, some as young as 9 years old, have no options,'' Mr. Beshear said this past week in Richmond, Ky. ''The people of Kentucky have enough empathy to believe that those little girls ought to have options.''\par \par \uc2 After the ad aired, Mr. Cameron, the state attorney general, flipped his position and said that he would support carving an exception in state law in instances of rape or incest. Even if Mr. Beshear wins re-election, he would most likely struggle to change the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law because Republicans control the Legislature.\par \par \uc2 Courtney Norris, a spokeswoman for Mr. Cameron, said in a statement, ''Andy mischaracterizes and flat-out lies about Daniel's position on a number of issues in an attempt to deflect attention away from his failures as governor and his extreme record on this issue.''\par \par \uc2 Still, not every Democrat running in a red state has embraced Mr. Beshear's approach. Just as in the midterms, when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  benefited Democrats most in states like Arizona and Michigan, where the right to the procedure was directly at risk, Democrats are leveraging the issue race by race.\par \par \uc2 In Mississippi, Brandon Presley, the Democratic candidate for governor, has promoted his ''pro-life'' stance in television ads and has focused on issues like Medicaid expansion. And Shawn Wilson, a Democratic who lost the race for governor in Louisiana last month, said he was personally ''pro-life.'' Both are deeply conservative states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned in almost all circumstances.\par \par \uc2 In Virginia, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal through the second trimester, Republicans are the ones mitigating their approach. Mr. Youngkin has tried to be proactive in his messaging on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , promising to sign a 15-week ban if he and his Republican allies take over both chambers of the Legislature.\par \par \uc2 Such a policy would have significant implications for the entire region, because Virginia has become a destination for patients across the South seeking the procedure. Currently, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal in the state until nearly 27 weeks, and afterward if needed to save the life of the woman.\par \par \uc2 Most doctors say there is no medical basis for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cutoff at 15 weeks of pregnancy. Nor would it stop the vast majority of abortions, given that more than 93 percent happen before that stage in pregnancy, according to data collected by the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 . But 15 weeks is the point at which many polls indicate that a majority of Americans would support restrictions.\par \par \uc2 That's one of the reasons Mr. Youngkin's political committee has spent $1.4 million on ads pushing what the spots call a ''reasonable'' 15-week limit and accusing Democrats of disinformation as a heartbeat can be heard in the background. ''Here's the truth: There is no ban,'' the narrator says.\par \par \uc2 National Republican strategists have been pushing that message as well, urging their candidates to embrace a 15-week ban and exceptions in cases of rape, incest and risks to the physical health of the woman -- all relatively popular positions with the general public.\par \par \uc2 Zack Roday, a top political adviser to Mr. Youngkin, said Republicans were trying to reclaim and redirect the extremist label. He said Republicans needed to proactively neutralize that attack and create a ''permission structure'' for voters who are wary of G.O.P. candidates' stances on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but like their approach to other issues.\par \par \uc2 ''They understand 40 weeks, no limits is extreme,'' Mr. Roday said. ''We're trying to reclaim and bat that down. Because when you do, the voters will look at you more broadly.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats say there are significant complications to Mr. Youngkin's strategy. Polls show that a plurality of voters dislike the Republican approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. In private meetings and research memos, even some Republican strategists have urged their candidates to move away from the ''pro-life'' label, saying that many Americans now equate the term with support for a total ban.\par \par \uc2 Celinda Lake, a Democratic pollster who worked for \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr\uc2 .'s campaign in 2020, said that voters tended to see the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a fight over personal autonomy, and were less interested in litigating a number of weeks or specific exceptions.\par \par \uc2 ''Before Dobbs, people were very willing to entertain exceptions and restrictions,'' she said. ''Now they are much less open to that conversation because they just think there's a bigger fundamental point here.''\par \par \uc2 She added, ''The fundamental freedom to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been taken away, and we want to guarantee that right.''\par \par \uc2 State Senator Scott Surovell, the campaign chairman of the Virginia Senate's Democratic caucus, said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained the No. 1 issue driving people to vote.\par \par \uc2 When Mr. Surovell first heard that Mr. Youngkin's operation was planning to spend more than $1 million on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ads, he said he felt like what ''the Union troops thought at Gettysburg,'' when the Confederate army made a famously ill-fated charge.\par \par \uc2 ''You're going to try to charge us here?'' he said. ''They're going to try to attack us while we're on the high ground here?''\par \par \uc2 Reid J. Epstein contributed reporting from Richmond, Ky.\par \par \uc2 Reid J. Epstein contributed reporting from Richmond, Ky.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Glenn Youngkin of Virginia and other Republicans are trying to flip the script in legislative elections, supporting a 15-week \uc2  ban. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CARLOS BERNATE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231107ejb700035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
