\par\par \uc2 MICHELLE COTTLE\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 Can Glenn Youngkin Save The G.O.P. From Trump?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Cottle \par \uc2 2,535 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It's a perfect fall weekend in Virginia horse country, about two weeks before Election Day, and \uc2 the American Legion\uc2  hall in Middleburg is decked out for a rally featuring Gov. Glenn Youngkin, who is not on the ballot but is stumping hard for his fellow Republicans. His name is everywhere: on a bright blue backdrop behind the stage, on the swag in the front room, on the side of a bus out front with the slogan ''Strengthening the Spirit of Virginia Together.'' The bus is a high-end prop for Mr. Youngkin's ''Secure Your Vote'' tour, which has him crisscrossing the state to promote early voting. His attempt to reverse Republican mistrust of early and absentee voting is one way the governor stands apart from the leader of his party, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But it is not the only way.\par \par \uc2 Looking over the crowd, you can't help but notice a dearth of Trump paraphernalia. One woman has on a blue ''Nikki Haley for president'' vest, and another one is rocking a ''Moms for Liberty'' T-shirt. Virginia's Republican base has plenty of Trump love, yet it's not a visibly MAGA-rific gathering. This makes a certain political sense: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won this county in 2020, as did Mr. Youngkin's Democratic rival in 2021. But the fact that Mr. Youngkin is aggressively campaigning in blue areas is not only a sign of his popularity, it differentiates him from Mr. Trump, who largely sticks to safe conservative spaces.\par \par \uc2 As Mr. Youngkin bounds into the hall in his signature red vest -- smile beaming, cheeks ruddy from the wind -- he radiates the upbeat, hunky-P.T.A.-dad vibe that helped carry him to victory in 2021. His voice ranges from an urgent whisper to a gargly rasp as he raves not about his personal grievances or some vision of American carnage, but about the ''common sense'' plans he and his party have for Virginia. He spotlights a handful of policy areas -- jobs, tax relief, crime, mental health care, education -- and contends that Republicans, and Virginians, ''win'' when sensible people come together. Mr. Youngkin's sales pitch casts the G.O.P. as a party filled with practical folks who want to get stuff done -- as opposed to the Democrats, he charges, who ''just want to sell fear.''\par \par \uc2 Remember that ''fear'' line. It's revealing about Mr. Youngkin's brand of politics, but it's also about as edgy as the guy gets. His performance is a far cry from MAGA.\par \par \uc2 American politics is pretty bleak these days, with the House hobbling out of a month of historic dysfunction, with everyone exhausted by polarization and inflation and now the turmoil over the war in Gaza. The race for the presidency looks like the same old, same old; the country feels a bit beaten down. But the flip side of disillusionment is this: Americans are hungry for winners. And no cohort is hungrier than the Republican Party, which looks increasingly out of control as it contends with multiple epicenters of chaos. (See: House hard-liners. See: government shutdown threats and foreign-aid fights. See: \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , George Santos and the once-and-possibly-future president himself.) Such are the fruits of Trumpism, with its glorification of disruption and middle-finger truculence.\par \par \uc2 In Tuesday's elections, Mr. Youngkin has a shot -- albeit a long one -- at emerging a big winner. If he manages to help turn the Virginia General Assembly solid red, it will be considered a victory not only for the state party but also for his style of leadership, so different from the Trump version. Mr. Trump may be leading the G.O.P. presidential field, but Republicans haven't entirely forgotten that he dragged them to defeat in 2018 (the House), 2020 (the presidency and the Senate) and 2022 (those winnable Senate and governor's races with Trump-backed losers).\par \par \uc2 For all his trademark swagger, the MAGA king hasn't been a winner in a while. His presumed successor, Mr. DeSantis, despite his Florida triumphs, is looking more like a big meatball, and the rest of the G.O.P. pack has yet to energize voters. This leaves an opening for someone like Mr. Youngkin, if he can offer a winning playbook -- and if enough Republicans decide they want an affable version of conservatism without all the crazy Trump trimmings.\par \par \uc2 This is no easy feat: The governor is selling something softer than a MAGA vision, but he's not exactly moderate. His agenda contains plenty of harsh conservative elements, including anti-trans measures and his call for a 15-week ban on most abortions. Will enough people in the center actually want that, even if he swaddles those hard edges in feel-good vibes? And will enough people on the right want someone genial-verging-on-boring when they remain in thrall to the fire and fury of Mr. Trump?\par \par \uc2 Since his surprise victory in 2021 -- in which he led Republicans in capturing every statewide office and flipping the House of Delegates from blue to red -- the governor has been talked about as a presidential contender by those eager to move the party past Mr. Trump. \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  pressed him to run in this election cycle. \uc2 Wilbur Ross\uc2 , the former commerce secretary, threw him a schmoozefest in the Hamptons. Big-money donors have dumped millions into his PAC. Some heavy hitters, aghast at the weakness of existing Trump challengers, are still hoping Mr. Youngkin will jump into the 2024 fray. As their dream scenario goes, if he succeeds in solidifying Republican control of Virginia's government, it will be such a feather in his cap that he can slide into a prime position among the pack.\par \par \uc2 And if not, well, there's always 2028.\par \par \uc2 At 56, Mr. Youngkin has time to watch where his party is headed and maybe even nudge it in a direction more amenable to his brand. One of his big themes this cycle is that ''the eyes of the nation are on Virginia'' -- that other states, languishing under Democratic control, are looking to the commonwealth for ''hope.'' And ''hope beats fear'' every time, he repeatedly tells audiences, sounding more like \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  than a Trump-era Republican.\par \par \uc2 This kind of happy talk strikes many Democrats as rich coming from a guy who won office in part by stoking parents' fears over what their children were being exposed to in public schools. But in politics -- especially presidential politics -- the messenger matters at least as much as the message. Whether a conservative agenda can win broad acceptance when delivered with a friendly face these days is an open question: Voters certainly weren't looking for that in 2016. Neither could \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  close the deal with his nice-guy persona in 2012. For now, Mr. Youngkin may be the best test case of whether, at the national level, the Republican electorate still has any use for a champion who isn't an aggrieved bomb thrower.\par \par \uc2 Jo Watts has faith, or at least hope, that Mr. Youngkin can pass that test. ''I think he's going to save our party,'' she tells me the following Saturday as we wait for a rally to begin in Manassas. Ms. Watts used to be the executive secretary of the county's Republican committee. ''I worked my buns off for nine years for everybody that ran,'' she says. This included both Trump campaigns. But after the lunacy surrounding the 2020 presidential election, she recalls, ''my heart wasn't in it.'' And so she quit the job she had poured so much into for so long.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Youngkin is giving Ms. Watts fresh optimism: ''He's made us what my dream is -- to be back to America. But not in a crass way. In a Youngkin way!'' And by that she means? ''Common sense. Innovative. Positive.''\par \par \uc2 You hear these themes a lot from Youngkin fans, along with gushing about how he's just a really likable guy. Rallygoers Gary and Barbara Pentek trip over each other in praising him. ''I'm a father ...'' Gary begins, just as Barbara predicts, ''I think he'll be a future president,'' and Gary continues, ''He's the kind of guy that I would let take my daughter out!''\par \par \uc2 No question, Mr. Youngkin is good at retail campaigning. After his speeches, he all but dives into the crowd, shaking hands and posing for selfies. At 6 foot 5, he has to lean down to engage with many of the folks. He beams at each one as though he or she is the most fascinating creature in the room.\par \par \uc2 This is not to ignore Mr. Youngkin's substance. Republicans at multiple events extolled his parental-rights agenda in particular. ''It's a very common sense and universal American value,'' says Natalie Erdossy, whom I saw at two rallies. A member of the conservative group Moms for \uc2 Liberty\uc2 , Ms. Erdossy says that she is a former Democrat and that Mr. Youngkin is the first Republican she supported for ''higher office.''\par \par \uc2 Parental rights was a defining issue in Mr. Youngkin's 2021 win, and he continues to beat that drum hard. But 2023 is not 2021. And this cycle, the topic that looms large is one that has proved a drag on Republicans in many states: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It is, notably, one policy area Mr. Youngkin is not talking up on his tour. And by that I mean the word did not pass his lips during any of the speeches I saw.\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  has emerged as a mobilizing force for Democrats, and the party is now looking to hang the issue around Virginia Republicans' necks. Some conservative candidates are embracing the fight. But most seem eager to focus on anything else. Through mid-October, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was mentioned in over 40 percent of the ads from Democratic candidates for the state legislature, compared with 3 percent from Republican contenders.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Youngkin is not avoiding the debate entirely. This is, in fact, arguably the most delicate piece of his experiment, trying to turn the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue back to Republicans' advantage. He is pitching his 15-week plan as a ''reasonable'' compromise, and his PAC has poured $1.4 million into an ad campaign painting Democrats as dishonest extremists. On the trail, he comes at the topic obliquely, claiming the Democrats have nothing to offer but ''fear'' -- of what he doesn't bother to say. He doesn't need to; the crowds know what he means.\par \par \uc2 At one of the tour stops, I asked him to unpack this charge. ''They're saying we're going to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The reality is that they're the ones who want to change the law,'' he insists, noting that Democrats in the State Senate backed a constitutional amendment to codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. ''We support a bill to protect life at 15 weeks, with full exceptions for rape and incest and when the mother's life is at risk. We support common sense, and they support extremism.''\par \par \uc2 Except that a 15-week ban would, in fact, be a major change to Virginia law, which currently protects \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access up to around 26 weeks. Also, the governor last year said he would ''happily and gleefully'' sign any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions that passed the legislature. And, during his 2021 run, he was caught on tape saying, ''When I'm governor, and I have a majority in the House, we can start going on offense'' on the issue.\par \par \uc2 So you can see why \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters might be anxious.\par \par \uc2 ''Are we scared?'' asks Susan Swecker, the chairwoman of the Virginia Democratic Party. ''Hell yeah! We are scared about what he and the MAGA Republicans are trying to do. But we are not making stuff up. We are very factual.''\par \par \uc2 The governor ''presents as a nonthreatening, affable, vest-wearing, basketball-playing dad,'' says Ms. Swecker. ''In reality, we have seen him to be every bit as right wing and MAGA, just with a smile on his face.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Youngkin is indeed slippery when it suits him, such as his dance around Mr. Trump's election fraud lies in 2021. And paeans to unity and common sense sound less convincing coming from someone who lent his star power to some of the most extreme MAGA candidates of the 2022 midterms. (Kari Lake? Seriously?)\par \par \uc2 But the difference between success and failure often boils down to voters' gut feelings about the person. Mr. Youngkin's sunny, steady brand is ultimately what is being pressure-tested. The governor lacks the crackle of Mr. Trump's apocalyptic showmanship. His buoyancy can shade into goofiness. And even some Republicans who praise his ''nuance'' and ''reasonable'' demeanor -- with him, you won't have ''a bull in a china shop-type situation,'' says rallygoer Amy Roark -- also delight in Mr. Trump's fire. ''I support Trump 100 percent,'' clarifies Ms. Roark. ''His style is my favorite.'' Republicans love a fighter, and Mr. Youngkin has yet to prove himself in the political ThunderDome.\par \par \uc2 Finally! At my last tour stop with the governor, this one at a private farm in The Plains, I see someone sporting a Trump hat. Brian Hagarty believes the former president ''got a raw deal'' -- and continues to get one. He loves Mr. Trump's fighting spirit and fears that a Trump substitute like Mr. DeSantis is simply ''not aggressive enough'' to survive the swamp.\par \par \uc2 That said, he loves Mr. Youngkin, whom he thinks could be a winner on the national stage someday, even if he can't put his finger on exactly what he likes so much. ''What I find appealing about the governor is that whenever he speaks, he delivers on whatever he says he's going to do.''\par \par \uc2 Such as? I ask.\par \par \uc2 ''I can't pinpoint anything specifically, right? But it's his vibe as far as what I get his intention is.'' Mr. Hagarty insists he's not a ''big political guy at all. But I know clarity and I know non-clarity. And with the governor, in my opinion, it has always been clarity.''\par \par \uc2 Clarity is, of course, in the eye of the beholder. And one could argue that the Youngkin brand was built on his talent for blurring lines and soft-pedaling divisive policies with a soothing manner and smooth talk about ''progress'' and ''bringing people together.'' But after the perpetual instability and conflict of Trumpism, it could turn out to be a brand that resonates with a frazzled electorate.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 A domestic correspondent in Opinion and a host of the Matter of Opinion podcast.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY MATT EICH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (SR5) This article appeared in print on page SR4, SR5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231105ejb50004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
