\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Running on a President's Agenda Without Uttering His Name\b0\par\par\uc2 By Reid J. Epstein \par \uc2 1,820 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , the popular incumbent, is campaigning for re-election on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the economy and infrastructure -- but distancing himself from the unpopular president.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  of Kentucky is conducting one of this year's most intriguing political experiments: What happens when an incumbent Democrat campaigns on President Biden's record and agenda, but never mentions the party's unpopular leader by name?\par \par \uc2 Mr. Beshear is running for re-election in his deep-red state as a generic version of Mr. Biden, promoting himself as having led Kentucky through dark times to emerge with a strong post-Covid economy.\par \par \uc2 Like Mr. Biden, he is counting on voters' distaste for aggressive Republican opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is banned in almost all circumstances in Kentucky, as well as those with good will toward his stewardship during crises like natural and climate disasters.\par \par \uc2 Yet he is doing whatever he can to separate himself from Mr. Biden, whose approval ratings remain mired around 40 percent nationally and are much lower in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 ''This race is about Kentucky,'' Mr. Beshear said on Monday in Richmond, Ky. ''It's about what's going on in our houses, not about what's going on in the White House.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Beshear is among the most popular governors in the country, and Democrats are cautiously optimistic about his prospects in Tuesday's elections, even though former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  won the state by about 26 percentage points in 2020.\par \par \uc2 As in-person early voting begins on Thursday, officials in both parties in Kentucky say that every private poll of the race has shown Mr. Beshear leading his Republican challenger, \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2 , the attorney general. That could suggest the continuation of a national political environment that has been favorable to Democrats since the Supreme Court's decision in Dobbs v. Jackson in June 2022 ended the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Biden remains toxic in the state: A poll released Tuesday by \uc2 Morning Consult\uc2  found that 68 percent of Kentuckians disapproved of him, while 60 percent -- including 43 percent of Republicans -- approved of Mr. Beshear.\par \par \uc2 Since Mr. Beshear won the governor's race in 2019, the number of registered Democrats in Kentucky has fallen while the number of Republicans has increased. And local Republicans believe they'll outperform polling after surveys underestimated support for Mr. Trump in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky's voters have a knack for providing a preview of national trends. The state's last six elections for governor have forecast presidential election results a year later.\par \par \uc2 On the campaign trail in counties that Mr. Trump carried -- which is 118 of Kentucky's 120 -- Mr. Beshear tries to extricate the Biden from Bidenomics, the tagline much heralded by the president's campaign. Mr. Beshear celebrates record-low unemployment rates, a major bridge project paid for by Mr. Biden's infrastructure law and what he says are the ''two best years for economic development in our history.''\par \par \uc2 No new business development is too small. At a Monday morning stop in Richmond, Ky., Mr. Beshear cited the recent opening of a rest stop just outside town. ''We even brought a Buc-ee's to Madison County,'' he said, referring to the franchise's first outpost in the state and a point of local pride.\par \par \uc2 Left unmentioned in Mr. Beshear's pitch to voters is the Biden administration's significant role in his r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9. Mr. Biden's infrastructure law has directed $5.2 billion to at least 220 Kentucky projects, including $1.1 billion for high-speed internet and $1.6 billion for the rebuilding of the Brent Spence Bridge, which connects Cincinnati to its Kentucky suburbs. It's a long-awaited project that Mr. Beshear mentions in his closing TV ad.\par \par \uc2 Democrats on the Kentucky ballot with Mr. Beshear on Tuesday have all gotten the message about Mr. Biden.\par \par \uc2 Kim Reeder, the Democrat running for state auditor, laughed when asked if she had ever said the words ''Joe Biden'' out loud, then requested to go off the record when asked what she thought of his performance in office. Sierra Enlow, the party's candidate for agriculture commissioner -- whose Republican opponent is pledging in television ads to ''stop Biden and save Kentucky'' -- said she responded by ''talking about what voters need to hear and what this office actually does.''\par \par \uc2 And Pam Stevenson, the Democratic candidate for attorney general, said she didn't talk about Mr. Biden ''because for the last year, no one's asked me about him.''\par \par \uc2 Kentucky Republicans acknowledge that Mr. Beshear is popular and leading even in their polling. Mr. Cameron, who is a prot\u233\'00\'E9g\u233\'00\'E9 of Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , acknowledges in his TV ads that Mr. Beshear is ''a nice guy.''\par \par \uc2 The most popular topics in TV ads aired by Mr. Cameron and his Republican allies are crime, opposition to Mr. Biden, Mr. Cameron's endorsement from Mr. Trump, opposition to L.G.B.T.Q. rights, and jobs, according to AdImpact, a media tracking firm.\par \par \uc2 Mac Brown, the chairman of the Republican Party of Kentucky, said Mr. Beshear's popularity was a remnant of the billions directed to the state from the Biden administration. Crime is the foremost concern, said Mr. Brown, whose home in the Louisville suburbs was vandalized and burned last year.\par \par \uc2 ''When you sit down and look at it, he's very good at taking credit for what other people do,'' Mr. Brown said. ''That's probably the easiest way to say it.''\par \par \uc2 As with Mr. Biden and other Democrats, the most potent political weapon for Mr. Beshear is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. With Republican supermajorities in the Kentucky Legislature, there's little Mr. Beshear can do to change the state's near-total ban on the procedure. The building in downtown Louisville that housed one of Kentucky's last \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics is now for sale.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Beshear's campaigning is a reversal of decades of red-state Democratic reticence on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics. Where Democrats have in the past avoided the issue or watered down their support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Mr. Beshear has blasted Mr. Cameron for his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance and attacked Kentucky Republicans for passing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. He is airing striking ads that feature a woman who speaks of being raped by her stepfather when she was 12 years old.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cameron, who has defended the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in court, now says he would sign legislation to allow some exceptions if elected.\par \par \uc2 ''There's no ads saying, 'Don't elect the pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  guy,''' said Trey Grayson, a Republican who served as Kentucky secretary of state in the 2000s.\par \par \uc2 Last November, voters rejected an effort to write an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prohibition into the Kentucky Constitution. Now the Beshear campaign has found in its polling that just 12 percent of Kentuckians favor the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Mr. Beshear said he was trying to change the political language surrounding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  away from the old binary between choice and life.\par \par \uc2 ''Those terms were from a Roe v. Wade world that doesn't exist anymore,'' he said in Richmond this week. ''In the Dobbs world, we have the most draconian, restrictive law in the country. This race is about whether you think that victims of rape and incest should have options, that the couples that have a nonviable pregnancy should have to carry it to term even though that child is going to die.''\par \par \uc2 Steve Beshear, who is Mr. Beshear's father and a former governor of the state, was more succinct about where the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate stood in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 ''It's totally changed from a Republican issue to a Democratic issue,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Just as Mr. Biden's fate is likely to be determined by his performance in the counties that ring Atlanta, Milwaukee and Philadelphia, Mr. Beshear has concentrated on the suburban areas near Cincinnati, Lexington and Louisville. In 2019, he won Madison County, a Lexington suburb that includes Richmond, before Mr. Trump won it by about 27 points in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Jimmy Cornelison, a Democrat who is the elected coroner of Madison County, said people there appreciated that the state had far fewer deaths from the coronavirus pandemic because Mr. Beshear had put in place aggressive policies to restrict public gatherings and require masks in indoor spaces. But that doesn't mean such Kentuckians share Mr. Beshear's party identification.\par \par \uc2 ''There were a lot of people elected Democrats in this county that aren't Democrats now,'' Mr. Cornelison said. ''I'm the sole survivor.''\par \par \uc2 Voters who came to Mr. Beshear's campaign rallies this week spoke of his nightly coronavirus updates in 2020, his relentless travel schedule and a general satisfaction about how the state is doing. While Mr. Biden speaks of restoring ''the soul of America,'' Mr. Beshear has invited the entire state to join him on ''Team Kentucky.''\par \par \uc2 ''People disagree with Washington, you know, but they like what's going on in Kentucky,'' said Ralph Hoskins, a Democratic retired school superintendent from Oneida, Ky., who drove through the rain to see Mr. Beshear speak under a tent in the parking lot of an abandoned supermarket in London, Ky.\par \par \uc2 Nearby, Jean Marie Durham, a Democrat who is a retired state employee from East Bernstadt, Ky., showed off a poem she had written about Mr. Beshear during the early days of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 ''He cares about our protection from death and despair; He diligently considers our safety and personal care!'' she wrote.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Durham also had handy the response Mr. Beshear had sent her. He called her ''a very talented writer'' and wrote that he had displayed the poem in his office in Frankfort, the capital.\par \par \uc2 ''He's one of us,'' Ms. Durham said of Mr. Beshear, ''even though his dad was governor.''\par \par \uc2 Gov. Andy Beshear of Kentucky, holding a microphone, is among the most popular U.S. governors and Democrats are cautiously optimistic about his chances in Tuesday's elections. He is counting on voters' distaste for aggressive G.O.P. opposition to \uc2 , which is banned in almost all circumstances in his state.; The \uc2  debate has changed from a G.O.P. issue ''to a Democratic issue,'' said Steve Beshear, ex-governor of Kentucky and father of the incumbent.; Pam Stevenson, the Democratic candidate for attorney general, said she didn't talk about Mr. Biden ''because for the last year, no one's asked me about him.''; Kim Reeder, the Democrat running for state auditor, left, with a supporter, laughed when asked if she had ever said the words ''Joe Biden'' out loud. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JON CHERRY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); The most popular topics in TV ads aired by Mr. Beshear's Republican challenger, Daniel Cameron, are about crime and opposition to President Biden. (PHOTOGRAPH BY TIMOTHY D. EASLEY/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231105ejb50003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
