\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Choice On Ohio Ballot Baffles Voters\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike and Lisa Lerer \par \uc2 1,620 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ballot questions have been a winning strategy for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, even in red states. But complicated ballot language and misinformation have some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters worried.\par \par \uc2 Volunteers canvassing in favor of a ballot initiative to establish a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped Alex Woodward at a market hall in Ohio to ask if they could expect her vote in November.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Woodward said she favors \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and affirmed her support. But as the canvassers moved on through the hall, she realized she was not sure how to actually mark her ballot. ''I think it's a yes,'' she said. ''Maybe it's a no?''\par \par \uc2 Anyone in Ohio could be forgiven some confusion -- the result of an avalanche of messaging and counter-messaging, misinformation and complicated language around what the amendment would do, and even an entirely separate ballot measure with the same name just three months ago. All this has \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters worried in an off-year election race that has become the country's most watched.\par \par \uc2 Across the country, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have been on an unexpected winning streak with ballot measures that put the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  straight to voters. They have prevailed in six out of six since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade last year, even in red states like Kansas.\par \par \uc2 But the measure in Ohio is their toughest fight yet. It is the first time that voters in a red state are being asked to affirmatively vote ''yes'' to a constitutional amendment establishing a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than ''no'' to preserve the status quo established by courts. Ohio voters have historically tended to reject ballot amendments.\par \par \uc2 Republicans who control the levers of state power have used their positions to try to influence the vote, first by calling a special election in August to try to raise the threshold for passing ballot amendments, then when that failed, by using language favored by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups to describe the amendment on the ballot and in official state communications.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, which were caught flat-footed against the wave of voter anger that immediately followed the court overturning Roe, have had more time to sharpen their message. They have stoked fears about loss of parental rights and allowing children to get transition surgeries, even though the proposed amendment mentions neither.\par \par \uc2 Democrats nationally are watching to see if the outrage that brought new voters to the party last year maintains enough momentum to help them win even in red states in the presidential and congressional races in 2024. And with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups pushing similar measures on ballots in red and purple states next year, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups are hoping they have found a winning strategy to stop them.\par \par \uc2 ''Certainly, we know that all eyes are on Ohio right now,'' said Amy Natoce, the spokeswoman for Protect Women Ohio, a group founded by national anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups including Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America to oppose the amendment.\par \par \uc2 With early voting underway since mid-October, the state is a frenzy of television and social media ads, multiple rallies a day and doorknobs laden with campaign literature, with each side accusing the other of being too extreme for Ohio.\par \par \uc2 A ''yes'' on Issue 1, a citizen-sponsored ballot initiative pushed largely by doctors, would amend the state's constitution to establish a right to ''carry out one's own reproductive decisions,'' including on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The amendment explicitly allows the state to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after viability, or around 23 weeks, when the fetus can survive outside the uterus, unless the pregnant woman's doctor finds the procedure ''is necessary to protect the pregnant patient's life or health.''\par \par \uc2 But that language does not appear on the ballot. Instead, voters see a summary from the Secretary of State, \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2 , a Republican who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pushed the August ballot measure to try to thwart the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights amendment. That summary turns the provision on viability on its head, saying the amendment ''would always allow an unborn child to be aborted at any stage of pregnancy, regardless of viability.''\par \par \uc2 Other Republicans have helped spread misinformation about the amendment. The state attorney general, who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , issued a 13-page analysis that said, among other claims, that the amendment could invalidate a law requiring parental consent for minors seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (Constitutional scholars have said these claims are untrue. And the amendment would allow some restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 The ballot measure Republicans put forward in August trying to make this one harder to pass was also called Issue 1. Across the state, some lawns still have signs up from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups urging ''No on Issue 1.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have reminded voters of the consequences of Ohio's six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that was in effect for 82 days last year -- and could go into effect again any day, pending a ruling from the state's Supreme Court. They repeatedly mention the 10-year-old rape victim who traveled to Indiana for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after doctors in Ohio refused to provide one because of the ban.\par \par \uc2 In a television ad, a couple tells of their anguish when doctors told them at 18 weeks that a long-desired pregnancy would not survive, but that they could not get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ohio, forcing them, too, to leave the state for care: ''What happened to us could happen to anyone.''\par \par \uc2 The ''yes'' side has also appealed to Ohioans' innate conservatism about government overreach, going beyond traditional messages casting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as critical to women's rights. John Legend, the singer-songwriter and Ohio native whose wife, Chrissy Teigen, has spoken publicly about an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that saved her life, urged in a video message, ''Issue 1 will get politicians out of personal decisions about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 The ''no'' side makes little mention of the six-week ban, or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Yard signs and billboards instead argue that a ''no'' vote protects parents' rights. Protect Women Ohio has spread messages on social media and in campaign literature claiming that because the amendment gives ''individuals'' rather than ''adults'' the right to make their own reproductive decisions, it could lead to children getting gender transition surgery without parental permission -- which constitutional scholars have also said is untrue.\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  side is trying to reach beyond the conservative base, and it will have to in order to win. In polls in July and October, 58 percent of Ohio residents said they would vote in favor of the amendment to secure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and that included a majority of independents.\par \par \uc2 Kristi Hamrick, the vice president of media and policy for Students for Life, which opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has been ''dorm knocking'' on college campuses in Ohio, said the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  side had relied too much on ''vague talking points'' to try to win earlier ballot measures. ''It wasn't direct in what was at stake and how people would be hurt,'' she said. ''What is at stake is whether or not there can be limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whether we can have unfettered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  side has leaned into arguments that the amendment would encourage ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up until the moment of birth.'' An ad aired during the Ohio State-Notre Dame football game featured Donald Trump warning, ''In the ninth month, you can take the baby and rip the baby out of the womb of the mother.''\par \par \uc2 Data shows late-term abortions are rare and usually performed in cases where doctors say the fetus will not survive. In Ohio, there were roughly 100 abortions after 21 weeks of pregnancy in 2020.\par \par \uc2 National groups have poured in money, making this an unusually expensive off-year race. Ohioans United for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the coalition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups supporting the amendment, has spent $26 million since Labor Day, nearly three times as much as Protect Women Ohio, and most of that money has come from outside the state.\par \par \uc2 At the market hall, the group of pediatricians leading the canvass for the ''yes'' side landed mostly on people who had heard about the amendment and supported it.\par \par \uc2 One voter, Ashley Gowens, introduced herself to one of the doctors as ''Stephanie's mom,'' thanking him for ''standing up for my daughter's rights.'' Ms. Gowens worried that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters would be misled by the language on the ballot, or not realize they had to vote again -- and differently -- after the August election called by Republicans. ''I know that it was done purposefully,'' she said. ''The only way they could knock this down was to confuse people.''\par \par \uc2 David Pepper, a former state Democratic Party chair, said he too feared the August election had sapped some energy, and that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  messages against extremism will appeal to Ohioans' reluctance to change their Constitution.\par \par \uc2 ''You kind of have to run the table on your arguments, and they all have to be pretty persuasive for people to vote yes,'' he said. ''All you have to do to convince someone to vote ''no'' is give them one reason.''\par \par \uc2 Marsha Chenin, left, and Dr. Lauren Beene, executive director of Ohio Physicians for \uc2 , talking about Issue 1.; Literature from the group Students for Life, which opposes \uc2  and has been ''dorm knocking'' on college campuses in Ohio.; Simone Davis, right, and her mother, Ruth Hartman, canvassed door to door for Planned Parenthood after the start of early voting.; Members of the anti-\uc2  group Students for Life handed out information and talked with students at Otterbein University. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY MADDIE McGARVEY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A14) This article appeared in print on page A1, A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231104ejb40002x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
