\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 As  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Access Shrinks, Hospitals Fill Gaps\b0\par\par\uc2 By Allison McCann \par \uc2 1,726 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A. wanted a cheeseburger and to go home. She had made the three-hour trip from Indianapolis to Chicago a day earlier and had been at the hospital since 6:30 a.m., with an empty stomach, waiting to be taken into an operating room to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It was her second trip to Chicago in two weeks, and the third time she had tried to end her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 She ordered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills online in July, but they were ineffective. A few weeks later, after Indiana enacted a total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she made an appointment at a clinic in Chicago. But an ultrasound revealed that her placenta was growing abnormally, increasing the risk of bleeding. She was told she would need to have the procedure done at a hospital instead.\par \par \uc2 ''This is not what I was expecting,'' said A., 36, who asked to be identified by only her first initial. ''I had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before and it was a one-two kind of thing. I was out of the clinic in an hour.''\par \par \uc2 Abortions are extremely safe, but for women with certain pregnancy complications, it can be risky to take \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills or to have the procedure done in an outpatient clinic. The same is true for women with severe underlying or untreated health conditions, including asthma, diabetes, hypertension and hyperthyroidism.\par \par \uc2 For patients with these conditions seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , having it done in a hospital is the safest option because of the ability to do emergency procedures, like blood transfusions. But a hospital procedure is often more onerous, expensive and time-consuming, especially since the Supreme Court's decision last year to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 ''I know this is not open-heart surgery, but you're being treated like it is,'' A. said. ''Mentally, it freaks you out.''\par \par \uc2 Nationally, about 3 percent of abortions occurred in hospitals before the Dobbs decision, usually in instances of fetal anomalies or high-risk pregnancies. In states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, hospitals now perform the procedure in only the most limited cases. But in places like Illinois, hospitals are playing a larger role in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access as more patients travel to seek care.\par \par \uc2 ''Hospitals have always had a role in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, but hospitals have not always filled that role,'' said Dr. Jenni Villavicencio, an OB-GYN and the interim director of public affairs and advocacy at the Society for Family Planning, a group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''The spotlight on them is brighter now post-Dobbs because the number of places where people can access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shrunk by almost half,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Laura Laursen belongs to a group of Illinois \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who started meeting informally last year, anticipating the state would become a hub for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for much of the Midwest and South.\par \par \uc2 In the months that followed, providers noticed that they were not only seeing more patients overall -- abortions rose an estimated 69 percent in Illinois during the first half of 2023 -- but also more patients with complex health issues that prevented them from having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a clinic.\par \par \uc2 ''Sick people get abortions, and healthy people get abortions. Just like sick people get colonoscopies, and healthy people get colonoscopies,'' Dr. Laursen, an OB-GYN who provides abortions at Rush University Medical Center, said.\par \par \uc2 ''It's like we've siloed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  so much that we think that it doesn't have any other issues, but it's just like any other procedure.''\par \par \uc2 Many of her patients are traveling from states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, where chronic health conditions and maternal mortality are worse to begin with, she said. Seven of the 10 states that have declined to expand Medicaid to cover most poor adults also ban or restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Women in these states are less likely to have access to routine medical care to treat their chronic health issues, and providers said they are also less likely to seek obstetric care back home, for fear that a physician might disclose their desire for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Several pregnancy risk factors like eclampsia and previous C-section delivery are also higher in these states, particularly among Black women.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not that they just don't have appropriate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. They also don't have the care they need for their regular medical problems,'' said Dr. Allison Cowett, the medical director at Family Planning Associates, the clinic in Chicago where A. made an appointment.\par \par \uc2 Doctors thought A.'s ultrasound showed placenta accreta, one of the more common reasons an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be done in a hospital rather than a clinic. It occurs when the placenta grows too deeply into the uterine wall, and it was most likely caused by scarring from the C-sections she had to deliver her two children, who are now 13 and 18.\par \par \uc2 A. didn't know she had it until she arrived at her clinic appointment in Chicago and was told she couldn't have the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  there. ''I thought my world was going to end,'' she recalled. ''I didn't want to start over again.''\par \par \uc2 It is unlikely she would have qualified for an exception under Indiana's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and the next available hospital appointment in Chicago was a week away. She went to the zoo and waited around, hoping another patient would cancel, but nothing opened up.\par \par \uc2 ''It just sucks that I have to go through all these hoops and run around because I can't go in my own state,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Indiana, like most other states that have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has pledged to better support health care for mothers -- primarily by extending Medicaid coverage for up to one year postpartum. But doctors in Chicago said the emphasis on postpartum care offered a narrow window to address the underlying health issues many women in these states face.\par \par \uc2 ''I know that a certain number of my patients -- if they are unable to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- are not going to survive their pregnancy,'' said Dr. Jonah Fleisher, an OB-GYN who provides abortions at UI Health, a Chicago hospital.\par \par \uc2 At the end of last year, Illinois \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers met with state officials to suggest a program to better serve the sickest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients. In July, the state awarded them a $600,000 grant to start the Complex \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Regional Line for Access, or CARLA.\par \par \uc2 Two full-time nurses now coordinate referrals between Illinois \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and four Chicago-area hospitals. The nurses conduct a full medical intake over the phone; track down medical records; and figure out coverage when patients have insurance or connect them with the Chicago \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fund when they don't.\par \par \uc2 ''We are here to help with the medical and logistical side of things, but we also navigate a lot of the emotional side,'' said Caroline Nyheim, one of the two staff nurses. ''Oftentimes patients have already been through a lot, and they are really frustrated and overwhelmed to be starting at square one again.''\par \par \uc2 A similar statewide hospital referral system was started in Massachusetts in 2003 and remains the only other program in the country of this kind.\par \par \uc2 Since the program started in August, CARLA has helped more than 100 patients, including A., secure an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a Chicago hospital. About a third of patients traveled from out of state.\par \par \uc2 Medicaid covers the procedure cost for Illinois residents, but others are left to pay in full or seek help from an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund. In Chicago, hospital procedures typically cost $3,000 to $6,000, and as much as $24,000 at one hospital.\par \par \uc2 The Chicago \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fund has covered nearly all of the costs for CARLA patients so far, including the procedure, food, travel and lodging. But as more women with health issues seek \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in Illinois, spending will go up. Already, about 30 percent of the fund's budget is going to help the 4 percent of patients whose abortions must be done in a hospital.\par \par \uc2 The city pledged $1 million to the fund this year, and in July, Gov. \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  of Illinois announced a series of initiatives around \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care -- including CARLA -- totaling more than $23 million.\par \par \uc2 ''When you're in the midst of an emergency -- and that's what we're in right now with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care -- you're simultaneously trying to deliver services and figure out how to hone it and make it better,'' Mr. Pritzker said.\par \par \uc2 By the time of A.'s hospital appointment in mid-September, she was nearly 12 weeks pregnant. She was nervous, asking the anesthesiologist about whether she would feel anything. She didn't want to.\par \par \uc2 She was out of the operating room in less than an hour and asked the doctor what to do if she had any complications. ''God forbid I need to go to the emergency room in Indiana,'' she said. ''What do I say?''\par \par \uc2 The doctor advised her to say that she underwent a procedure for a miscarriage, before reassuring her that she would not end up in an emergency room.\par \par \uc2 A. twisted her belly-button ring back into place, grabbed her belongings and climbed into a wheelchair, where a nurse was waiting to take her outside. A friend would drive her back home.\par \par \uc2 ''I think what scared me the most is that they were, like, you need it in the hospital because something could go wrong,'' she said. ''Imagine if I went to full term, and then something happened to me. Then it's three babies left alone without a mom?''\par \par \uc2 A., 36, who asked to be identified by only her first initial, said of having an \uc2  in a hospital.; ''Sick people get abortions, and healthy people get abortions,'' said Dr. Laura Laursen, an OB-GYN at Rush University Medical Center.; Above, from left: Dr. Erika Hinz at the University of Illinois Hospital in Chicago with an ultrasound machine; Rush University Medical Center, also in Chicago; and A. gathering her belongings from her hospital bed, below, last month. A.'s trip to the city was her third attempt to have an \uc2 . Her home state of Indiana has banned the procedure.; Qudsiyyah Shariyf, the deputy director of the Chicago \uc2  Fund, left, and Megan Jeyifo, the executive director. The fund covers care. (A16-A17) This article appeared in print on page A16, A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231026ejaq0004y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
