\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Small Rise in Legal Abortions Despite Spread of State Bans\b0\par\par\uc2 By Claire Cain Miller and Margot Sanger-Katz \par \uc2 1,628 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The first full-year census of U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers shows significant increases in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in states where it's legal.\par \par \uc2 In the year after the Supreme Court ended the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , something unexpected happened: The total number of legal abortions in the United States did not fall. Instead, it appeared to increase slightly, by about 0.2 percent, according to the first full-year count of abortions provided nationwide.\par \par \uc2 This finding came despite the fact that 14 states banned all abortions, and seven imposed new limits on them. Even as those restrictions reduced the legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate to near zero in some states, there were large increases in places where abortions remained legal. Researchers said they were driven by the expansion of telemedicine for mail-order \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, increased options and assistance for women who traveled, and a surge of publicity about ways to get abortions.\par \par \uc2 The response by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and activists to the end of Roe v. Wade, it seems, has resulted in more access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in states where it's still legal -- not just for women traveling from states with bans but also for women living there.\par \par \uc2 Still, new bans and restrictions have had far-reaching effects. Many women, especially in the South, have turned to methods outside the U.S. medical system or carried their pregnancies to term, researchers said. These women are likely to be poor, teenagers or immigrants, and to have young children or jobs that don't give them time off.\par \par \uc2 ''I always think that should be the focal point to the story: The loss of access is profound and enormous,'' said Dr. Alison Norris, a professor at Ohio State and a chair of WeCount, which gathered the data. ''But it's also a story of what happens when health systems increase access. Underlying unmet need for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be being met now because of changes post-Dobbs.''\par \par \uc2 John Seago, the president of Texas Right to Life, said he was not surprised to see so many women continuing to seek abortions. He said that to decrease the number, policymakers needed to increase enforcement of bans and provide more support for pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 ''For us, it's such a straightforward ethical issue to cause the death of another human being that it's not a question of whether we need to prohibit it or not,'' he said. ''Now we are into what is effective.''\par \par \uc2 In the 12 months after the Dobbs decision in June 2022, there were on average 82,298 abortions a month, compared with 82,115 in the two months before Dobbs, WeCount found. The group, part of the Society of Family Planning, which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, collects monthly numbers from providers across the country. The new data, released Tuesday, included 83 percent of known providers, and researchers estimated the remainder based on historical trends and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  data from states.\par \par \uc2 Another estimate of abortions by the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , using a smaller sample of providers and for a shorter period, also found that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was most likely increasing nationwide.\par \par \uc2 The report does not include abortions outside the U.S. medical system -- such as ordering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills from abroad or traveling across the border. On average, there were around 5,000 more requests a month for pills from the largest overseas provider, Aid Access, in the 11 months after Dobbs than in the period before that decision was leaked, according to new data from Abigail Aiken, who leads the Self-Managed \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Needs Assessment Project at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The biggest increases in legal abortions occurred in states that border those with bans, suggesting that many patients traveled across state lines. In New Mexico, the average number of monthly abortions in the year after Dobbs increased 61 percent from the two months before, to 1,910. In Illinois, the rise was 33 percent, to 7,302. In Florida, which bars abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy but is surrounded by states with stricter bans, abortions were up 28 percent, to 7,705.\par \par \uc2 Patients regularly travel hundreds of miles, and confusion about laws and continuing changes to them have made travel options progressively more difficult, said Alexia Rice-Henry, co-executive director of ARC-Southeast, which helps people in six states arrange and pay for travel to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 In New Mexico, several new clinics opened after Dobbs. One is operated by Whole Woman's Health, which was forced to close three locations in Texas in July 2022. \uc2 Amy Hagstrom Miller\uc2 , the organization's founder and chief executive, said 98 percent of the new clinic's patients are Texans. Yet the New Mexico clinics serve many fewer patients than the Texas clinics did, she said. ''It's devastating how few people are making it,'' she said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  also rose in states that don't border states with total bans. In California, the average number of monthly abortions was up 6 percent to 13,704. In New York, it was up 2 percent to 9,198.\par \par \uc2 ''There was more unmet need than we realized in the legal states,'' said Ushma Upadhyay, a professor at the University of California, San Francisco School of Medicine and a chair of WeCount.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood Northern California, which operates 17 clinics, began hiring and expanding appointments and telehealth months before Dobbs. It was in part to prepare for an overturn of Roe, and in part a realization that demand for women's health care had built up during the pandemic, said Dr. Sara Kennedy, its chief operating and medical officer. It also started a program to cover the cost of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for patients who needed it.\par \par \uc2 She called the resulting increase ''very dramatic'': Medication abortions increased 25 percent in the year after Dobbs, and procedural abortions 18 percent.\par \par \uc2 In New York, patients have flown from Texas, Florida and Georgia, said Chelsea Williams-Diggs, interim executive director of the New York \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Fund. Even if New York City clinics, for example, aren't close, there are many flights, and patients may know family or friends in the region.\par \par \uc2 ''When we think about the people needing to travel and needing financial support, we're thinking about Black and brown folks, immigrants, and a lot of those people have connections to New York,'' she said. ''We hear that all the time: 'My cousin or auntie lives here.'''\par \par \uc2 She was surprised to also see an increase in New Yorkers asking for assistance. In rural parts of the state, she said, clinics can be far and often don't perform abortions past the first trimester, but even in New York City, calls have increased. She attributes it to publicity -- policymakers talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , celebrities sharing their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stories and billboards advertising ways to find one.\par \par \uc2 ''We're seeing resident New Yorkers who are saying, 'I thought we didn't have any options,''' she said. ''People didn't know \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds existed.''\par \par \uc2 Since Dobbs, 20 states and Washington, D.C., have passed laws improving access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and protecting providers and patients. California eliminated out-of-pocket costs for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, and Oregon invested $15 million to open new clinics and pay for people to travel from out of state. Some states allowed nurse practitioners and physician assistants to do abortions.\par \par \uc2 Another contributor to the apparent increase is something that preceded Dobbs: the legalization of telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Food and Drug Administration began allowing providers to mail pills without an in-person visit when health care offices closed early in the pandemic, and made the policy permanent in December 2021.\par \par \uc2 In response, start-ups opened to provide abortions this way to women in states where it is legal. Some prescribe pills based only on an intake form. They charge as little as $150, compared with $500 to $800 for a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a clinic.\par \par \uc2 Existing clinics, including many \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  affiliates, also began offering telemedicine abortions. Some primary care providers now prescribe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, and since January, retail pharmacies have been able to dispense them.\par \par \uc2 More recently, women in states with bans have also been able to order the pills because of shield laws that protect providers that prescribe and mail pills to such patients. After the period covered in the report, at least two, Aid Access and the Massachusetts Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Project, began prescribing and mailing pills to patients in all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 Even as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has expanded in some ways, so have limitations. Several states have banned or restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months, and others, like Florida, are poised to impose further limitations. At the federal level, a case making its way through courts could ban the mailing of one of the two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. And \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds say that after a surge of post-Dobbs donations, fund-raising has tapered off.\par \par \uc2 ''Folks came together and did the best they could to make sure they could see as many patients as possible,'' said Danika Severino Wynn, vice president for access and care at \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2 . She described the current system as held together with Band-Aids: ''I don't feel confident that we're in a space where we can say, 'Oh, we subverted a crisis.'''\par \par \uc2 Allison McCann contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Allison McCann contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 The biggest increases in legal abortions occurred in states that border those with bans. In New Mexico, the average number of monthly abortions after Dobbs increased 61 percent. Those numbers also increased in states that don't border states with total bans, such as California (6 percent). (PHOTOGRAPH BY JASON HENRY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A19. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231026ejap0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
