\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Georgia Supreme Court Opts to Keep State's 6-Week  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban in Effect\b0\par\par\uc2 By David W. Chen \par \uc2 936 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A lower court is now expected to hear arguments on the broader question of whether the right to privacy in the state constitution protects a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Georgia's Supreme Court upheld the state's prohibition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday, rejecting arguments made by doctors and advocacy groups that the law was unconstitutional when the state legislature approved it in 2019, more than three years before the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The ruling means that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains banned in the state, with limited exceptions, after the sixth week of pregnancy -- a point when most women have not yet even realized they are pregnant. With North Carolina now banning most abortions after 12 weeks of pregnancy, South Carolina after six weeks, and Florida doing the same, pending a legal challenge, the ruling ensures that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be largely inaccessible in the Deep South, forcing women there to travel farther for care, to more distant states like Virginia or Illinois.\par \par \uc2 The Georgia case is not over, because the court addressed only the question of whether the state ban should have been voided because of when it was enacted. The court sent the case back to a lower court for a trial on the separate question of whether the state constitution protects a right to privacy, and whether that right encompasses \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, the latest ruling in Georgia is a reminder that state constitutions have become key arbiters in the nation's state-by-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  battles.\par \par \uc2 In its 6-1 decision, with one justice disqualified and another not participating, the Georgia Supreme Court leaned heavily on the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's Dobbs decision, which overturned a national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in reviewing Georgia's law, the Living Infants and Equality Act, known as the LIFE Act.\par \par \uc2 ''Because Dobbs is controlling precedent on whether the United States Constitution confers a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and because the parties and the trial court do not dispute that the LIFE Act complies with Dobbs, it follows that the LIFE Act did not violate the United States Constitution when enacted in 2019,'' the state court concluded.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday morning, after the court issued its ruling, \uc2 Chris Carr\uc2 , the attorney general of Georgia, said in a statement, ''We are pleased with the court's decision and will continue to defend the constitutionality of Georgia's LIFE Act.''\par \par \uc2 When the Dobbs decision revoked the federal constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022, it kicked the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the states to regulate. Since then, many of them have enacted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, and many lawsuits have been filed to challenge the bans, with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights lawyers arguing that the prohibitions are unconstitutional under state guarantees of privacy, health, liberty or family planning.\par \par \uc2 One of the most closely watched cases involves Florida, where the state Supreme Court heard oral arguments in September challenging that state's 15-week ban.\par \par \uc2 Other states' supreme courts that have ruled already this year have split on the issue: North Dakota and Oklahoma found that their state constitutions protected the right in some situations. But Idaho ruled that its Constitution did not protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and courts in South Carolina and Indiana upheld \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans passed since Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 The Georgia case, which was brought by the SisterSong Women of Color Reproductive Justice Collective, focused on a doctrine of state law known as void ab initio, meaning void from the beginning. That doctrine says, in essence, that a Georgia law that violates the state or federal constitution at the time it is enacted is forever unconstitutional, said Julia Kaye, a senior staff attorney with the A.C.L.U. Reproductive Freedom Product, the lead attorney for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the case.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs had prevailed in November at a lower level, when Judge Robert C.I. McBurney of Fulton County Superior Court ruled that the six-week ban was enacted when ''everywhere in America, including Georgia, it was unequivocally unconstitutional for governments -- federal, state, or local -- to ban abortions before viability.''\par \par \uc2 But one week later, the state Supreme Court temporarily reinstated the six-week ban, setting the stage for Tuesday's ruling.\par \par \uc2 During oral arguments in March, the justices had seemed receptive to the arguments made by Stephen Petrany, the state's solicitor general, that the Dobbs decision applied retroactively and that Roe v. Wade was incorrect all along.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, the Georgia justices had seemed skeptical of the arguments made by Julia Blackburn Stone, a lawyer for the plaintiffs.\par \par \uc2 ''Dobbs expressly says the prior precedent was wrong,'' said Justice Charles J. Bethel.\par \par \uc2 In a statement underscoring the political impact of the issue, Julia Chavez Rodriguez, the campaign manager for the Biden-Harris re-election campaign, noted calls by some Republicans for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and said, ''The stakes couldn't be higher: This election could determine whether every woman in America faces the same terrifying reality Georgians will now face.''\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, congratulated and thanked Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  of Georgia and other allies who pushed for the six-week ban. She said in a statement that 25 states now ''have pro-life protections in their law in the new Dobbs era.''\par \par \uc2 ''When you lead and stand boldly for life, Americans will stand with you,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 In its 6-1 ruling, the Georgia Supreme Court leaned heavily on the Dobbs decision. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ERIK S. LESSER/EPA, VIA SHUTTERSTOCK) This article appeared in print on page A21. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231025ejap0003k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
