\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 With Tactical Retreat on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Lake Tests New Message for G.O.P.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael C. Bender \par \uc2 1,603 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kari Lake, along with other Republicans in battleground states, has come out against a national ban as candidates try to attract general election voters. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists aren't pleased.\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake campaigned for governor of Arizona last year as a fierce ally of former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  who was in lock step with her party's right-wing base, calling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the ''ultimate sin'' and supporting the state's Civil War-era restrictions on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 This week, she made a remarkable shift on the issue as she opened her bid for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 : She declared her opposition to a federal ban.\par \par \uc2 ''Republicans allowed Democrats to define them on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Ms. Lake said in a statement to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  about her break from the policy prescription favored by many anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and most of her party's presidential contenders. She added that she supported additional resources for pregnant women, and that ''just like President Trump, I believe this issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be left to the states.''\par \par \uc2 The maneuvering by Ms. Lake, along with similar adjustments by Republican Senate candidates in Pennsylvania and Michigan, is part of a broader strategic effort in her party to recalibrate on an issue that has become a political albatross in battleground states and beyond.\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade last year, eliminating federal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and handing Republicans one of their most significant policy victories in a generation, voters have turned out repeatedly to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, even in red states.\par \par \uc2 The campaign arm for Senate Republicans, the National Republican Senatorial Committee, is now coaching candidates to take the same tack as Ms. Lake -- that is, clearly state their opposition to a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, according to people familiar with the new strategy.\par \par \uc2 The group has also urged candidates to state their support for ''reasonable limits'' on late-term abortions with exceptions for rape, incest and the life of the mother, the people said. Rather than trying to avoid the topic, like many candidates did last year, it is advising Republicans to go on offense.\par \par \uc2 Senate Republicans were briefed last month on detailed research commissioned by One Nation, a nonprofit group aligned with Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  of Kentucky, the minority leader, showing that many Americans equated the term ''pro-life'' -- traditionally used by Republicans -- with support for a total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without any exceptions.\par \par \uc2 The research also showed that while voters opposed the idea of a total ban, there was wider support for restrictions after 12 to 15 weeks of pregnancy, particularly with exceptions for rape, incest and the life or health of the mother.\par \par \uc2 The nonprofit has suggested that Republicans communicate their views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with empathy and compassion. Steven Law, who is the president of One Nation, is also the president of the Senate Leadership Fund, which has spent more than $1 billion on federal campaigns since 2016.\par \par \uc2 Whether or not Republican candidates for Congress -- and the White House -- can persuade voters that they have become more moderate on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  promises to be one of the central questions of the 2024 elections.\par \par \uc2 ''Voters have repeatedly rejected Republican politicians for supporting dangerous policies that deny a woman's right to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Sarah Guggenheimer, the spokesperson for the Senate Majority political action committee dedicated to electing Democratic candidates. ''This cynical effort by \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and Republican candidates to mask their positions won't change that.''\par \par \uc2 The already challenging rebranding effort also carries significant risks, none more so than alienating anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists in the party.\par \par \uc2 Since the fall of Roe v. Wade and the nationwide rollback of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the party's base of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  voters, which include mostly evangelical Christians, has had heightened expectations that Republican politicians will push to implement the strict anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies they have spent decades promising.\par \par \uc2 Kristan Hawkins, the president of Students For Life of America, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization with more than 1,000 groups on campuses across the country, said equivocating on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be viewed as a betrayal by these voters.\par \par \uc2 To counter the shifting views among some Republican candidates, Ms. Hawkins's group has distributed a nine-page memo to members of the House of Representatives and the Senate. The memo, which was previously unreported, urged the members to continue their support for strict measures but also encouraged them to be personal, caring and specific in their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hawkins said that only ''squishy Republicans'' would back away from a federal ban, as Ms. Lake has, by insisting that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was now an issue that should be decided by states.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court ruling that overturned Roe, known as Dobbs v. Jackson, provided an opportunity to debate the issue on all levels of government, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''They obviously didn't read the Dobbs decision very well,'' Ms. Hawkins said in an interview. ''It doesn't say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only a state issue -- it says this issue can be acted upon at the federal, state and local levels.''\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Trump has made an apparent political calculus, insisting that hard-line positions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cost the party a red wave of victories last year, and that it must avoid similar mistakes in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Blaming \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows Mr. Trump to sidestep the sense among many Republicans that it was in large part his elevation of candidates who embraced his lies about the 2020 presidential election -- which ultimately proved unpopular to general election voters in key states -- that cost the party control of the Senate and delivered just a razor-thin House majority. He also ignores his own role in appointing three of the five Supreme Court justices who overturned Roe. But there is ample evidence that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue mattered.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has refused to take an explicit position on whether he would support a federal ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks, the baseline position of many Republicans as well as \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America, a leading anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group. Last month, he criticized Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, a presidential rival, for signing a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban into law.\par \par \uc2 Republican candidates in competitive states appear to be increasingly siding with the former president, even as the shifts represent a clear break from his base of evangelical voters who care deeply about the issue.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, former Representative Mike Rogers's platform for his Senate campaign includes opposition to a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, even though he voted as a House member in 2012 and 2013 to enact federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. In 2010, he said he supported exceptions ''only to prevent the death of the mother.''\par \par \uc2 But Michigan voters adopted a measure last year to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the State Constitution. At a campaign stop last month, Mr. Rogers promised not to support national proposals to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that were ''inconsistent with Michigan's law.''\par \par \uc2 ''Will I go to Washington, D.C., and try to undo what the citizens of Michigan voted for?'' Mr. Rogers said last month in DeWitt, Mich., according to The Detroit News. ''I will not.''\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, David McCormick began his second Senate bid last month and announced on the same day that he did not want a national ban.\par \par \uc2 In his campaign for Senate last year, Mr. McCormick gave multiple responses to questions about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  exceptions. At a Republican primary debate in April 2022, he said that ''in very rare instances, there should be exceptions for the life of the mother.'' At other events, he suggested that rape and incest should be included as exceptions.\par \par \uc2 This year, he has backed all three exceptions. In a Fox News interview last month, he said that he was opposed to a national ban.\par \par \uc2 ''This is also an issue where I think we have to show a lot of compassion and look for common ground,'' Mr. McCormick told Fox News. ''We should have contraception and we have reasonable limits on late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that is a compassionate position and a consensus position -- and that's the position I support.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. McCormick has collected endorsements from Republicans across the state, and no other serious challengers for the party's nomination have emerged.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lake spent several minutes talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during her first speech as a Senate candidate in Arizona last week, which she acknowledged was rare for a Republican to bring up. She described her position broadly, saying she wanted to ''save babies and help women.''\par \par \uc2 ''The Republican Party is going to put their money where their mouth is,'' Ms. Lake said to the cheering crowd. ''We are going to give them real choices so they can make better choices and not live with that regret.''\par \par \uc2 Still, Ms. Lake didn't mention her opposition to a national ban to the crowd, even though it is laid out on her campaign website.\par \par \uc2 ''Kari Lake has repeatedly said she is a pro-life candidate,'' said \uc2 Cathi Herrod\uc2 , the president of the Center for Arizona Policy, a nonprofit group that promotes anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. ''I think the advice to oppose a federal ban is misguided.''\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake called \uc2  the ''ultimate sin'' in a campaign she lost last year. Now she says she opposes a federal ban. (PHOTOGRAPH BY REBECCA NOBLE/GETTY IMAGES); Running for the Senate in Pennsylvania, David McCormick says he opposes a national ban and backs some \uc2  exceptions. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JEFF SWENSEN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A21. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231016ejag0003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
