\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 As Policies Shift, American Women Are Turning to Mexico for Abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Zolan Kanno-Youngs and Edyra Espriella \par \uc2 1,509 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 American women are seeking help from Mexico for abortions, crystallizing the shifting policies of two nations that once held vastly different positions on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The text message Cynthia Menchaca received this summer was one she was seeing more and more: A woman living in Texas said she had left a violent relationship only to discover she was pregnant, and she desperately wanted an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The woman had learned that Ms. Menchaca could send her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills from Mexico, where the procedure has been decriminalized in several states.\par \par \uc2 But the growing U.S. demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care is not limited to deliveries of medication, according to advocates like Ms. Menchaca, who lives in Coahuila state in northeastern Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Clinics in Tijuana and Mexico City, as well as activists in the northwestern city of Hermosillo, say they have seen women crossing the border from Texas, Louisiana and Arizona seeking access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Before, the women from Sonora would go to the United States to access abortions in clinics,'' said Andrea Sanchez, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activist, referring to the Mexican state that borders Arizona. ''And now the women from the United States come to Mexico.''\par \par \uc2 More than a year after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade, Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists have seen a rise of American women crossing the border to seek abortions -- crystallizing the shifting policies of two nations that once held vastly different positions on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 For decades, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was criminalized in Mexico and much of Latin America with few exceptions, while in the United States, the 1973 Roe v. Wade ruling established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Today, Mexico's Supreme Court has decriminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide, making it legally accessible in federal institutions and eliminating federal penalties for the procedure. Twelve of the country's 32 states have also decriminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and activists say they have renewed momentum to push local officials in the remaining states.\par \par \uc2 By comparison, more than 20 American states currently ban or restrict the procedure after 18 weeks of pregnancy or earlier, with 14 states completely forbidding the procedure in almost all circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Mexican activists, anticipating the Supreme Court could overturn Roe when it was still weighing the case, began organizing and have established an underground system, sending thousands of pills north and helping women travel south across the border. They say the longstanding restrictions in Latin America prepared them to now handle the influx of demand.\par \par \uc2 ''The truth is that years ago, we neither had nor envisioned collaboration with the United States,'' said Ver\u243\'00\'F3nica Cruz, who 20 years ago helped found the \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive-rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization Las Libres, which means ''the free ones.''\par \par \uc2 She added: ''But faced with the urgency, the increasing restrictions, and having a model, resources like the pills, and as our territory progresses, it became evident that we needed to build international solidarity.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cruz initially planned to help shuttle women in the United States to Mexico, but found it to be too financially burdensome both for her organization and those seeking abortions. She has instead focused on sending mifepristone and misoprostol, the two-drug regimen to end a pregnancy, over the border to American women, particularly those living in states that ban the procedure or ban providers from prescribing the pills.\par \par \uc2 In U.S. studies, the combination of these pills causes a complete \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than 99 percent of patients, and is as safe as the traditional \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure administered by a doctor in a clinic. Growing evidence from overseas suggests that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills are safe even among women who do not have a doctor to advise them.\par \par \uc2 Since the lifting of Roe, Ms. Cruz said she has helped roughly 20,000 women in 23 states secure the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. She said she will continue to help these women even as certain states move to penalize those who assist with abortions.\par \par \uc2 Activists involved in sending the pills to the United States declined to specify their shipping and delivery methods, though most said they are coordinating with American activists over the border. One organizer in Mexico, who requested anonymity out of fear of retaliation, said she conceals the medication in electronic accessories, clothing, stuffed animals or dietary supplements when shipping to states that restrict it.\par \par \uc2 While the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs can be delivered by mail, several states banned this shipping method, or require that the drugs be dispensed by providers in person.\par \par \uc2 Carol Tobias, the president of the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 , one of the largest anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups in the United States, said she was not surprised that women were traveling to Mexico for abortions. Americans have long crossed the border for various procedures, she said.\par \par \uc2 But she called for tougher enforcement in the United States to prevent people from easily delivering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in the mail. ''I think it's very sad that women are being told the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill is an easy, safe way out of a difficult situation,'' Ms. Tobias said. ''It's much more complicated than that.''\par \par \uc2 There is no reliable national data on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Mexico, according to public health experts. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists say they are mainly sending medication north to help Americans rather than providing access in Mexico itself.\par \par \uc2 Luisa Garc\u237\'00\'EDa, director of Profem's clinics in Tijuana and Mexico City, said she would typically see only one patient a month crossing the border to Mexico, where clinics offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care at a lower price than in the United States. But this year, she has received at least 80 calls from American numbers requesting appointments.\par \par \uc2 ''I can't believe it,'' Ms. Garc\u237\'00\'EDa said. ''America was free and open-minded about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but now with these decisions about the court, women need to relocate their reproductive and sexual rights.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Garc\u237\'00\'EDa said that Americans occasionally arrive at her clinic alone, nervous and speaking minimal Spanish. Some women have told her staff that they traveled to Mexico secretly without telling family members who disapprove of the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Huberfeld, a professor of health law at \uc2 Boston University\uc2 , said the decision to cross the border for abortions shows just how desperate many American women are to get the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ''When we see more people crossing the border for care it shows something is wrong in the U.S.,'' Ms. Huberfield said.\par \par \uc2 Mexican organizers say that even amid recent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights rulings in Mexico, the procedure is still not completely available nationwide. The Mexican Supreme Court ruling did not void criminal penalties at the local level, and private or state institutions can still ban the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups in Mexico adamantly opposed the top court's decision this month.\par \par \uc2 Marcial Padilla, director of the Mexico-based ConParticipaci\u243\'00\'F3n, told the Catholic news agency ACI Prensa that Mexico's Supreme Court decision would pressure senators to ''remove the protection of the right to life.''\par \par \uc2 The recent court decisions in Mexico have conveyed ''that a son or daughter does not deserve the same protection of the law before birth as after birth,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Some Americans seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in Mexico were surprised to find lasting restrictions south of the border.\par \par \uc2 Vanessa Jimenez Ruvalcaba, a Mexican activist who opens her home in Nuevo Le\u243\'00\'F3n state near the border to women seeking abortions, received a call in July from a father who traveled to the state with his daughter from Nebraska, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned after 12 weeks.\par \par \uc2 But Nuevo Le\u243\'00\'F3n only permits the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the case of incest, rape or when the mother's life is in danger. Ms. Jimenez said the father and daughter were turned away from a clinic before they were referred to her organization, the I Need to Abort Collective.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Jimenez and her fellow activists helped the young woman eventually access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 Even in the face of abortions bans, Mexican groups have formed a model known as ''accompaniment,'' in which they disseminate pills while providing medical counseling and psychological support to women.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Sanchez and her colleague, Carolina Castillo, said they have been implementing the model in Sonora for years. They are now fielding questions on social media from American women who fear facing criminal punishment for seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication in the United States. They say the women are relieved to hear from organizers who have spent years confronting such restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''We have been living for many years in a context of social and legal penalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Ms. Sanchez said. ''Which is why we, as women, have had to organize ourselves.''\par \par \uc2 Emiliano Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez Mega contributed research from Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 Emiliano Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez Mega contributed research from Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 Women in Saltillo, Mexico, gathered this month to commemorate a 2021 court ruling that began to dismantle the criminalization of \uc2 . Ver\u243\'00\'F3nica Cruz, right, a founder and leader of Las Libres. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY DANIEL BECERRIL/REUTERS; MARIAN CARRASQUERO FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A6. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230926ej9q0004c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
