\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Group Renames Itself as Mission Grows Beyond 'Pro-Choice' in the Post-Roe Era\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer \par \uc2 669 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2  is now Reproductive Freedom for All, as the post-Roe landscape reshapes the policy fights over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 , one of the country's largest advocacy groups for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, announced on Wednesday that it had changed its name, a switch that illustrates the issue's shifting politics after the Supreme Court overturned federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 For decades, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists cast their mission as a fight over health care and women's rights. NARAL's new name -- Reproductive Freedom for All -- is intended to align the group's goals with a different argument: In the post-Roe era, the battle for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is a fight for fundamental freedoms.\par \par \uc2 For \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters, the term ''pro-choice'' -- once widely used by Democrats -- feels particularly dated in a country where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are now determined by individual states and jurisdictions, leaders of the group said.\par \par \uc2 ''Pro-choice'' does not resonate with the moderate, younger and male voters who have become more engaged since the Supreme Court ended the nationwide right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year, said Mini Timmaraju, NARAL's president. The group's old name also failed, she said, to reflect the work of Black and Hispanic women long on the front lines of the fight for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 ''NARAL is incredibly resonant for the political world, but we're not necessarily in the business anymore of just winning political opinion within elected officials and policymakers,'' Ms. Timmaraju said. ''We are now in a much bigger fight for the heart and soul of the American people and those are folks who are brand-new to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate.''\par \par \uc2 Along with the new name, the group plans to increase its focus on state organizing and to adopt a broader approach, joining causes like eliminating the Senate filibuster, supporting voting rights and expanding the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Supporters and opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have both started to reposition themselves as the contours of the political battle rapidly change. Some Republicans have urged their party to move away from the term ''pro-life,'' arguing that the label has become politically damaging for their candidates. Others have leaned in: A few weeks before the ruling that overturned Roe v. Wade, Susan B. Anthony List, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy group, changed its name to Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America.\par \par \uc2 But NARAL's name change underscores how sharply \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics have shifted since the Supreme Court's decision last year in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 For years, surveys showed that those who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were more energized by the issue, viewing it as a litmus test for candidates. After the ruling, the politics flipped, with those supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights growing more motivated by the issue, while those opposed became less so.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights movement has shifted its message from talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as health care to casting the legality of the procedure as an American liberty. It's a message NARAL has been pushing since 2018, when an internal research project found the argument to be the most broadly persuasive.\par \par \uc2 It was a message many Democrats embraced in the 2022 midterm elections, casting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions as a kind of government overreach. Republicans, meanwhile, used similar arguments during the pandemic and often invoked the word ''freedom'' to criticize mask mandates and other public health measures.\par \par \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All is the fourth name change for the organization, which started as the National Association for the Repeal of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Laws in 1969. After Roe was decided in 1973, it became the National \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rights Action League. \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  was added to its name in 1993. Then, in 2003, the group became \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 , a change that coincided with a multimillion-dollar effort to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a central topic in the 2004 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A21. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230921ej9l0004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
