\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 'Terrible Mistake': Trump Criticizes DeSantis on 6-Week  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Swan and Maggie Haberman \par \uc2 923 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The former president, while denouncing his chief rival for the Republican nomination, also largely evaded questions on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , whose Supreme Court appointments led to the overturning of Roe v. Wade, harshly criticized his top rival in the Republican presidential primary, Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, for a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that he called a ''terrible thing.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump issued his broadside -- which could turn off socially conservative Republican primary voters, especially in Iowa, where evangelicals are a crucial voting bloc -- during an interview with the new host of NBC's ''Meet the Press,'' Kristen Welker, that was broadcast on Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether Mr. DeSantis went too far by signing a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, Mr. Trump replied: ''I think what he did is a terrible thing and a terrible mistake.''\par \par \uc2 Since announcing his candidacy last November -- just a week after Republicans underperformed expectations in midterm elections shaped by a backlash against the overturning of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling -- there has been no policy issue on which Mr. Trump has appeared more uncomfortable than on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In interview after interview since the repeal of the landmark 1973 Roe v. Wade \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , Mr. Trump has ducked questions about whether he would support a federal ban on most abortions at 15 weeks -- the baseline position of many Republicans, including the leading anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America.\par \par \uc2 With Ms. Welker on Sunday, Mr. Trump again refused to clarify his position.\par \par \uc2 ''What's going to happen is you're going to come up with a number of weeks or months,'' Mr. Trump said. ''You're going to come up with a number that's going to make people happy.''\par \par \uc2 He made a far-fetched promise that as president he would ''sit down with both sides'' and negotiate a deal on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would result in ''peace on that issue for the first time in 52 years.''\par \par \uc2 In reality, Mr. Trump -- who years ago said he supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights before switching his position in 2011 as he considered a presidential campaign that year -- appointed three conservative justices to the Supreme Court, providing a majority to reverse the Roe ruling. Democrats have made clear they plan to make Mr. Trump's role in Roe's end a key focus in the 2024 general election if he is the nominee.\par \par \uc2 What's more, Iowa's popular Republican governor, \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 , signed a measure similar to the one Mr. DeSantis made law. A spokesman for Ms. Reynolds did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 ''This further confirms to Iowans this is not the same Trump we once knew,'' said Steve Deace, a conservative Iowa talk show host who has endorsed Mr. DeSantis. ''This Trump only attacks Republicans from the left.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's advisers are mindful that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a political loser for Republicans in the 2022 midterm cycle. Mr. Trump himself, when a draft of the court opinion undoing Roe leaked publicly, told advisers it would hurt his party's electoral chances.\par \par \uc2 So as he looks ahead to the general election, Mr. Trump -- the front-runner for his party's nomination by a wide margin in national polls -- has tried to avoid taking a clear position in the hopes of not alienating additional voters.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm almost like a mediator in this case,'' Mr. Trump told Ms. Welker. Pushed on whether he would support a federal ban, he said: ''It could be state or it could be federal. I don't frankly care.''\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of S.B.A. Pro-Life America, who had previously described Mr. Trump's presidency as ''the most consequential in American history for the pro-life cause,'' indicated she was less than thrilled by Mr. Trump's attack on Mr. DeSantis's anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation. Yet she did not directly criticize him for it.\par \par \uc2 ''We're at a moment where we need a human rights advocate, someone who is dedicated to saving lives of children and serving mothers in need,'' Ms. Dannenfelser said on Sunday morning in response to Mr. Trump's comments on ''Meet the Press.'' ''Every single candidate should be clear on how they plan to do that.''\par \par \uc2 She added, ''It begins with focusing on extremes of the other side, and ambition and common sense on our own. Anything weaker than 15 weeks as a federal minimum standard makes no sense in this context.''\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Mr. DeSantis, Andrew Romeo, responded to Mr. Trump's attack by criticizing the former president for suggesting he could negotiate with Democrats on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding that the ''disastrous results of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  compromising with Democrats'' while he was president included ''$7 trillion in new debt'' and ''an unfinished border wall.''\par \par \uc2 Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , a strict social conservative who has run to the right of everyone in the Republican presidential field on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue, cast his former running mate's comments in stark moral terms.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continues to walk away from the pro-life legacy of our administration,'' Mr. Pence said in a statement Sunday morning. ''There's no negotiating when it comes to the life of the unborn. We will not rest, we will not relent, until the sanctity of life is restored to the center of American law in every state in the nation.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230918ej9i00042\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
