\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Clinics Will Resume Abortions in Wisconsin\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julie Bosman \par \uc2 690 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The clinic had suspended abortions during a legal battle over an 1849 law, which is ongoing.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  said on Thursday it would resume providing abortions in Wisconsin, where the procedure has been largely unavailable since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade more than a year ago.\par \par \uc2 The decision to restart services is a victory for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups in the state, where the issue has reshaped politics and defined a State Supreme Court election.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban from 1849 that was still on the books, but it had been unenforceable since Roe v. Wade made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal nationwide in 1973. \uc2 Josh Kaul\uc2 , the state attorney general and a Democrat, sued to overturn the law days after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decision in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The announcement from \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  came after a judge in Dane County, which includes Madison, issued a preliminary ruling in July saying that she did not believe the 1849 law made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal. ''There is no such thing as an '1849 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban' in Wisconsin,'' she wrote. The judge's final ruling is expected in the coming weeks. The dispute is likely to be decided by a higher court.\par \par \uc2 Beginning on Monday, patients can receive abortions at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics in Madison and Milwaukee, the organization said.\par \par \uc2 ''With the recent confirmation from the court that there is not an enforceable \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Wisconsin, our staff can now provide the full scope of sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care to anyone in Wisconsin who needs it, no matter what,'' Tanya Atkinson, president and chief executive of Planned Parenthood of Wisconsin, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  of Wisconsin, also a Democrat, called the announcement ''critically important news for Wisconsin women.''\par \par \uc2 ''Our fight to restore the same \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and freedoms Wisconsinites had up until the day the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe must continue,'' Mr. Evers said.\par \par \uc2 Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  denounced the decision.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a devastating day for preborn children and women facing unexpected pregnancies in Wisconsin,'' Gracie Skogman, the legislative director for Wisconsin Right to Life, said in a statement. ''\uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is once again showing that they value profit over the lives of vulnerable women and children.''\par \par \uc2 For the last year, many women in Wisconsin who were seeking abortions traveled over state lines into Illinois, frequently to a clinic in Waukegan, Ill., a city only miles from the border with Wisconsin. Planned Parenthood of Illinois saw a 600 percent increase in patients from Wisconsin since Roe was overturned, the organization said.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin, a critical swing state in the 2024 presidential contest, is in the midst of deep, ongoing political clashes over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , redistricting and elections.\par \par \uc2 In April, voters elected Janet Protasiewicz, a liberal candidate, to the State Supreme Court. She made her support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights clear, and won the seat by 11 percentage points that gave the court a new 4-to-3 liberal majority.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans in the State Legislature have threatened to impeach Justice Protasiewicz before she can rule on any case, hoping to block her from voting in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and help toss out heavily gerrymandered legislative maps that favor Republicans.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Republicans, who hold a majority in the State Senate, voted to fire \uc2 Meagan Wolfe\uc2 , the state's nonpartisan head of elections. Ms. Wolfe, the Wisconsin Elections Commission administrator since 2018, has been targeted by conservatives who say she is responsible for a lack of confidence in elections, though a 14-month investigation into the state results of the 2020 election found no evidence of significant fraud.\par \par \uc2 A legal battle over Ms. Wolfe's firing is expected to follow.\par \par \uc2 Protesters at the State Capitol in Madison, Wis., in 2022. Planned Parenthood's decision Thursday is a win for \uc2 -rights groups. (PHOTOGRAPH BY AMBER ARNOLD/WISCONSIN STATE JOURNAL, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS); For the last year, many women in Wisconsin seeking abortions traveled to a clinic in Waukegan, Ill. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CLAIRE SAVAGE/REPORT FOR AMERICA, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230915ej9f0004c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
