\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Despite State Restrictions, Abortions in U.S. Increase As Overall Access Improves\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amy Schoenfeld Walker and Allison McCann \par \uc2 931 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Legal abortions most likely increased in the United States in the first six months of the year compared with 2020, an analysis of new estimates shows, as states with more permissive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws absorbed patients traveling from those with bans and access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills via telemedicine continued to expand.\par \par \uc2 New research from the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  offers the latest view of legal abortions since the Supreme Court's Dobbs decision last year upended access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide and allowed more than a dozen states to ban or restrict the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The data suggests that thousands of women have crossed state lines to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in the face of restrictions at home. It also indicates a rise in abortions among those living in states where the procedure is legal.\par \par \uc2 ''You have two forces at work,'' said Caitlin Myers, an economist at \uc2 Middlebury College\uc2 , who reviewed the Guttmacher report. ''On the one hand, you have people trapped in ban states, and, on the other, you have people in a whole lot of the country where access has improved.''\par \par \uc2 Altogether, about 511,000 abortions were estimated to have occurred in areas where the procedure was legal in the first six months of 2023, a review of Guttmacher's data shows, compared with about 465,000 abortions nationwide in a six-month period of 2020.\par \par \uc2 Abortions rose in nearly every state where the procedure remains legal, but the change was most visible in states bordering those with total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Many of these states loosened \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, and providers opened new clinics to serve patients coming from elsewhere. In Illinois, for example, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal, abortions rose an estimated 69 percent in 2023 compared with the same period in 2020, to about 45,000 from 26,000.\par \par \uc2 Other states with restrictive neighbors, like Colorado, Kansas, New Mexico and South Carolina, also had jumps in estimated counts of abortions.\par \par \uc2 Arizona, Georgia and Indiana, by contrast, sought to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and all three states had drops in their estimates. Arizona and Georgia have gestational limits on the procedure, and Indiana recently enacted a total ban.\par \par \uc2 Researchers emphasized that the rise in abortions so far in 2023 does not mean that state bans have had no impact on access.\par \par \uc2 ''Travel doesn't come without a cost,'' said Isaac Maddow-Zimet, a data scientist at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  and the lead researcher on the institute's report. ''Just because someone isn't denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't mean it was an easy experience. And we know that some can't leave their state.''\par \par \uc2 There are no estimates yet of women who were denied abortions or those who were unable to get them. The increases in most states may also mask the number of abortions prevented by the bans.\par \par \uc2 ''Abortions had begun rising in advance of Dobbs, and they may have continued to rise even more steeply than observed if it weren't for the bans,'' Dr. Myers said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 's report is based on a survey of physical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and telehealth and virtual providers. The institute, which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, does not contact all clinics in each state but relies on a sample to estimate the number of abortions.\par \par \uc2 The report excludes abortions obtained outside of the formal health care system, through, for example, pills mailed into states with bans from other countries or states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal. Other data suggests that thousands of people, especially those living in states with bans, have ordered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills online from overseas.\par \par \uc2 Populous states like California, Florida, Illinois and New York had the most abortions. Because the researchers relied on a statistical model, they reported a range of uncertainty in their counts, and there was more uncertainty in states with more \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. Data was not collected from the 14 states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in effect in the first part of the year.\par \par \uc2 Researchers compared this year's data with 2020, the most recent year of their annual provider survey and the last year before major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions took effect. (Texas passed a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in 2021, before the Dobbs decision.) Abortions were already rising that year, reversing a decades-long decline.\par \par \uc2 The data may also capture effects of other policy shifts in recent years, including the Biden administration's decision during the pandemic to allow abortions via telemedicine, as well as other unknown impacts of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The state-level monthly estimates are in line with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statistics from January through March of this year released by WeCount, another group that surveys providers.\par \par \uc2 Both reports show significant increases in abortions in states without \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, a change that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates and legislators are watching closely.\par \par \uc2 ''There is the feeling among legislators that we've passed strong laws here and yet our neighbors in Colorado or Illinois are allowing these businesses to pop up on our border,'' said Katie Daniel, the state policy director at Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America.\par \par \uc2 Researchers caution that more work is needed to better assess both the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions as well as the explosion of access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills through telehealth and underground networks.\par \par \uc2 The picture is expected to shift later this year, as more restrictions take effect. Guttmacher researchers collected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  data before legislatures enacted bans and restrictions in Indiana, North Carolina and South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Claire Cain Miller and Margot Sanger-Katz contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A11. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230911ej9b00020\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
