\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Justice Dept. Asks Justices to Hear Case Over Limiting Distribution of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 By Abbie VanSickle \par \uc2 788 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The case sets up a showdown before the court on the availability of the most common method of terminating pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department asked the Supreme Court on Friday evening to hear a challenge to the availability of a commonly used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, raising the possibility that the justices will rule on the fate of the drug.\par \par \uc2 The move sets up a showdown over access to the medication, mifepristone, which is used in more than half of all pregnancy terminations in the United States, and would bring the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  back to the court more than a year after the justices eliminated the constitutional right to it. The case centers on the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's approval of the drug more than two decades ago and could have broader implications for the pharmaceutical industry, including the agency's regulatory authority over other medications.\par \par \uc2 The request came in response to a ruling by a federal appeals court last month that upheld the legality of the pill but imposed significant restrictions on its distribution. The decision by a three-judge panel of the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit would prevent the drug from being sent through the mail or prescribed by telemedicine.\par \par \uc2 For now, the pill remains available because the Supreme Court determined in April that access to the drug would remain unchanged until the appeals process finished.\par \par \uc2 The court, which is likely to act in the coming months, could decline to hear the case, leaving the lower court's decision in place. Or it could agree to hear the case in the coming term, which starts in October, and issue a ruling by the summer.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department, in its filing, called the appeals court ruling an ''unprecedented decision'' that ''would threaten to severely disrupt the pharmaceutical industry and prevent F.D.A. from fulfilling its statutory responsibilities according to its scientific judgment.''\par \par \uc2 The department also vouched for the efficacy and safety of the pill, pointing to studies that have shown that ''serious adverse events are exceedingly rare.'' It argued that losing access to the pill ''would be damaging for women and health care providers around the nation.''\par \par \uc2 ''For many patients, mifepristone is the best method to lawfully terminate their early pregnancies,'' lawyers for the government wrote. ''They may choose mifepristone over surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of medical necessity, a desire for privacy or past trauma.''\par \par \uc2 The consequences for the pharmaceutical industry were vast, the department wrote in its brief, emphasizing that the F.D.A.'s approval had been in effect for years. Lower-court decisions in the case signaled ''the first time any court has restricted access to an F.D.A.-approved drug based on disagreement with F.D.A.'s expert judgment about the conditions required to assure that drug's safe use,'' it added.\par \par \uc2 Danco Laboratories, a manufacturer of mifepristone, filed a similar request hours earlier, outlining the wide-ranging reverberations of any decision.\par \par \uc2 ''For the women and teenage girls, health care providers and states that depend on F.D.A.'s actions to ensure safe and effective \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care is available, this case matters tremendously,'' the company wrote. ''And for the pharmaceutical and biotechnology industry, permitting judicial second-guessing of F.D.A.'s scientific evaluations of data will have a wildly destabilizing effect.''\par \par \uc2 Danco added that the case sought to limit access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than determine the safety of a drug approved in 2000. The company argued that the lower court had moved to limit access to the drug ''at the request of a group of plaintiffs who do not prescribe or use the drug and whose real disagreement with F.D.A. is that they oppose all forms of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 The case arose from a lawsuit filed in federal court in Amarillo, Texas, months after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in June 2022. The plaintiffs, a coalition of four doctors and a group opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , asked a federal judge to invalidate the F.D.A.'s approval of the pill, the first in a two-drug regimen for terminating a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In March, Judge Matthew J. Kacsmaryk of the Northern District of Texas, a Trump appointee known for his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  views, issued a preliminary ruling that said the F.D.A.'s approval should be suspended, removing mifepristone from the market.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration asked the Supreme Court to intervene, and the court ordered that mifepristone would remain widely available until the appeals process concluded.\par \par \uc2 Pam Belluck contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Pam Belluck contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A11. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230911ej9b0000p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
