\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Mexico Court Strikes Down Criminal Code For  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Simon Romero and Emiliano Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez Mega \par \uc2 669 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The decision builds on an earlier high court ruling and reflects how Latin American countries are expanding women's rights.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's Supreme Court decriminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide on Wednesday in a sweeping decision that builds on an earlier ruling giving officials the authority to allow the procedure on a state-by-state basis.\par \par \uc2 The court struck down the federal penal code that criminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , deeming it ''unconstitutional'' and making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legally accessible in all federal health institutions across the country. It also ruled against bans on medical providers, including midwives, who perform the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The ruling in Mexico, a predominantly Catholic country of 130 million people, points to how nations in Latin America are taking a leading role in broadening \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm very moved and very proud,'' said Rebeca Ramos, executive director of GIRE, a leading \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group that filed an injunction last year against the Mexican regulation from 1931 that criminalized the procedure. ''This makes possible what we had not achieved in many years, which is that at least in certain institutions all across the country legal and safe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services can be provided.''\par \par \uc2 The Mexican Supreme Court first ruled that criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unconstitutional in 2021, but that ruling applied only to the state of Coahuila, which borders Texas. Other Mexican states have already eliminated criminal penalties for the procedure, with Aguascalientes becoming the 12th to do so last week.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's ruling has no effect on local laws, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains illegal in 20 of the country's 32 states. But even in those states, women can now legally seek \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in federal hospitals and clinics. The ruling also prohibits employees at these facilities from being penalized for carrying out abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''Hopefully, this is the preamble so that the court can go state by state helping local legislatures eliminate the crime of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , since legislators do not do their job,'' said Ver\u243\'00\'F3nica Cruz, founder of the feminist group Las Libres in Guanajuato, Mexico.\par \par \uc2 In addition to Mexico, countries such as Colombia, Argentina, Uruguay and Guyana have moved to either legalize or decriminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The regional trend stands in contrast to the United States, where the Supreme Court's overturning of Roe v. Wade in 2022 placed the country among a small group of nations making it harder for women to end their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Mexico's Supreme Court said the ''criminalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  constitutes an act of gender-based violence and discrimination, as it perpetuates the stereotype that women and people with the capacity to get pregnant can only freely exercise their sexuality to procreate and reinforces the gender role that imposes motherhood as a compulsory destiny.''\par \par \uc2 Some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists in Mexico were quick to respond to the ruling. ''This sends the message to society that the life of a son or daughter can be taken before they are born,'' said Marcial Padilla, director of ConParticipaci\u243\'00\'F3n, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, in comments to ACI Prensa, a Catholic news agency.\par \par \uc2 The court's decision reflects profound changes in Mexican society and some of its institutions. Much of the country remains culturally conservative, but decades of feminist activism have reshaped how many people think about women's rights. \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have also fought to have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases heard by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the Supreme Court lost some conservative justices, and a former chief justice, Arturo Zald\u237\'00\'EDvar Lelo de Larrea, who was raised by practicing Catholic parents, emerged as an unexpected champion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''We're on a very good path,'' said Ms. Ramos. ''This is a recognition that women and people with the ability to gestate have agency and we are first-class citizens. That democracy is coming to us, as well.''\par \par \uc2 A demonstration last year in Mexico City on International Safe \uc2  Day. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MARCO UGARTE/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230907ej9700044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
