\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 The Dramatic, and Unverifiable, Story About an ' \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Survivor'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer and Nicholas Nehamas \par \uc2 1,202 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida has been retelling Miriam Hopper's 1955 birth story. The details are jarring, highly unusual and unverifiable.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  wanted to dodge a debate question about a six-week federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. So the Florida governor pulled out a personal story, one that had recently become part of his pitch to voters on the need for greater regulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''I know a lady in Florida named Penny,'' he said. ''She survived multiple \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  attempts. She was left discarded in a pan. Fortunately, her grandmother saved her and brought her to a different hospital.'' He then pivoted to attack Democrats for their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''extremism.''\par \par \uc2 The jarring anecdote caught the attention of viewers on social media, who speculated that Mr. DeSantis was fabricating the story.\par \par \uc2 But Penny does exist. Mr. DeSantis's campaign says the governor has met her. She is Miriam Hopper, who goes by Penny and is an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist who lives in Florida and calls herself an ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  survivor.''\par \par \uc2 The details of Ms. Hopper's birth in 1955 are impossible to verify. But at least one prominent obstetrician noted that medical advances and practices had changed so dramatically in the nearly seven decades since then that her story had little relevance for the current debate about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and policy. At the time of her birth, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was illegal. Even an attempted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have resulted in fines and imprisonment for a provider.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hopper did not return a call for comment this week. But she told her story in an online video posted by Protect Life Michigan, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy group. The video, part of a broader campaign, was posted in September 2022 amid a campaign against a ballot initiative that would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Michigan's Constitution. So-called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  survivors have been a staple of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement for years, frequently appearing in campaign ads and testifying on Capitol Hill in favor of federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 According to Ms. Hopper, her mother sought medical care at a clinic in central Florida in 1955 because of bleeding and other complications. She was 23 weeks pregnant, right at the outer edge of when a fetus is considered able to survive outside the womb. The doctor who examined Ms. Hopper's mother said he could not hear a heartbeat. He induced labor, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''You do not want this baby to live -- if it lives, it will be a burden on you all of your life,'' Ms. Hopper says the doctor told her parents before instructing a nurse to discard the baby -- ''dead or alive.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hopper said she had weighed one pound 11 ounces at her birth. The nurse ''placed me in a bedpan on the back porch of the clinic,'' she said. When her grandmother and aunt arrived, they found Ms. Hopper. Her grandmother called the police. A nurse helped take Ms. Hopper to a hospital in Lakeland, Fla., where she survived several bouts of pneumonia.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hopper's mother, aunt, father and grandmother have died. It does not appear that the incident was covered in news reports.\par \par \uc2 After an extended stay, Ms. Hopper went home and had a ''great life.'' She married her high school sweetheart, had two children of her own and has seven grandchildren. ''Life has value, and all lives matter,'' she said, at the end of the video.\par \par \uc2 In a 2013 interview with the Florida radio station WFSU, conducted in the middle of a statehouse debate over new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, Ms. Hopper said that her story was based on what she had been told by her family. She said that her father, raised during the Great Depression, did not want another child and that she suspected a botched \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sent her mother to the hospital with the complications.\par \par \uc2 Diane Horvath, an obstetrician and gynecologist who performs abortions until 34 weeks at a clinic in Maryland, said it was difficult to parse Ms. Hopper's account.\par \par \uc2 ''There's a lot of parts of this story that don't make sense to me,'' she said, noting that 68 years ago, physicians had lacked the current-day technologies to keep very premature babies alive.\par \par \uc2 In the 1950s, death was ''virtually ensured'' when an infant was delivered at or before 24 weeks of gestation, according to a report published in 2017 by the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  and the \uc2 Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By contrast, a study conducted last year by a team of neonatologists found that nearly 56 percent of infants who are born at 23 weeks survive -- if they receive aggressive treatment in a neonatal intensive care unit.\par \par \uc2 Even if Ms. Hopper's story is accurate, it's not particularly germane to a discussion of current \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices or regulations, Dr. Horvath said.\par \par \uc2 ''It doesn't represent the reality of medical practice at this moment,'' she said. ''It's not really relevant to what we should be talking about when we talk about access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Fewer than 1 percent of abortions occur after 21 weeks' gestation, according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 . Such procedures are generally difficult to receive, with only a limited number of facilities offering them.\par \par \uc2 The Republican presidential primary debate wasn't the first time Mr. DeSantis had told a version of this story. He debuted the narrative last weekend at a town hall in Nashua, N.H., amid a shift in his messaging that was meant to evoke a more personal touch.\par \par \uc2 The moment came in response to a question from a voter who described himself as a ''traditional Catholic'' and asked Mr. DeSantis, who has signed a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Florida and has tried to dodge questions on whether he supports a similar ban nationwide, how he would ''protect the life of the unborn.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis said he had met ''Penny'' in person in central Florida, and then launching into a similar version of the story he told on Wednesday night, including the details about Ms. Hopper's grandmother and the pan, and trying to paint Democrats as the extremists on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''You know, that's a very callous thing to happen,'' Mr. DeSantis said. Most Democratic officeholders say the government should not legislate such decisions and should leave them to a woman and her doctor.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Tyson, a top DeSantis campaign adviser, said the governor was making an effort to talk more about the people he had encountered on the trail. His campaign did not provide details about the circumstances of his meeting with Ms. Hopper.\par \par \uc2 ''He's out there -- he's meeting people,'' Mr. Tyson said in an interview after the debate. ''He's hearing their stories as he gets across the country. And I think that's why you saw he had a moment there, because it does take a toll on you.''\par \par \uc2 Questioned in Thursday's debate about an \uc2  ban, Ron DeSantis told a story about a ''lady in Florida named Penny.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENNY HOLSTON/THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230826ej8q00046\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
