\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In S. Carolina, Court Upholds  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,228 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The ruling, by a newly all-male bench, allows the state's new six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to take effect.\par \par \uc2 The South Carolina Supreme Court on Wednesday upheld the state's new near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by a 4-1 vote, reversing a decision it had made in January that struck down a similar ban and declared that the State Constitution's protections for privacy included a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The court's decision was not unexpected, because the makeup of the bench had changed, and Republicans in the State Legislature had passed a new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in the hopes that it would find a friendlier audience with the new court. The decision in January was written by the court's only female justice; she retired and South Carolina now has the nation's only all-male high court.\par \par \uc2 The decision repeated what the justices said in January about a right to privacy in the State Constitution, but said the Legislature had addressed the concerns in the first law and ''balanced'' the interests of pregnant women with those of the fetus.\par \par \uc2 ''To be sure, the 2023 Act infringes on a woman's right of privacy and bodily autonomy,'' Justice John Kittredge wrote for the majority.\par \par \uc2 But, he added, ''We think it is important to reiterate: we are constrained by the express language in the South Carolina Constitution that prohibits only 'unreasonable invasions of privacy.'\par \par \uc2 ''The legislature has made a policy determination that, at a certain point in the pregnancy, a woman's interest in autonomy and privacy does not outweigh the interest of the unborn child to live.''\par \par \uc2 The new law bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after embryonic cardiac activity can be detected, which is generally around six weeks of pregnancy. Until now, South Carolina had allowed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until 22 weeks, which had increasingly made the state a haven for women seeking abortions as other Southern states banned the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The leaders of the Republican majority in the Senate celebrated the decision, saying in a statement that South Carolina was ''no longer an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  destination but a refuge for the unborn.''\par \par \uc2 They suggested they would push for a total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is the goal of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups.\par \par \uc2 Jenny Black, the chief executive of Planned Parenthood South Atlantic, said the decision would force women to carry pregnancies against their will, and puts the ''dangerous politicization of South Carolina's highest court on full display.''\par \par \uc2 ''This \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is nearly identical to the ban struck down by this court just months ago -- the only thing that has changed is the makeup of the court,'' she said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina, one of the nation's most reliably red states, has become an unlikely battleground over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the year since the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade, which for 50 years protected a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the federal constitution.\par \par \uc2 The state's Republican-controlled Legislature had passed a law in 2021 banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when cardiac activity can be detected, typically around six weeks of pregnancy, and that law took effect when Roe was overturned. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates and medical providers sued, as they did against bans in other states, arguing that a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was protected in the state's constitution, which clearly states a right to privacy.\par \par \uc2 The court surprised many \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  supporters in January when it agreed with them. But the court's 3-2 decision also said that right ''was not absolute, and must be balanced against the state's interest in protecting unborn life.''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers had been trying to pass an even stricter ban, one starting at conception, and quickly set about trying to craft a law that would satisfy the justices' concerns in the January decision.\par \par \uc2 They hoped to take advantage of a key change on the bench: the January decision had been written by Kaye Hearn, the court's only female justice, who has since retired. The legislature replaced Justice Hearn with a man, who joined the majority Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Still, Republican leadership had run into unexpected opposition from female lawmakers. Nicknaming themselves ''the Sister Senators,'' the only five women in the State Senate -- a group that included three Republicans -- blocked a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by filibustering until the legislative session ran out.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2 , also a Republican, then called a rare special session to get the legislature to vote on a bill for a six-week ban. While the Sister Senators held out on their opposition, the men who had joined them in blocking a total ban supported the six-week law.\par \par \uc2 As long as the Legislature could not pass a new law, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained legal in South Carolina until 22 weeks of pregnancy. Many lawmakers worried about statistics showing that the number of abortions in South Carolina had increased largely because so many women were coming from other states that had passed bans.\par \par \uc2 The oral arguments in the case in June revolved around whether a six-week ban affords women enough time to have what one justice in his January concurrence called ''meaningful choice'' in deciding whether to end a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 A lawyer for the plaintiffs argued the law was ''materially indistinguishable'' from the one the court struck down in January, and that six weeks is too early for many women to know they are pregnant and make arrangements for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Doctors date pregnancy to the first day of a woman's last monthly period, so six weeks is roughly two weeks after she has missed a regular period. The state requires her to have multiple doctor visits and scans before she can get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the state argued that the Legislature had ''in very good faith'' addressed the objections in the court's January decision, adding three provisions including one that explicitly states that emergency contraception -- known as the morning after pill, which prevents rather than terminates pregnancy -- would remain legal.\par \par \uc2 Women could ''adjust their behavior accordingly,'' they argued, using pills instead of resorting to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The lawyers noted that according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , 45 percent of abortions nationwide in 2020 occurred in the first six weeks, arguing that this indicated many women did in fact know they were pregnant at six weeks. (Most abortions -- 93 percent -- take place in the first trimester.)\par \par \uc2 Grayson Lambert, a lawyer for the state, argued that women could know they were pregnant seven to 10 days after conception, or at three to four weeks of pregnancy -- before she would be expecting her regular period.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice Donald W. Beatty, who sided with the majority to overturn the earlier ban, was dubious: ''Could know?'' he asked. ''Anything is possible, what about probable?'' He was the sole dissenter in the court's decision on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In his dissenting opinion, Chief Justice Beatty wrote: ''The result will essentially force an untold number of affected women to give birth without their consent. I am hard-pressed to think of a greater governmental intrusion by a political body.''\par \par \uc2 The state's Republican-controlled Legislature had passed a law in 2021 banning \uc2  when cardiac activity can be detected. (PHOTOGRAPH BY AUDRA MELTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A12) This article appeared in print on page A1, A12. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230824ej8o0003h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
