\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Looking Past Debate, G.O.P. Candidates Are Spending Big on Ads\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rebecca Davis O'Brien \par \uc2 1,634 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Many of the party's presidential candidates have spent heavily as they try to introduce themselves to voters. Ads for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , meanwhile, look ahead to a matchup with President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Americans who don't live in early presidential nominating states -- that is to say, most Americans -- might not be aware of the advertising wars already underway in the 2024 campaign. For months, Republican candidates have been on the airwaves, plugging away at themes we are likely to see more of during the party's high-stakes first debate on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 This year, they face an unusual challenge: Former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  has effectively taken on the role of an incumbent. The rest of the candidates have spent tens of millions of dollars to introduce themselves to primary voters, stake out policy positions and chart a course to the general election -- only to be overshadowed by Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 ''I think of advertising as spitting out Ping-Pong balls,'' said \uc2 Ken Goldstein\uc2 , a professor of politics at the \uc2 University of San Francisco\uc2  who has researched political advertising. Mr. Trump's influence, he said, means that other candidates' messages often do not reach voters: ''There's this big, huge wind blowing those Ping-Pongs back in their face.''\par \par \uc2 The gamble for the challengers is that the wind will shift -- or go away entirely.\par \par \uc2 ''If your opponent is winning 57 percent of the vote and you have 2, there is zero percent chance you are making that difference up with advertising,'' said Lynn Vavreck, a professor of political science at the \uc2 University of California, Los Angeles\uc2 . Even in a typical election year, Dr. Vavreck said, the persuasive effects of campaign television advertisements are small, and fade fast.\par \par \uc2 ''That doesn't mean everybody polling under 10 percent should stop,'' Dr. Vavreck said. ''They need to be seen as a candidate who's taking it seriously. That includes advertising.''\par \par \uc2 Here's a look at some of the themes and strategies emerging in the campaign advertising for the more than a dozen Republican candidates.\par \par \uc2 How are the candidates dealing with Trump?\par \par \uc2 Many of the Republican candidates, particularly the lower-polling ones, do not address the former president at all in their ads. Others take indirect shots at him.\par \par \uc2 Former Gov. \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  of New Jersey and his allies are the loudest exception.\par \par \uc2 In a series of acerbic ads, a super PAC supporting Mr. Christie has ripped Mr. Trump over his indictments, his electoral losses and his impeachments. In an ad that ran nationally after Mr. Christie qualified for the debate on Wednesday, the narrator goads Mr. Trump to join him onstage: ''Are you a chicken, or just a loser?''\par \par \uc2 Ads on New Hampshire and Iowa stations by the main super PAC backing Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida have criticized Mr. Trump elliptically -- for instance, asking why the former president is attacking Republican governors rather than focusing his attention on Democrats and President Biden. (Mr. Trump, one ad concludes, ''is all about himself.'') In another ad, a man covers his Trump bumper sticker with a DeSantis one.\par \par \uc2 Other groups not connected to any candidate have spent millions opposing Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 Win It Back, a super PAC that shares leadership with the Club for Growth, a conservative anti-tax group, has bought $5.6 million in ads, according to an analysis by AdImpact, a media-tracking firm. The ads including lengthy broadcast spots in Iowa and South Carolina that feature voters who once supported Mr. Trump but are now looking for a new candidate.\par \par \uc2 A political action committee supporting Mr. Trump, in the meantime, has turned its attention to the general election, with a 60-second ad attacking Mr. Biden.\par \par \uc2 Who is spending the most?\par \par \uc2 The main super PAC supporting Mr. DeSantis has spent $17 million buying television ads, while MAGA Inc, a PAC supporting Mr. Trump, has spent $21.4 million, according to the AdImpact analysis.\par \par \uc2 But that doesn't come close to the $46.2 million spent in support of Senator Tim Scott of South Carolina, between his campaign and a super PAC backing him. That figure includes a huge outlay on ads planned for the weeks after Wednesday's debate.\par \par \uc2 A PAC supporting Nikki Haley has spent $8.4 million on ads -- about the same amount spent on ads for Gov. \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  of North Dakota, between his largely self-funded campaign and a super PAC supporting him. Ms. Haley's ads include broadcast spots in New Hampshire and Iowa that draw on her experience as ambassador to the United Nations, and a clip describing her as the ''surprise rock star'' of the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Perry Johnson, a businessman who has lent his own campaign $8.4 million, has spent $1.9 million on ads. One ad that ran in Illinois features him walking determinedly through a blizzard of computer-generated charts and mathematical equations, representing his love of statistics and quality standards.\par \par \uc2 Many of his online ads have included a plea for donations to get him over the threshold of 40,000 donors required to participate in Wednesday's debate. (The \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  said on Tuesday that he had not qualified.)\par \par \uc2 Pleas for donors to contribute just $1 -- a clear attempt at meeting the debate threshold -- also featured heavily in digital ads by SOS America PAC, which is supporting Mayor \uc2 Francis X. Suarez\uc2  of Miami. The super PAC has spent $1.7 million on ads, the AdImpact analysis shows.\par \par \uc2 What themes are emerging?\par \par \uc2 Border security, China, a touch of Ukraine, inflation, cleaning up Washington. And, of course, the culture wars.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis's super PAC has amplified his resistance to coronavirus lockdown orders, and lauds him for ''pushing back against the woke left.'' In a video clip in one of the ads, he says: ''If you're coming for the rights of parents, I'm standing in your way.'' The group's ads have also gone after \uc2 Disney\uc2  and Bud Light.\par \par \uc2 Another ad from the group aims to appeal to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  voters, quoting Mr. Trump relaying criticism that the six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban Mr. DeSantis signed in Florida was ''too harsh.''\par \par \uc2 The super PAC supporting Ms. Haley ran a digital ad in May that highlighted her ''pro-life'' voting record in South Carolina, and criticized Mr. Biden for encouraging protests after Roe v. Wade was overturned. ''We need a president who unites Americans,'' she says, ''even on the toughest subjects.''\par \par \uc2 Perhaps no candidate has made more of his opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , and his ads have addressed this head-on. One of his longer ads focuses entirely on his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  record.\par \par \uc2 Both Ms. Haley and Mr. Pence have used the phrase ''the ash heap of history'' in stump speeches that wind up in their ads -- Ms. Haley in reference to the future of ''Communist China,'' and Mr. Pence in reference to the overturning of Roe.\par \par \uc2 What's the visual style of the ads?\par \par \uc2 So far, most of the ads have been pretty ''cut-and-paste,'' as Mr. Goldstein put it. Inspiring personal stories, a few grim shots of Mr. Biden, uplifting music, a few wives offering endorsements of their husbands, adoring crowds, American flags.\par \par \uc2 The entrepreneur \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  (total ad outlay: $334,000, plus $240,000 more from a supporting super PAC) has made a slightly different presentation. Unlike other candidates' campaign ads, his spots do not rely on dramatic voice-overs, but feature him in a room, speaking directly at the camera.\par \par \uc2 The super PAC supporting Mr. Scott tried another approach to introduce the candidate: ads featuring prospective voters speaking to the camera about the senator, as if speaking to their neighbor: ''Have you seen him work a crowd?'' ''Did you see Tim Scott on 'The View'?'' ''He will crush Joe Biden.''\par \par \uc2 Who has the best \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  cameo?\par \par \uc2 Does a 40-year-old endorsement count? An ad for former Gov. \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2  of Arkansas -- seeking $1 donations to help him reach the debate stage -- consists almost entirely of a short clip of former President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , sitting at his desk in the Oval Office and addressing the camera.\par \par \uc2 ''If you believe in the values I believe in, there's a man you should get to know,'' Mr. Reagan says. ''His name is Asa Hutchinson.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Reagan had nominated Mr. Hutchinson to serve as the United States attorney for the Western District of Arkansas. The clip appears to be from an endorsement for Mr. Hutchinson in the 1986 Senate race. (He lost to Dale Bumpers, an incumbent Democrat.)\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ramaswamy invokes Mr. Reagan in a digital ad, saying the former president ''led us out of our national malaise'' carried over from the 1970s. Mr. Ramaswamy pledges to lead America out of its latest ''national identity crisis.''\par \par \uc2 A PAC for Gov. Ron DeSantis has spent $17 million. Ads in New Hampshire and Iowa take an indirect approach to criticizing the front-runner, Donald J. Trump. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JOE BUGLEWICZ FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Senator Tim Scott of South Carolina and his allies have spent the most, $46.2 million, including a huge outlay planned for the weeks after Wednesday's debate. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JORDAN GALE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Ads for many candidates have appealed to voters opposed to \uc2 , including one ad that targets Mr. Trump for criticizing Florida's \uc2  law as ''too harsh.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY MERIDITH KOHUT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Unlike other candidates, who often use dramatic voice-overs in their ads, Vivek Ramaswamy's TV spots usually feature him speaking directly at the camera. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CHRISTIAN MONTERROSA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230823ej8n0004z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
