\par\par \uc2 NEWS ANALYSIS\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 An Arcane Ballot Issue Shows  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 's Potency to Reshape Elections\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer \par \uc2 1,311 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 August 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Dobbs ruling has turned a coalition of liberal, swing and moderate Republican voters into a political force. Even in August in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  of Ohio, a Republican, argued that Tuesday's vote over how to amend the State Constitution was about protecting the state from a flood of special interest money. Secretary of State \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2 , another Republican, urged voters to protect the ''very foundational rules'' of their constitution.\par \par \uc2 But Ohio voters clearly didn't buy it. About three million of them showed up for a vote dominated by the debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights -- an issue that was not technically on the ballot, but was the undeniable force that transformed what would have normally been a little-noticed election over an arcane legislative proposal into a national event.\par \par \uc2 For decades, a majority of Americans supported some form of legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the Supreme Court's Dobbs decision overturning Roe v. Wade has shifted the political intensity on the issue, reshaping a once mostly silent coalition of liberal, swing and moderate Republican voters into a political force. It's a force Democrats are working hard to harness in elections across the country next year, often with ballot measures, and it's a power Republicans have yet to figure out how to match, or at least manage.\par \par \uc2 ''We've taken it on the chin since Dobbs,'' said Michael Gonidakis, president of Ohio Right to Life in Columbus, Ohio, who helped organize efforts supporting the proposal on Tuesday. ''One of the things we learned was to get out in front and get out ahead and don't wait because you'll be run over by the train.''\par \par \uc2 Officially, Ohio voters were being asked whether to make it harder to amend the State Constitution by raising the threshold to enact a new constitutional amendment from a simple majority to 60 percent and increase the requirements to get such initiatives on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 In remarks before party activists and in strategy memos, Republican officials acknowledged that the measure was an attempt to make it harder for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters to pass a ballot measure scheduled for November that would add an amendment protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to the State Constitution. Those private comments fueled a firestorm of national media coverage, nearly $20 million in political spending and surprisingly high turnout for an election in the dead of summer.\par \par \uc2 Nearly twice as many people voted on the Ohio measure than cast ballots in primaries for governor, Senate, House and other marquee statewide races last year.\par \par \uc2 The power of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to mobilize a majority coalition has armed Democrats with a potent new political tool, particularly in crucial battlegrounds like Michigan, Ohio and Arizona where Republican legislatures moved quickly to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Already, Democrats are looking ahead to 2024, with activists in around 10 states considering efforts to put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in state constitutions.\par \par \uc2 If they succeed, those efforts could help boost Democratic turnout in key states -- including Arizona, both a presidential battleground and home to a key Senate race next year, and Florida, a traditional swing state that has slipped away from the party in recent elections.\par \par \uc2 The Ohio defeat was powered by a strong showing from Democratic and swing voters. Opponents overperformed in some critical suburban battleground counties. In Athens, for example, a Democratic bastion and the home of \uc2 Ohio University\uc2 , voters opposed the measure by 71 percent. Last fall, former Representative \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , the Democratic candidate who lost a Senate race to J.D. Vance, a Republican, won the county by 61 percent.\par \par \uc2 But there were also signs that moderate, and even some conservative voters, were against the idea. In November, 66 percent of voters in Defiance County, a conservative area in the northwest corner of the state, backed Mr. Vance. Only 61 percent supported the proposal to amend the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 ''We've never seen this amount of spending or attention on an issue related to ballot measure processes and I can tell you it's not because everyone inherently cares about what the rules are on ballot issues,'' said Kelly Hall, executive director of the Fairness Project, which has helped run nearly three dozen ballot measures. ''The attention from both sides can only be attributed to the implications for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue.''\par \par \uc2 After spending nearly a half century pushing against Roe, Republicans have struggled to adapt, trapped between a party base that still largely opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and a country that broadly supports them.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  played a significant role in motivating key parts of the Democratic base to the polls during the midterm elections. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related initiatives won in all six states where they appeared on the ballot in 2022 and likely helped to boost turnout for the Democratic ticket in those places. In red and purple states -- Michigan, Kentucky and Kansas -- the vote for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights was between 52 percent and 59 percent -- just below the 60 percent threshold Ohio Republicans were trying to set.\par \par \uc2 This year, Democrats prevailed in a contest for the Wisconsin Supreme Court where their candidate focused on her support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in a state with a law banning the procedure.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in Ohio until 22 weeks of pregnancy. After the Supreme Court decision, a law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at six weeks took effect but was blocked by a state judge while litigation proceeded -- which it still is.\par \par \uc2 With Tuesday's referendum, Republican lawmakers attempted a version of the kind of two-track strategy their party had done successfully for years. To conservative voters, they emphasized the measure's role in raising the bar for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment while, to other audiences, they talked about other potential impacts.\par \par \uc2 For Republicans, the challenge is that most of their voters are out-of-step with the broader electorate. Polling conducted last month by The New York Times/Siena College found that 61 percent of voters believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be all or mostly legal, a view shared by majorities in every region of the country, across all income levels, ages, racial groups and of both men and women. But 57 percent of Republicans believe the procedure should be all or mostly illegal.\par \par \uc2 On the presidential primary campaign trail, Republican candidates have largely tried to avoid spending too much time on the specifics of the issue. Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida signed legislation prohibiting most abortions after six weeks in his home state, but has stopped short of embracing a federal ban.\par \par \uc2 Others, including Senator Tim Scott, back a 15-week federal ban. And former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , who takes credit for appointing three of the Supreme Court Justices who voted to overturn Roe, has not endorsed any kind of restrictions. He's expressed concerns that moving too far to the right on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cost Republicans votes, saying it could make it ''very, very hard to win an election.''\par \par \uc2 But Republicans are unlikely to evade the topic in the general election.\par \par \uc2 In a post-Roe world, where protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has become a priority for a larger swath of voters, the old strategies don't work quite as well. Katie Paris, the founder of Red, Wine and Blue, a group that organizes suburban women voters for Ohio Democrats, said she saw voters who wouldn't normally have tuned into a summer election on an obscure political process get engaged. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she said, snaps them to attention.\par \par \uc2 ''There's constant evidence of how personal this is,'' she said. ''It's the perfect case study.''\par \par \uc2 Celebrating the defeat of Issue 1, which Republican officials acknowledged was an attempt to make it harder to pass an \uc2 -rights amendment that is scheduled to be on the November ballot. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JAY LAPRETE/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230810ej8a00047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
