\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Harris Is Taking On a Forceful New Role in Biden's 2024 Campaign\b0\par\par\uc2 By Zolan Kanno-Youngs \par \uc2 1,516 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 August 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The vice president is trying to reclaim the momentum that propelled her to \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr\uc2 .'s side as a candidate and into the White House in 2020.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has dashed off to Florida on short notice. She sparred with the state's conservative governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , over how to teach slavery in schools. And she flew into Iowa to defend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights while 13 Republican presidential candidates were having dinner a few miles away.\par \par \uc2 Although her words were directed at Republicans, her message was also aimed at all her doubters.\par \par \uc2 Once a rising star as a senator in California, Ms. Harris has for years been saddled by criticism of her performance as vice president. She has struggled with difficult assignments on issues such as the roots of illegal migration and the narrow path to enduring voting rights protections. Concerns about her future spread as Democrats pondered whether she would be a political liability for the ticket.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris's recent moves are her latest attempt to silence those concerns and reclaim the momentum that propelled her to \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr\uc2 .'s side as a candidate and into the White House in 2020.\par \par \uc2 ''It's good to have her out there,'' said Cedric Richmond, a senior adviser for the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 , who added that the vice president's decision to take on the Republican Party -- assertively and in real time -- was central to the campaign's 2024 strategy.\par \par \uc2 It also keeps President Biden above the fray.\par \par \uc2 ''He is still uniting the West against Russian aggression, and he's tackling the economy and inflation,'' Mr. Richmond said. ''She can go highlight the accomplishments, and she can take on people like DeSantis.''\par \par \uc2 In interviews, aides and advisers acknowledge that Ms. Harris has been affected by the years of criticism. She has often approached events defensively, focusing on not making mistakes, rather than looking for opportunities to attack.\par \par \uc2 But now, galvanized by what she has described as rising extremism in the Republican Party, Ms. Harris is expanding her profile.\par \par \uc2 The tussle with Mr. DeSantis, who is struggling to break through as he campaigns to be the Republican presidential nominee, provides a glimpse into Ms. Harris's role as something of a one-woman rapid-response operation.\par \par \uc2 When Florida last month approved an overhaul to its standards for teaching Black history, which now say middle schoolers should be taught that enslaved people developed skills that could be of personal benefit, Ms. Harris directed her staff to get her down immediately to Jacksonville, a White House official said.\par \par \uc2 She was on the ground within 24 hours, speaking to a packed audience in a historically Black neighborhood, about ''extremist so-called leaders'' who want to sanitize history.\par \par \uc2 ''How is it that anyone could suggest that in the midst of these atrocities that there was any benefit to being subjected to this level of dehumanization?'' Ms. Harris said, drawing a standing ovation from the crowd.\par \par \uc2 Her appearance caught the eye of Mr. DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 ''You clearly have no trouble ducking down to Florida on short notice,'' he said in an open letter last week, accusing her of trying to score political points and inviting her to discuss the new standards.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris, who returned to Florida for her second trip in less than two weeks, had a swift reply.\par \par \uc2 ''Well, I'm here in Florida,'' she said before pausing as the crowd at an African Methodist Episcopal Church event in Orlando erupted in applause. ''And I will tell you, there is no round table, no lecture, no invitation we will accept to debate an undeniable fact: There were no redeeming qualities of slavery.''\par \par \uc2 The vice president's press secretary, Kirsten Allen, said Ms. Harris would ''continue to call out extremist leaders as they attempt to pull our country backward with book bans, revisionist history and barriers that make it harder for Americans to participate in our democracy.''\par \par \uc2 Despite her more public role, Ms. Harris's approval ratings have remained stubbornly low. About 52 percent of Americans have a negative view of her, while 40 percent have a positive view, according to FiveThirtyEight's poll tracker. Mr. Biden has also had trouble with persistently low approval ratings.\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Harris connects to sections of the electorate that are not always a natural fit for Mr. Biden, including women, minority groups and younger voters. At 58, Ms. Harris is decades younger than the 80-year-old president, who would be 86 at the end of a second term.\par \par \uc2 As Ms. Harris fans out across the country, some of her longtime allies said she was showing the kind of swagger they remembered from much earlier in her career, dating back to her days as district attorney of San Francisco and attorney general of California.\par \par \uc2 ''Seeing her in this role, understanding she has a president who she reports to, it's kind of funny to me,'' said Lateefah Simon, who was hired by Ms. Harris in 2005 to lead a new program aimed at keeping first-time drug offenders out of jail.\par \par \uc2 She recalled a confident Ms. Harris walking through the office when she won re-election for district attorney in 2007, reminding each staffer that she would be the boss for another four years. Ms. Simon believes Ms. Harris is making an impact as vice president but wonders how she is adjusting to being second in command.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm like, 'Kamala with a boss?''' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris often draws on her legal background on the campaign trail as a way to emphasize her expertise -- a strategy that serves as a counterweight to Republican claims that she is incompetent.\par \par \uc2 At a recent speech on gun reform, she said she had studied autopsy photographs and had ''seen with my own eyes what a bullet does to the human body.''\par \par \uc2 And in July, when she made a trip to Iowa for a discussion on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she said that she had investigated sex crimes, so she understood that denying a woman an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was an ''immoral'' approach to survivors of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The timing of the trip to Iowa was no accident: As she spoke at \uc2 Drake University\uc2 , saying opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in state legislatures around the country ''don't even know how women's bodies work,'' former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  and a dozen of his rivals for the Republican presidential nomination were in Des Moines for a G.O.P. dinner.\par \par \uc2 Her appearance came just two weeks after the state's Republican governor signed a strict new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban into law, making it illegal to have the procedure past six weeks of pregnancy. (A judge has put the ban on hold.)\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris's decision to go on the offensive is a notable shift.\par \par \uc2 For all of her boundary-breaking as the first woman, the first African American and the first Asian American to serve as vice president, she has long been known for pragmatism and, to her critics, for a defense of the status quo.\par \par \uc2 She has described herself in the past as a ''pragmatic prosecutor'' who owns a gun for personal safety and also believes in criminal justice reform. As vice president, she has had to appeal to broad constituencies; being seen as a moderate is a benefit at a time when conservative critics have tried to portray her as radical and out of step with the nation.\par \par \uc2 But now, with the campaign in full swing, the White House is giving Ms. Harris room to make more assertive moves against Republican opponents.\par \par \uc2 She also has been freed up to travel more, something that has been in the works since the midterm elections when Democrats held off a widely expected red wave.\par \par \uc2 Because the Senate was split evenly for the first two years of the Biden administration, Ms. Harris could never be more than 24 hours away from the Capitol when the Senate was in session in case her tiebreaking vote was needed.\par \par \uc2 With Democrats now holding a 51-to-49 edge, at least in cases when Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 , the Arizona independent, votes with them, Ms. Harris has more flexibility to move. Some are hoping she continues to seize on the opportunity.\par \par \uc2 Stefanie Brown James, a co-founder of the Collective PAC, an organization that helps elect Black officials, has urged Ms. Harris's staff to have her out in front on affirmative action and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues, in particular. She said for the past two and a half years, Ms. Harris was ''a little too much in the background and not seen enough or heard enough.''\par \par \uc2 ''She definitely is having a moment,'' Ms. James said. But she added a note of caution, saying she hoped it would be ''a sustainable moment.''\par \par \uc2 Vice President Kamala Harris has been propelled by what she describes as rising G.O.P. extremism. (PHOTOGRAPH BY AILEEN PERILLA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A9. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230808ej8800038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
