\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Judge Clarifies Exceptions to  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans in Texas\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 936 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The ruling sought to clarify the medical exceptions in the state's bans, and was in a response to a lawsuit from women who were denied abortions despite medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 A Texas judge ruled on Friday that the state must allow doctors to provide abortions to pregnant women whose health or lives are in danger, or whose fetuses have little likelihood of survival.\par \par \uc2 The ruling broadens and clarifies the limited exceptions granted in the state's bans, among the strictest in the country. And it temporarily bars state officials -- until the full case is decided -- from prosecuting doctors who, in their ''good faith judgment and in consultation with the pregnant person,'' determine that an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is medically necessary.\par \par \uc2 The Texas Attorney General's office said late Friday it appealed the ruling, which would immediately put the order on hold, calling it ''an activist Austin judge's attempt to override Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.''\par \par \uc2 Texas law already has exceptions that allow for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some cases of serious health risk or risk of death. But doctors and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates have complained that the law is vague.\par \par \uc2 In her ruling, Judge Jessica Mangrum, elected as a Democrat in 2020, wrote that ''the uncertainty regarding the scope of the medical exception and the related threat of enforcement of Texas' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans has created an imminent risk'' for doctors who ''will have no choice but to bar or delay the provision of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care'' to women who require the procedure to prevent death or serious health risks.\par \par \uc2 The ruling was in response to a lawsuit from 13 women who had been denied abortions despite pregnancy complications that they said caused grave and potentially fatal risks to their health.\par \par \uc2 The Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  filed the case in March on behalf of the 13 women and two OB-GYNs, citing their stories as examples of what the complaint called the ''catastrophic harms'' to women since the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade, which for five decades protected a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Today's ruling should prevent other Texans from suffering the unthinkable trauma our plaintiffs endured,'' said \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , president and chief executive of the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups and providers had filed many other lawsuits since Roe was overturned in June 2022, this was the first suit brought by women denied abortions under state bans.\par \par \uc2 That same month, Gov. Greg Abbott of Texas signed a law seeking to clarify exceptions for some specific medical conditions.\par \par \uc2 During two days of court hearings in July, the lead plaintiff, Amanda Zurawski, described how she went into septic shock and nearly died when her water broke at 18 weeks, after doctors told her they could not abort the fetus because it still had a heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 Another plaintiff, Samantha Casiano, began to cough and gag on the stand as she recalled having to carry and ultimately deliver a fetus that had no skull -- and no chance of survival -- because doctors could not perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The judge called a recess after Ms. Casiano became physically ill.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the state had described the women as publicity seekers, saying that while their stories were ''indisputably tragic,'' the women were on an ''ideological crusade.''\par \par \uc2 In cross-examinations, the state tried to shift blame to doctors and the women themselves, suggesting that Ms. Zurawski should have known her pregnancy was high-risk because she was 35, and that the plaintiffs should have filed malpractice suits or complaints against their health care providers.\par \par \uc2 The state's lawyers repeatedly pressed the women to say that no state official had directly told them they could not have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Unlike other lawsuits from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups, this one had not sought to overturn the state's three separate bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Instead, the suit asked the court to expand and elaborate on the exceptions allowed under the bans.\par \par \uc2 Like most states with bans, Texas allows exceptions when a physician determines that there is risk of ''substantial'' harm to a pregnant woman. But the plaintiffs in the case said that the severe penalties -- including potential for prison sentences of up to 99 years, $100,000 fines and the loss of medical licenses -- had scared doctors into not providing abortions even where the law would seem to allow them.\par \par \uc2 The case asked the court to establish that doctors could perform abortions if, in their good-faith judgment, continuing the pregnancy would be unsafe for the woman's health, or the fetus had a condition ''where the pregnancy is unlikely to result in the birth of a living child with sustained life.''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups argue that even the strictest state bans do not harm women's health, that no law prevents doctors from providing lifesaving care and that the laws prevent only what they and the lawyers for Texas call ''elective'' abortions, or those that are intended to end an unwanted pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 But medical associations, doctors and women seeking abortions say the bans have resulted in chaos because of fears about what is and is not allowed. Data shows that very few women have been granted exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Some states, such as Louisiana, have tried to issue lists of what conditions qualify, but those lists have failed to anticipate some fatal fetal anomalies, including fetuses born without a skull.\par \par \uc2 Amanda Zurawski spoke in March in Austin about nearly losing her life because of Texas' bans. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ILANA PANICH-LINSMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A19. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230806ej8600042\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
