\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Appeals Court Further Limits  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Access on Guam by Requiring an In-Person Doctor Visit\b0\par\par\uc2 By David W. Chen \par \uc2 681 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The News\par \par \uc2 A federal appeals court ruled late Tuesday that women on the American territory of Guam who are seeking medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  must first have an in-person consultation with a doctor, which is likely to make access to the procedure on the remote island even more difficult.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters said that since there were no doctors on Guam who provided abortions, the ruling created a significant obstacle for women seeking the procedure. The only two doctors licensed to provide abortions on the U.S. territory are based in Hawaii, an eight-hour flight away. Until now, those doctors have been prescribing pills over video calls.\par \par \uc2 But in a unanimous ruling, the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit said other doctors on the island could provide in-person consultations, even if those physicians did not want to provide abortions themselves.\par \par \uc2 ''Guam has legitimate interests in requiring an in-person consultation,'' the judges said. ''The consultation can underscore the medical and moral gravity of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and encourage a robust exchange of information.''\par \par \uc2 Why It Matters\par \par \uc2 There are at least 15 states where most abortions are now banned, requiring women who seek to terminate pregnancies to travel elsewhere, sometimes at great cost and risk to their health.\par \par \uc2 But none are as isolated as the territory of Guam -- making the island of 154,000 people a ''litmus test'' of what life would be like under a near-total ban, its attorney general, Douglas Moylan, a Republican who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 Though \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in Guam up to 13 weeks of pregnancy, and later in certain cases, the last doctor who performed abortions left the island in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Central to the case decided on Tuesday before the appellate court, which is based in San Francisco, was whether Guam residents could continue receiving that care via telemedicine, or whether they would have little choice but to leave the island -- something that is prohibitively expensive for most residents.\par \par \uc2 Background\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long been a taboo topic on the culturally conservative island where about 80 percent of the inhabitants are Catholic.\par \par \uc2 In 1990, Guam passed what was then described as among the most draconian \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the country, making it a crime to perform, undergo or seek an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- except in some medical emergencies -- or to encourage women to have abortions. A federal court ruled that the ban was unconstitutional and blocked the territorial government from enforcing it, but the attorney general is fighting to try to revive the ban.\par \par \uc2 With the 1990 law still blocked, Guam's Legislature has since passed additional restrictions. One such measure was the in-person consultation law, which was challenged in January 2021 by a local lawyer, Vanessa L. Williams, in conjunction with \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 , on behalf of the two doctors based in Hawaii. That law was then blocked by a federal judge, allowing the doctors to send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 But with the momentum of the Supreme Court decision last year that overturned the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Guam attorney general's office said the injunction should be lifted. Oral arguments were heard in February in Honolulu before a three-judge panel -- two appointed by President Donald J. Trump and the third by President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What's Next\par \par \uc2 The court's ruling takes effect in 21 days.\par \par \uc2 Alexa Kolbi-Molinas, deputy director of the A.C.L.U. Reproductive Freedom Project, said the plaintiffs were considering their options. But she said that she was ''deeply disappointed'' by the ruling, and that it ''imposes unnecessary obstacles on people seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Guam.''\par \par \uc2 In a WhatsApp message, Mr. Moylan applauded the decision, saying it reflected the island's ''rich cultural values.''\par \par \uc2 ''Over the past 50 years since Roe v. Wade, Guam's People have shown a clear desire to prohibit and restrict abortions,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A11. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230803ej8300036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
