\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Popular AIDS Program Faces Threat as G.O.P. Seeks Limits on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sheryl Gay Stolberg \par \uc2 1,802 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A decades-old program created by President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  to combat AIDS around the world is at risk of being sucked into a partisan dispute over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with some Republicans threatening to block its renewal.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , the Democratic former House speaker, and \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , the Republican former president, do not agree on much. But earlier this year, they joined a high-powered gathering in Washington -- with the Irish rock star Bono on video from Dublin -- to mark the 20th anniversary of America's biggest and, arguably, most successful foreign aid program.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bush created that program, the President's Emergency Program for AIDS Relief, in 2003. In the two decades since, PEPFAR, as it is known, has saved 25 million lives and served as a powerful tool for soft diplomacy, a symbol of America's moral leadership in the world. It has had extraordinary support from a bipartisan coalition of liberals and Christian conservatives.\par \par \uc2 But now PEPFAR is in danger of becoming a victim of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics -- just as the State Department is reorganizing to make the program permanent.\par \par \uc2 The program is set to expire at the end of September. But House Republicans are not moving forward with a bill to reauthorize it for another five years, because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents -- led by a G.O.P. congressman who has long been a supporter of PEPFAR -- are insisting on adding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The stalemate is the latest example of how Republicans are using their majority in the House of Representatives to impose their conservative views on social policy throughout the federal government. They have focused in particular on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a year after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade and, with it, the right to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Earlier this summer, House Republicans loaded up the annual military policy bill that has long been bipartisan with provisions to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and transgender care.\par \par \uc2 The fight over PEPFAR, a $7 billion-a-year program that operates in more than 50 countries, is similar, because it is a broadly bipartisan program that now appears at risk of being sucked into a partisan fight over cultural and social issues.\par \par \uc2 PEPFAR continues to have wide support, including from Representative Michael McCaul of Texas, the Republican chairman of the Foreign Affairs Committee, which oversees the program and approve the reauthorization legislation. But so far, Mr. McCaul has not advanced it because of the objections of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes, including his Republican colleague, Representative Christopher H. Smith of New Jersey, one of the leading anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  voices in Congress who also helped draft the legislation creating PEPFAR.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Smith now says he will not agree to renew the program unless it is subject to the so-called Mexico City policy -- enacted by Republican presidents but lifted by Democrats, including President Biden -- that would bar the program from partnering with any organization that provides \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, no matter the source of the funding.\par \par \uc2 That is a non-starter for Democrats, who are demanding a ''clean'' five-year reauthorization -- one with no added policy restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''We've done clean reauthorizations for 20 years,'' said Representative Barbara Lee, Democratic of California and a chief sponsor of PEPFAR.\par \par \uc2 But there is a substantial stumbling block: Three influential outside groups that oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- the \uc2 Family Research Council\uc2 , \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 's political action arm and \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America -- have sided with Mr. Smith and intend to ''score'' the vote when they compile their annual ratings of members of Congress. A vote for renewing PEPFAR without the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  language would be counted as a demerit, making it politically toxic for most Republicans.\par \par \uc2 The situation has alarmed champions of the program. In an email, Bono called the impasse ''madness,'' and called on Congress to ''protect the bipartisan commitment to keeping politics out of PEPFAR.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. McCaul said he is ''talking to supporters both inside and outside the government, and working with my colleagues on both sides of the aisle in the House and the Senate'' to resolve the dispute. He has also been texting with Bono, who in turn has been in touch with congressional leaders on the matter.\par \par \uc2 A senior White House official, speaking on the condition of anonymity to describe the negotiations, said on Thursday that the White House was ''engaging closely with Congress at senior levels'' in pursuit of a straight five-year reauthorization.\par \par \uc2 The program is an important legacy for Mr. Bush and other Republicans of his era, including \uc2 Bill Frist\uc2 , the former Senate majority leader, and \uc2 Rick Santorum\uc2 , a former senator from Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 ''I look back on the things I did as a member of Congress, and feel like I was able, as the pro-life warrior in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , to forge a compromise to get conservatives to support this,'' Mr. Santorum said in an interview. ''It's been a great thing for our country, and it's been a great thing for humanity.''\par \par \uc2 Last week, Mr. Santorum publicly pleaded for a ''five-year, clean extension'' in an opinion essay in the conservative outlet \uc2 Newsmax\uc2 . He said he intended to use Congress's upcoming August recess to try to forge a compromise.\par \par \uc2 But in recent months, Mr. Smith, the New Jersey Republican, and right-wing groups have begun accusing the Biden administration of injecting progressive politics into the program.\par \par \uc2 In late May, a Heritage Foundation scholar published an essay in The Hill arguing that PEPFAR had become ''increasingly politicized'' and needed an overhaul. Mr. Smith followed in early June with ''Dear Colleague'' letter asserting that Mr. Biden had ''hijacked PEPFAR.''\par \par \uc2 In an interview, he pointed to new language in a PEPFAR country and regional operational plan calling for the program to partner with organizations that advocate for ''institutional reforms in law and policy regarding sexual, reproductive and economic rights of women.'' He argued that was code for a plan to ''integrate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with H.I.V./AIDS work.''\par \par \uc2 The document also says PEPFAR programs should ''advance human rights and decriminalization for lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender, queer, and intersex (L.G.B.T.Q.I.+) communities.'' That did not sit well with the \uc2 Family Research Council\uc2 , whose chief lobbyist, \uc2 Travis Weber\uc2 , recently called PEPFAR ''a massive slush fund for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and L.G.B.T. advocacy.'' In an interview, he said he stood by those words.\par \par \uc2 The senior White House official said that the ''intentional focus on health equity is new'' and that it came in response to requests from local communities for PEPFAR to ''address barriers to health for children, adolescent girls and young women, and key populations.'' The mention of women's rights refers to ensuring that adolescent girls have access to schools, the official said.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has added a footnote to the document clarifying that PEPFAR does not fund abortions, an assertion that Mr. Smith called ''meaningless.''\par \par \uc2 There is no evidence that PEPFAR or its foreign partners have used federal tax dollars to promote or perform abortions; U.S. law does not allow it. But some PEPFAR grantees, such as \uc2 Population Services International\uc2 , a global health nonprofit based in Washington, do provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related services with money from other sources.\par \par \uc2 ''The assertion is made by critics is that organizations like P.S.I. are working with U.S. money and involved in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- and shouldn't that be illegal?'' said Karl Hofmann, the group's president and chief executive. ''I guess my answer is, those two facts are true, and it's not illegal.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lee, the California Democrat, said she is working to ''clarify this misrepresentation'' being made by Mr. Smith.\par \par \uc2 But the prospects for a five-year extension seem bleak.\par \par \uc2 ''We are in a very precarious place,'' said Shepherd Smith, an evangelical Christian and a co-founder of Childrens AIDS Fund International, a nonprofit. He has organized other faith-based groups to issue a letter in support of reauthorization.\par \par \uc2 Christopher H. Smith, the New Jersey congressman, has proposed an alternative: He persuaded fellow Republicans to insert language into a State Department spending bill that would allow PEPFAR to keep operating for a year without a new authorization, but subject to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. That measure, however, like many of the spending bills making their way through the Republican-led House, is unlikely to be acceptable to the Democratic-controlled Senate.\par \par \uc2 And the symbolism of such a move would be devastating, PEPFAR's backers say -- a signal to other nations that the United States is abandoning its bipartisan commitment to end the AIDS epidemic by 2030, and that Washington is truly broken. It would also be a setback for global health; PEPFAR has created an infrastructure of clinics in poor nations that provide other services, including Covid-19 testing and vaccines.\par \par \uc2 ''This is not an issue that at all involves \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- it's about having health care facilities in countries to deal with pandemic type of challenges,'' said Senator \uc2 Benjamin L. Cardin\uc2 , Democrat of Maryland. He called PEPFAR's creation ''one of the great moments in American foreign policy.''\par \par \uc2 As the debate plays out on Capitol Hill, the State Department official who oversees PEPFAR, Dr. John N. Nkengasong, is about to get a big promotion. On Tuesday, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony J. Blinken\uc2  is expected to announce the establishment of a new Bureau of Global Health Security and Diplomacy, to be led by Dr. Nkengasong. The idea is to integrate PEPFAR into the department's broader global health work and to transition it out of emergency status.\par \par \uc2 Since its inception, the program has invested more than $100 billion in fighting the global AIDS crisis. Independent analyses by K.F.F., formerly the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 , have found that PEPFAR has helped improve maternal and child health and is ''associated with large, significant declines in mortality'' in countries where it has operated.\par \par \uc2 Backers of the program are hoping that Mr. Bush will weigh in. Earlier this year, Emily Bass, an author and activist, sent the former president a signed copy of her book, ''To End a Plague: America's Fight to Defeat AIDS in Africa,'' which chronicles the PEPFAR story.\par \par \uc2 A few weeks ago, Ms. Bass said, Mr. Bush sent a letter thanking her.\par \par \uc2 ''Laura and I will remain invested in this mission for the rest of our lives,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 was established by George W. Bush, top, in 2003. Representative Christopher H. Smith, long a supporter, is now objecting to a five-year reauthorization. Dr. John Nkengasong is a key part of State Department plans to make the program permanent. POOL (PHOTOGRAPHS BY DOUG MILLS/THE NEW YORK TIMES; DANIEL IRUNGU/EPA, VIA SHUTTERSTOCK PEPFAR; KEN CEDENO) This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230801ej7t0000d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
