\par\par \uc2 IN THE MOMENT\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Asked About  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Haley Talks Instead of Consensus\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jazmine Ulloa \par \uc2 954 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The former South Carolina governor -- who is trying to break out in a packed 2024 Republican presidential primary -- gave a nuanced response on one of the thorniest issues facing Republicans.\par \par \uc2 At town halls and political events in New Hampshire, where she has made far more campaign stops than her rivals, Nikki Haley has mostly sidestepped any discussion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a fraught issue for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 As the G.O.P. activist base tries to pull state lawmakers further to the right in curbing access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , moderates worry that the hard-line stance has already handed electoral wins to Democrats and could have dire consequences in 2024. Ms. Haley, a former governor of South Carolina and United Nations ambassador, has tried to pull off a difficult balancing act on the issue, and her attempts haven't always resonated -- partly because, her critics say, she has avoided discussing details.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, with more than 100 people gathered in a drizzle for a town hall at a picturesque vineyard in Hollis, Christina Zlotnick, 55, an undeclared voter from Amherst, posed a hard question. Ms. Haley provided little by way of concrete policy proposals, but this time she drew an enthusiastic response.\par \par \uc2 A Question for Nikki Haley\par \par \uc2 ''You said on TV that women who get abortions should not be put in jail and should not be subject to the death penalty. But how exactly should women who get illegal abortions -- women like me -- how do you specifically think we should be punished?''\par \par \uc2 The Subtext\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, Republican-dominated state legislatures have rushed to institute new laws outlawing or imposing stringent restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even as the issue has fueled Democratic victories across the nation, with Americans supporting at least some access to the procedure at record levels. Most abortions are now banned in 14 states. Some state laws stipulate narrow but vague exceptions, and carry years of prison time. Last week, a teenager in Nebraska who used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to end her pregnancy, and who pleaded guilty to illegally concealing human remains, was sentenced to 90 days in jail. At the time, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was banned in Nebraska after 20 weeks.\par \par \uc2 In the past, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have largely maintained that they don't support punishing women who seek out the procedure illegally. But more recently, some state lawmakers have proposed legislation with criminal consequences for patients as well as those who may help them.\par \par \uc2 Haley's Answer\par \par \uc2 ''In order for us to have a federal law, we're going to have to have consensus. What does that consensus look like? Can't we all agree that we don't want late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Can't we all agree that we want to encourage more adoptions and good-quality adoption so that children feel more love, not less? Can't we all agree that doctors and nurses who don't believe in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shouldn't have to perform them? Can't we all agree that contraception should be accessible? And can't we all agree that a woman who gets an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not be subject to the death penalty or get arrested? That's where I think we start -- we start, and we do it with a level of respect. No more demonizing this issue. We're going to humanize this issue. I had a roommate who was raped in college. I wouldn't wish on anyone what she went through, wondering if she was OK. Everybody has a story. Let's be respectful of everybody's story, and let's figure out what we can do together instead of sitting there and tearing each other apart.''\par \par \uc2 The Subtext\par \par \uc2 Ms. Haley began her reply by flatly stating that she did not believe women needed to be punished, adding that she was ''unapologetically pro-life,'' but did not ''judge anyone for being pro-choice.'' She went on to explain her support for the Supreme Court decision that thrust the issue back to the states but said she did see a role for the federal government in setting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, as long as there was congressional consensus between Republicans and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Her answer mostly echoed her previous remarks on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but delivered with passion, it seemed to pack a more powerful punch in front of a smaller, friendlier audience. Attendees nodded along or expressed whoops and hollers in agreement, breaking into applause as she ended.\par \par \uc2 Many Republican and independent women at her events this week have said they see Ms. Haley as a strong leader and messenger on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the only Republican woman in the presidential race, and believe she might be able to blunt attacks from Democrats on the issue. Ms. Haley's positions on the topic tend to be more moderate than the rest of the field, and it is among the issues she has used to set up her bid as a test of the Republican Party's attitudes about female leaders, without leaning too far into her gender.\par \par \uc2 After the event, Ms. Zlotnick, who once had to take a drug to end an unviable pregnancy -- a procedure that is not illegal -- said she phrased her question as a hypothetical because if she found herself in different circumstances, she would consider having an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She said she was a strong believer in \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  but had been satisfied with Ms. Haley's response.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley, the former governor of South Carolina, recently gave a nuanced answer on one of the thorniest town-hall questions facing Republicans. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JOHN TULLY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230727ej7r0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
