\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Ohio Voters Will Have Say On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Amendment, But There May Be Hurdles\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,045 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Backers of a proposal to establish a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Ohio Constitution submitted enough valid signatures to put the question on the November ballot.\par \par \uc2 Ohio voters will decide in November whether to amend their state Constitution to establish a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after state officials said on Tuesday that proponents had submitted over 495,000 valid signatures from voters, more than enough to put the question on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of the measure still face another hurdle. Republicans in the state legislature want to make the amendment harder to approve, and have put up a ballot question of their own that would raise the threshold of voters required to amend the state constitution to 60 percent instead of a simple majority. That question is on the ballot in a special election on Aug. 8; early voting on that amendment is already in progress.\par \par \uc2 In a statement Tuesday, Lauren Blauvelt and Lauren Beene of Ohioans United for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the coalition behind the ballot measure, hailed the announcement.\par \par \uc2 ''Every person deserves respect, dignity, and the right to make \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care decisions, including those related to their own pregnancy, miscarriage care, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  free from government interference,'' they said in the statement. ''Now that the petition drive is complete, we're eager to continue the campaign to enshrine those rights in Ohio's Constitution and ensure that Ohioans will never again be subject to draconian \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care policies imposed by extremists.'' Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups portray the amendment as ''extreme,'' and have argued that it would permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''until birth'' and also enshrine a right for minors to undergo gender-transition surgery without their parents' consent, even though the amendment allows the state to prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in pregnancy and does not include any language about gender transition.\par \par \uc2 They urged voters to make the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment harder to pass by participating in the special election in August. ''Knowing the serious danger of this amendment and that these out-of-state special interest groups still have millions to spend to get it passed, it is even more imperative that every pro-life Ohioan votes yes on Issue 1 this August to ensure that our Constitution, our preborn, and our families are protected,'' Peter Range, the chief executive of Ohio Right to Life, said in a statement.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates have put increasing hopes on state ballot measures in the year since the Supreme Court reversed of Roe v. Wade, which for five decades established a national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They are encouraged by public opinion polls showing that most Americans -- and a majority in all but a few states -- think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in at least some cases. A USA Today Network/\uc2 Suffolk University\uc2  poll in early July found that 58 percent of likely voters in Ohio backed the amendment establishing a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with 32 percent opposed.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights prevailed in six out of six measures on ballot measures that were put to voters last year, including those in conservative states like Kentucky and Kansas. But none of those measures received more than 60 percent of the vote. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers and groups are trying to stay one step ahead by trying to making measures harder to place on the ballot or to pass.\par \par \uc2 Ohio has become a test case.\par \par \uc2 The state's Republican-led legislature passed a law in 2019 that would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after roughly six weeks of pregnancy -- before many women know they are pregnant -- with exceptions to save the life of the mother or to prevent ''major impairment'' of a bodily function, but not for rape or incest. That law took effect after Roe v. Wade was overturned last year, but a county court judge put a hold on it, leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal in Ohio until 22 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The ballot measure on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would amend the Ohio Constitution to add a right much like the one that was protected by Roe. It would establish a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but allow the state to prohibit the procedure after the fetus would be viable outside the womb, generally around 23 or 24 weeks. It would also allow limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before viability, as long as those laws used the ''least restrictive means to advance the individual's health'' according to ''widely accepted and evidence-based standards of care.''\par \par \uc2 The question on the Aug. 8 special-election ballot would raise the threshold of yes votes necessary to amend the constitution to 60 percent. Proponents of future amendments would have to collect signatures from at least 5 percent of the voters in all 88 of the state's counties; under current law, they only need 5 percent of voters in half the counties. And the measure would do away with a 10-day ''curing'' period that allows proponents to collect additional signatures to make up for any that authorities strike as invalid.\par \par \uc2 The Republican-led legislature passed a law earlier this year banning most August elections, saying they were needlessly expensive and had such low turnout that they were anti-democratic. Mr. LaRose, the secretary of state, himself had testified that they allowed ''just a handful of voters'' to make weighty decisions.\par \par \uc2 But the legislature approved a special election in August this year for the sole purpose of considering a constitutional amendment that would make it more difficult to propose and pass other amendments. A simple majority is required to enact it.\par \par \uc2 Though Republicans have said the move was aimed directly at blocking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the language on the special-election ballot does not include the word ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' That may make it difficult for supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to rally their voters, especially in the dog days of summer.\par \par \uc2 Backers of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights amendment submitted a total of roughly 710,000 signatures collected from all 88 counties. Under current state law, they needed 413,466 valid signatures for the measure to qualify for the November ballot.\par \par \uc2 A signature drive in Cincinnati. The effort had enough support to get the question on the ballot. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MADDIE MCGARVEY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230726ej7q0003h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
