\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A Year of Navigating A 50-State Patchwork Of Laws on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Kelly and Marisa Schwartz Taylor \par \uc2 2,344 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A Supreme Court decision created a 50-state patchwork of differing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. An eclectic network of volunteers and organizations has tried to fill the gaps in health care and support.\par \par \uc2 For organizations that work with pregnant women, it has been a year of seismic change.\par \par \uc2 With access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  becoming more restricted, aid networks that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have rushed to adapt, seeking new ways to help women travel across state lines and distributing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups are stepping up efforts to support women preparing for childbirth by providing formula, diapers and counseling.\par \par \uc2 The year since the Supreme Court rescinded a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by reversing the landmark Roe v. Wade decision has been a time of fear and retrenchment for groups that provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services and support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. It has been a period of elation and opportunity for those who oppose them. And it has produced widespread confusion as organizations across the ideological spectrum scramble to keep up with legal, political and social fallout from the court decision.\par \par \uc2 The first few months after the decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health ''just felt like the Wild, Wild West,'' said Maren Hurley, who works as an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doula in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hurley's state, which provided \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access up to 20 weeks of pregnancy, saw an influx of patients from states with tighter restrictions for much of that first year. But as of July 1, access was rolled back to 12 weeks in most cases as part of a new state law.\par \par \uc2 Groups that provided funding for abortions in Alabama and Louisiana, where the procedure is mostly illegal now, have had to change their approach. Providers of telehealth services and medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have felt obligated to restrict their client base to roughly half the states in America or fewer at any given time.\par \par \uc2 Hey Jane, which prescribes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications that can be delivered to homes, has grown busier. Indigenous Women Rising, which provides \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services to Native women across the country, has doubled its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  budget to help women travel to states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains accessible. Private pilots established Elevated Access, which provides free flights to out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers.\par \par \uc2 Some groups that were once focused mostly on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access are now providing more prenatal and birth support, including defraying medical expenses and some costs of raising children.\par \par \uc2 ''We don't want people terminating pregnancies they want simply because they can't afford it,'' said Jenice Fountain, the executive director of the Yellowhammer Fund in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 On May 9, volunteer pilots flew a pregnant Minnesota woman more than 1,000 miles to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Erica, a 30-something mother of four, puffed a cigarette on a clear afternoon at a suburban Midwest airfield where she was waiting to board a small plane for the second leg of her trip. The flight from the Twin Cities area that morning had been her first plane ride. Though it had gone smoothly, she was anxious about the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself, she said during an interview conducted on the condition that she be identified only by her first name.\par \par \uc2 She had a drug problem, she explained, and was unable to have a healthy pregnancy. She fought tears as she recalled waiting a month for an appointment with \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , only to learn there that it was too late to receive her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Minnesota. The waiting list in nearby Illinois was also a month long.\par \par \uc2 Although the two states protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the wait lists put Erica's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in potential jeopardy under the states' laws at the time. A patient coordinator had arranged for Erica's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take place in Maryland -- and had found the pilots who could fly her there for free.\par \par \uc2 ''I think this is the best thing to do for sure,'' Erica said, ''and I'm glad that I'm able to get it done.'' She fiddled with a sunflower ring her best friend had given her for good luck.\par \par \uc2 Erica's pilots that day were volunteers with Elevated Access, a nonprofit set up last year to help people obtain abortions, often across state lines.\par \par \uc2 The pilot who had flown Erica to the layover spot, who spoke on the condition that he be identified only as Andy, watched her climb aboard a second eastbound plane with two Elevated Access pilots from the Washington, D.C., area. He then returned to his white Cessna 182.\par \par \uc2 Andy, a 51-year-old tech executive and self-described liberal, generally avoids talking politics. But the state limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said, have made him want to help women like Erica.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't like how some of these people have to suffer for this political game,'' he said as the plane cruised 8,000 feet above southern Wisconsin. ''It's tragic.''\par \par \uc2 After assisting people who sought abortions for years, the Yellowhammer Fund in Alabama has broadened its mission.\par \par \uc2 Until the Dobbs decision, Yellowhammer, a nonprofit based in Birmingham, Ala., was funding 100 to 200 abortions per week with an annual budget that had reached $150,000, said the organization's deputy director, Kelsea McLain. After Roe was overturned, incoming calls about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access dwindled to three to five per week.\par \par \uc2 Because Alabama placed strict limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- and because of the possibility that people who assist \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients could face legal consequences -- Yellowhammer staff members try to avoid speaking directly to callers about how and where to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even out of state. Instead, said Ms. McLain, they send out emails with links to information from third parties.\par \par \uc2 Since the Dobbs decision, Yellowhammer has refocused on sex education and contraception, which had previously been one piece of the operation. The fund also recently trained two dozen people based throughout the Southeast to share information with people in their communities about self-managed medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and supplies like condoms and emergency contractive medication, also known as the ''morning-after pill.''\par \par \uc2 On a recent Friday evening, Ms. Fountain, the Yellowhammer executive director, held a ''packing party'' at her Birmingham home to prepare supplies for mailing out as well as for a six-week bus tour the fund had planned through Alabama. As her team stuffed emergency contraception tablets, pregnancy tests and personal lubricant into colorful bags, she reflected on their work.\par \par \uc2 Reproductive justice groups ''need to transition to full-spectrum services,'' Ms. Fountain said -- including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  support where possible, but also pregnancy support when needed, including baby supplies or clinic referrals.\par \par \uc2 Later, she added: ''We need to support people who are going to birth, whether it's by force or whether it's by choice,'' given the limited options available in the wake of the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , church-backed pregnancy center called Mountain Area Pregnancy Services confronted a harassment incident.\par \par \uc2 Overnight on June 7, 2022 -- after a version of the Supreme Court's Dobbs ruling was leaked but before it was finalized -- the center's front walk in Asheville, N.C., was painted with the message: ''If abortions aren't safe, neither are you!'' An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group called Jane's Revenge took credit.\par \par \uc2 Employees at the center were disturbed, said Jeff Porter, the assistant director. But he said they were not deterred from providing alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through counseling, parenting classes and material support for local women -- most of whom are unmarried and without obstetric care. Two-thirds of them live on an average of $29,000 or less per year.\par \par \uc2 ''We called it our Genesis 50:20 moment,'' said Mr. Porter, quoting that scripture as '''What you intended for evil, God used for good.' And so that became our story.''\par \par \uc2 Michelle Fenton, a 35-year-old event coordinator, discovered the center's satellite office in Waynesville, N.C., earlier this year. As someone who had struggled with fertility issues, she was thrilled to be pregnant. But when an early ultrasound indicated the possibility of Down syndrome, she began contemplating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She called the center for emotional support.\par \par \uc2 ''I told them I didn't have a good experience, is there any way I can get in there soon to have an ultrasound, just to make me feel better about the situation?'' Ms. Fenton recalled through tears. Within three days, she received an ultrasound at the center, where women on staff prayed for her and her baby. ''They made me feel better about everything,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 A few weeks later, blood test results indicated that the risk of Down syndrome was extremely low.\par \par \uc2 Though Ms. Fenton, who is expecting a boy in September, continues receiving prenatal care at a local medical practice, she has taken free parenting classes at Mountain Area Pregnancy Services to learn more about pregnancy and infant care, earning credits along the way. Those credits are exchangeable for supplies like a portable crib, bottles and a nursing pillow.\par \par \uc2 The center offers free supplies to women undergoing other types of counseling, too. Among them: a stuffed animal containing an audio recording of the fetus's heartbeat for pregnancies likely to end in a very brief life or stillbirth.\par \par \uc2 Serving the needs of Indigenous women has become especially complex.\par \par \uc2 Indigenous Women Rising helps Native women in states with tribal populations. Based in New Mexico, which allows expansive access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it provides funding for medical care and logistical support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers as well as funding for midwifery services for clients who give birth.\par \par \uc2 Since the Dobbs decision, the group has seen an influx of requests from women in Oklahoma, Texas, North Dakota and South Dakota, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  suddenly moved out of reach.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has always been limited for tribal communities, where clinics and medical centers that receive federal funding are barred from providing the procedure, forcing women to travel.\par \par \uc2 Before Dobbs, the group's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services operated on a budget of $20,000 per month. Since the court decision, the rapidly growing costs of transporting women further and further from home to reach somewhere allowing abortions has forced the group to double that budget.\par \par \uc2 Indigenous Women Rising had typically financed abortions for 30 to 40 people per month. Now it can fund only about 30 abortions per month, even with a budget twice as large.\par \par \uc2 ''Costs have gone up so much, and people are just starting to travel longer distances as well, and so that's more on a lot of us,'' said Jonnette Paddy, the group's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund director.\par \par \uc2 Founded in 2018, it caters to particular needs of Native communities. Fund organizers match clients with tribal healers and help arrange for traditional ceremonies to handle fetal remains. They have encouraged clinics to provide smudge spray, an herbal fragrance used by some Native midwives to aid in the birthing process that can also provide comfort to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients.\par \par \uc2 ''We can't just approach \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , health, freedom, sovereignty for Native people in the same way that non-Native communities operate,'' said Rachael Lorenzo, the group's executive director.\par \par \uc2 The online pharmacy Honeybee Health has become the country's biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication dispenser.\par \par \uc2 Tucked away on a side street in Culver City, Calif., Honeybee's unmarked headquarters belies its central role in the post-Roe United States.\par \par \uc2 During the pandemic, when Honeybee began shipping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication packages directly to patient homes, it was a small and experimental effort for the company, which primarily handled prescriptions for allergy pills, antidepressants and other common drugs. But the dearth of pharmacies willing to offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication meant that Honeybee soon became the main provider of the online-ordered, home-delivered pills. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication -- which now accounts for more than half of abortions in the United States -- produces roughly 40 percent of Honeybee's revenue.\par \par \uc2 Honeybee ships to 24 states where medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legally permitted, said Dr. Jessica Nouhavandi, the Los Angeles native who co-founded the company and runs it. She relies on a team of lawyers to stay abreast of state-by-state developments.\par \par \uc2 This spring, while awaiting the outcome of a federal lawsuit over access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Dr. Nouhavandi began stockpiling the drugs.\par \par \uc2 When the case moved to an appeals court, Honeybee filed a court brief arguing that suspending access to the drugs would inflict ''immense harm'' on the public.\par \par \uc2 As long as the federal government deems the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication to be safe and legal, she expects Honeybee to continue with the practice.\par \par \uc2 ''We came this far,'' she said. ''We cannot go backward now.''\par \par \uc2 Erica, far left, a 30-something mother of four, getting ready for the second leg of her trip. The flight that morning had been her first plane ride. Though it had gone smoothly, she was anxious about the \uc2  itself. She had a drug problem, she said, and was unable to have a healthy pregnancy. Andy, the pilot, above, avoids talking politics. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JULIA RENDLEMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Dr. Jessica Nouhavandi cofounded Honeybee Health, the main provider of online-ordered, home-delivered \uc2  pills. The medication accounts for about 40 percent of the company's revenue. (PHOTOGRAPH BY TRACY NGUYEN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Jennifer Lim of Indigenous Women Rising in Albuquerque. The group helps Native women find access to abortions and midwifery services. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SHARON CHISCHILLY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A12); Michelle Fenton, 35, was worried about her baby and asked Mountain Area Pregnancy Services in Waynesville, N.C., for help getting an ultrasound this month. She is expecting a boy in September. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MIKE BELLEME FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); he Yellowhammer Fund is doubling down on sex education and contraception. Jenice Fountain, above, the Alabama group's executive director, held a ''packing party,'' where her team stuffed bags with emergency-contraception tablets and pregnancy tests ahead of a six-week bus tour. (PHOTOGRAPH BY GABRIELA BHASKAR FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A13) This article appeared in print on page A12, A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230724ej7o00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
