\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Iowa Judge Temporarily Suspends Recent  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Legislation\b0\par\par\uc2 By Colbi Edmonds \par \uc2 605 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Judge Joseph Seidlin put a hold on the law, which prohibits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy, days after the governor signed it.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Iowa's Republican governor signed a strict new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban into law. And for three days, most abortions in Iowa were illegal past six weeks of pregnancy. Until Monday afternoon, when a district judge put the ban on hold.\par \par \uc2 Joseph Seidlin, a district court judge in Polk County, said that the new ban would be suspended while the larger legal case against it moved forward. He said in his ruling that the plaintiffs who filed a lawsuit against the ban, including \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 , \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, were likely to succeed on the merits of their case.\par \par \uc2 That means that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Iowa is once again legal up to around 22 weeks of pregnancy, at least for now.\par \par \uc2 ''We are deeply relieved that the court granted this relief so essential health care in Iowa can continue,'' said Dr. Abbey Hardy-Fairbanks, medical director of the Emma Goldman Clinic, one of the plaintiffs. ''We are also acutely aware that the relief is only pending further litigation and the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Iowa remains tenuous and threatened.''\par \par \uc2 The legal whiplash is the latest turn in the conservative state's yearslong struggle to restrict the procedure. At least a dozen Republican states have banned or severely restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  ruled last year that it was not protected in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 in 2018, Iowa legislators passed a six-week ban that was challenged in court, and could not be enforced when the State Supreme Court deadlocked over it in June. The governor, \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 , called a special session to pass another ban, which the Legislature did this month.\par \par \uc2 ''The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry's attempt to thwart the will of Iowans and the voices of their elected representatives continues today, but I will fight this all the way to the Iowa Supreme Court where we expect a decision that will finally provide justice for the unborn,'' Governor Reynolds said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 In his ruling, Judge Seidlin said that he chose to preserve the status quo set by the Iowa Supreme Court when it deadlocked on the previous ban, and called the new bill ''virtually identical'' to the one passed in 2018.\par \par \uc2 ''There are good, honorable and intelligent people -- morally, politically and legally -- on both sides of this upsetting societal and constitutional dilemma,'' he wrote in his ruling.\par \par \uc2 Iowa Democrats said the decision, though not the final word on the case, was a win.\par \par \uc2 ''Iowans support and deserve their fundamental right to make health care decisions about their own bodies,'' said Representative Jennifer Konfrst, the Democratic minority leader of the House.\par \par \uc2 The measure passed last week by Republicans allows for abortions until there is a ''detectable fetal heartbeat'' -- a term that medical groups dispute because the embryo does not have a fully formed heart at that stage. Rather, there is cardiac cellular activity, which generally occurs at roughly six weeks into pregnancy, before many women even realize they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Iowa providers and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates filed suit within hours of the Legislature's passage, saying it was unconstitutional under the standard of ''undue burden.''\par \par \uc2 The legislation includes exceptions after that point in cases of rape or incest, when the woman's life is in serious danger or she faces a risk of certain permanent injuries, or when fetal abnormalities ''incompatible with life'' are present.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230718ej7i0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
