\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Iowa Lawmakers Send Restrictive, 6-Week  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bill to the Governor\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mitch Smith \par \uc2 1,495 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  called the Legislature for a special session to consider sweeping new restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Less than a month after a deadlocked Iowa Supreme Court left a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban unenforceable, lawmakers returned to the State Capitol on Tuesday and passed a nearly identical set of restrictions on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 , a Republican who said, ''I believe the pro-life movement is the most important human rights cause of our time'' when she called the special session on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , plans to sign the bill into law on Friday, her office said.\par \par \uc2 ''The voices of Iowans and their democratically elected representatives cannot be ignored any longer, and justice for the unborn should not be delayed,'' Governor Reynolds said in a statement on Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 The session further cemented Iowa's sharp political shift to the right, and was set to end its increasingly rare status as a Republican-led state where abortions are allowed up to 20 weeks post-fertilization, or roughly 22 weeks into pregnancy. The new limits would add Iowa to a list of conservative states including Indiana, North Dakota and South Carolina that have passed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  ended the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 But courts in some of those states have temporarily blocked the laws from taking effect, pending the outcome of lawsuits from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who say the laws violate various provisions in state constitutions. And Iowa providers and advocates for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights filed suit within hours of the Iowa vote on Wednesday, saying it was unconstitutional under the standard of ''undue burden.''\par \par \uc2 As the legislature began its special session on Tuesday, Iowans on both sides of the debate gathered at the Capitol in Des Moines, holding signs with messages like, ''My Body, My Choice'' and wearing T-shirts with slogans like, ''Unborn Lives Matter.'' Every seat was claimed inside a public hearing before a House committee, with scores of others standing in the hallways and chanting, ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have got to go.''\par \par \uc2 The call for a special session infuriated but did not surprise Iowa Democrats, who celebrated the court's deadlock a few weeks ago but knew that Republicans, who control both chambers of the Legislature, were likely to try again.\par \par \uc2 The Iowa Supreme Court's deadlock left in place a lower court's injunction that blocked enforcement of a six-week ban, but it also left unsettled the broader question of whether such restrictions were permissible under the state's Constitution. Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights said the new limits passed by lawmakers endangered women's health and ran counter to public opinion.\par \par \uc2 ''They have overplayed their hand,'' Senator Pam Jochum, the leader of the Democratic minority, said in an interview at the Capitol on Tuesday. But while members of her caucus fought the measure, Ms. Jochum said the large Republican margins limited their options.\par \par \uc2 The bill passed by Republicans allows for abortions until the point where there is what the bill calls ''detectable fetal heartbeat,'' a term that medical groups dispute.\par \par \uc2 The legislation assumes this is roughly six weeks of pregnancy, before many women know they are pregnant. The legislation includes exceptions after that point for rape or incest, when the woman's life is in serious danger or she faces a risk of certain permanent injuries, or when fetal abnormalities ''incompatible with life'' are present.\par \par \uc2 In public testimony on Tuesday, supporters and opponents of the legislation each told wrenching personal stories and described how religious beliefs shaped their views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Rev. Katie Styrt, a Presbyterian pastor from Davenport, described seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  several years ago when she was pregnant with twins, one of whom was found to have severe complications and would not be able to survive. She said she had hoped to save the other twin by getting the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We had the health care and the doctors we needed to get us where we needed to be,'' Ms. Styrt said. ''Now, if this bill passes, we won't.''\par \par \uc2 Others said passing the restrictions was a moral imperative.\par \par \uc2 ''I understand both sides: I was a woman who exercised choice to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; this is why I advocate to prevent other women experiencing what I have gone through,'' Kristi Judkins, the executive director of Iowa Right to Life, told legislators. ''People have labeled me a hypocrite, but I ask you: Who here would tell someone to make the same negative, permanent, life-changing mistake?''\par \par \uc2 Sweeping restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Iowa would further erode access to the procedure in the Midwest, where it is already limited in many states. But a new law would almost certainly face a fresh legal challenge, and the outcome in the courts would again be uncertain.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned in almost all cases in the bordering states of Missouri, South Dakota and Wisconsin, and a new 12-week ban recently passed in Nebraska. Illinois and Minnesota, which are led by Democrats, have permissive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws and could become destinations for Iowa women seeking abortions. More than 3,700 abortions were performed in Iowa in 2021, according to state data, most of them by medication.\par \par \uc2 The new limits passed on Tuesday after hours of intense debate that extended well into the evening.\par \par \uc2 ''If this bill becomes law, women will die in Iowa,'' State Representative Beth Wessel-Kroeschell, a Democrat, told her colleagues. ''Common sense has walked out the door.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans defended the bill, and voted down several amendments from Democrats that would have expanded exceptions.\par \par \uc2 ''Unborn people, in my opinion, are babies, and government's job is to protect the rights of every person, including babies,'' State Representative \uc2 Brad Sherman\uc2 , a Republican, said on the House floor.\par \par \uc2 A Des Moines Register-Mediacom Iowa poll from this year found that 61 percent of adults in the state believed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in most or all cases, while 35 percent believed it should be illegal in most or all cases.\par \par \uc2 But last year, when Democrats nationally ran on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, retaking state legislative chambers and holding governorships, the party floundered in Iowa, which not so long ago was viewed as a state where voters might swing to either party. Governor Reynolds won re-election in a landslide, Republicans swept the state's congressional seats and voters unseated the attorney general and treasurer, both Democrats who had held office for decades.\par \par \uc2 Though Iowans voted twice for President Barack Obama, and Democrats held a majority in the State Senate as recently as 2016, the state is now solidly Republican. Only one Democrat, Rob Sand, the state auditor, still holds statewide office, and the national Democratic Party has moved to push Iowa's coveted first-in-the-nation caucuses later in the nominating calendar.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, for their part, have wasted no time remaking Iowa in a more conservative image. Ms. Reynolds signed laws this year that banned hormone therapy for transgender children, loosened child labor rules and limited Mr. Sand's powers. And with Republicans keeping Iowa at the start of their nominating calendar, the party's presidential hopefuls have been flooding the state.\par \par \uc2 State Representative Jennifer Konfrst, the leader of the Democratic minority in the Iowa House, said the state was not as conservative as recent election results suggested. Although Democrats are not likely to retake a legislative chamber next year, Ms. Konfrst said they saw an opportunity to expand their statehouse numbers in 2024 and regain a foothold in the congressional delegation. New \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits, she said, would have the potential to mobilize Democratic voters who sat out the last election.\par \par \uc2 ''Our best case is going to be to hold Republicans accountable for going against what Iowans want,'' said Ms. Konfrst, who represents parts of suburban Des Moines. ''The fact that they're hurrying it through in July, a year before an election, shows that politically they know this is unpopular.''\par \par \uc2 But Iowa Republicans have made no efforts to hide their support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, and they have kept winning elections anyway. ''Iowans have elected us on the promise to defend the unborn, and we will continue to follow through on that promise,'' State Representative Matt Windschitl, the majority leader in the House, said.\par \par \uc2 The same poll that showed broad support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights this year also showed that more Iowans approved than disapproved of how the State Legislature was doing its job. And nearly two-thirds of those surveyed disapproved of President Biden's job performance.\par \par \uc2 Kate Zernike contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Kate Zernike contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Volunteers from Planned Parenthood consoled one another after the bill passed, above. Even though recent polling indicates broad support for \uc2  in Iowa, Kim Reynolds, left, the state's Republican governor, is expected to sign the bill. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JORDAN GALE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; PHELAN M. EBENHACK/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230713ej7d00034\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
