\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Religious Freedom Is Invoked In Fight Against  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 1,942 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In lawsuits challenging state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, lawyers for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights plaintiffs are employing religious liberty arguments the Christian right has used for decades.\par \par \uc2 For years, conservative Christians have used the principle of religious freedom to prevail in legal battles on issues like contraceptive insurance mandates and pandemic restrictions. Now, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters are employing that argument to challenge one of the right's most prized accomplishments: state bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the year since Roe v. Wade was overturned, clergy and members of various religions, including Christian and Jewish denominations, have filed about 15 lawsuits in eight states, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and restrictions infringe on their faiths.\par \par \uc2 Many of those suing say that according to their religious beliefs, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be allowed in at least some circumstances that the bans prohibit, and that the bans violate religious liberty guarantees and the separation of church and state. The suits, some seeking exemptions and others seeking to overturn the bans, often invoke state religious freedom restoration acts enacted and used by conservatives in some battles over social issues.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuits show ''religious liberty doesn't operate in one direction,'' said Elizabeth Sepper, a law professor at \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Aaron Kemper, a lawyer representing three Jewish women who are suing to overturn Kentucky's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, said he studied and emulated federal and state religious liberty cases that conservatives won.\par \par \uc2 ''We were like, it works for them, so we thought we should use sections from those cases,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Though most lawsuits have not yet yielded court rulings, there are signs the arguments may have some legal traction. In Indiana, a judge issued a preliminary injunction blocking the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, saying it violated the state's Religious Freedom Restoration Act adopted in 2015 under then-Gov. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , an ardent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponent who is now running for president.\par \par \uc2 Recognizing a potential threat, Oklahoma and West Virginia recently amended their religious freedom restoration acts to explicitly prevent challenges to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans under the acts.\par \par \uc2 Some belief systems, including the \uc2 United Church of Christ\uc2 's, support women making their own decisions in pregnancy. Some, including the Episcopal Church and many branches of Judaism have traditions that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be supported in certain cases, especially where pregnancies threaten women's physical or mental health or involve serious fetal abnormalities. Some faiths do not define life as beginning with conception.\par \par \uc2 The Indiana case was filed by Hoosier Jews for Choice, three Jewish women and a woman with independent spiritual beliefs. Judge Heather Welch of Marion County Superior Court has certified it as a class-action lawsuit on behalf of ''all persons in Indiana whose religious beliefs direct them to obtain abortions in situations prohibited by'' the ban.\par \par \uc2 ''The court has concluded that the plaintiffs' religious exercise is being substantially burdened, that they are suffering irreparable harm,'' Judge Welch wrote in blocking the ban for plaintiffs with religious objections.\par \par \uc2 The state has appealed, arguing that '''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access' is not religious exercise.'' Like other states fighting such lawsuits, Indiana said it has a ''compelling interest'' to prohibit abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''Plaintiffs identify no principle that makes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a religious act any more than countless other actions that they believe to affect their well-being,'' Indiana's attorney general wrote, adding, ''Other acceptable means for plaintiffs to achieve such ends in the context of childbearing include sexual abstinence, contraceptives, IUDs and natural family planning, just to name a few.''\par \par \uc2 Indiana's ban had already been on hold because of a preliminary injunction in a lawsuit citing other objections to the law. On Friday, in a ruling in that lawsuit, an appeals court said the ban could be enforced. But those with religious freedom objections can still seek abortions until an appeals court rules in the religious case, lawyers said.\par \par \uc2 Decades ago, some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups warned that religious freedom arguments might be used to bolster \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. When Congress considered what became the 1993 Religious Freedom Restoration Act, the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2  and the U.S. Catholic Conference raised that concern.\par \par \uc2 ''The Act, if passed, will be used to seek access to abortions,'' the Catholic Conference's general counsel wrote in 1992.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, lawsuits filed by Episcopal, Buddhist, Unitarian Universalist, Jewish and \uc2 United Church of Christ\uc2  clergy say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions violate ''clerical obligations and faith'' and impose ''severe barriers'' on religious belief, speech and conduct.\par \par \uc2 ''We believe God is the source of all life and has caused us to share in the work of creation,'' said one plaintiff, the Rev. Dr. Laurie Hafner, senior pastor of Coral Gables Congregational United Church of Christ. ''The privileges and responsibilities of being part of co-creating,'' she said, mean ''women have the ability and wherewithal to make the decision that's right for them.''\par \par \uc2 Reverend Hafner said she had counseled parishioners deciding whether to terminate pregnancies, including a 14-year-old girl and a woman whose fetus was nonviable. Florida's six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is currently on hold, but, she said, ''what if it gets to that place where I can no longer sit at the bedside or in the living room or in my office with someone out of fear of what might happen?''\par \par \uc2 Within any faith, there may be varying opinions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But many of those suing say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans embed conservative Christian ideology into state law.\par \par \uc2 One Kentucky plaintiff, Sarah Baron, a 38-year-old mother of two and a board member of a Louisville synagogue, said, ''The Torah teaches us that the fetus does not have the same personhood status as the mother until its first breath.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Baron, who belongs to Judaism's conservative denomination, said her age and previous fertility struggles raised risks of pregnancy complications or fetal abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 Under Kentucky's ban, she said, ''I would be unable to make that extremely difficult decision of whether to continue carrying a fetus if the pregnancy is causing severe physical or psychological harm to me or the fetus is nonviable.''\par \par \uc2 ''It's not only cruel,'' she said, ''but it represents a situation where Jewish law may require the pregnancy to be terminated.''\par \par \uc2 Within Judaism, there are differing views, with some Orthodox Jews supporting only very limited circumstances for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But Mr. Kemper, the Kentucky plaintiffs' lawyer, said rabbis from every large Kentucky synagogue have supported the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuits by members of widely known faiths follow a trail blazed by a less conventional religion, the Satanic Temple, which began filing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related lawsuits after the Supreme Court's 2014 \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2  decision exempting family-owned corporations from the Affordable Care Act's mandate that insurance cover contraceptives. The temple, which is recognized by the I.R.S. as a religion and lists 46 American congregations, has lawsuits pending in Idaho, Texas and Indiana, and it recently started the first telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  service operated by a religion, with a goal of using it to challenge \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 A nontheistic religion that construes Satan not as a New Testament evildoer but as the English literary character who battles oppression, the Satanic Temple often employs a strategy of flamboyant provocation, said Joseph Laycock, a religion scholar at \uc2 Texas State University\uc2  and the author of a book about the temple. Its antics make some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters worry that it will stoke anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  sentiment. But some courts have taken its religious freedom claims on various issues seriously, including in a recent preliminary ruling ordering a school district in Hellertown, Pa., to allow its After School Satan Club to meet.\par \par \uc2 Marci Hamilton, a religious freedom expert at the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2  who represents clergy in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights lawsuits in Florida, called the temple's lawsuits ''extremely helpful.''\par \par \uc2 ''They are saying, OK, courts, if you're going to favor the religious right, we're going to show you a faith whose rights are being violated,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 The temple created an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ritual, a recitation of tenets about individual control over one's body and the importance of making decisions based on science. Its general counsel, Matthew Kezhaya, said the ritual strengthens legal claims by linking ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the religion itself'' and establishing a practice ''interfered with by these particular laws.''\par \par \uc2 The temple's telemedicine service is currently available in New Mexico, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal, but it plans to expand to states with bans and religious freedom laws, temple officials said. It has an intentionally inflammatory name, \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 's Mom's Satanic \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Clinic (after the Supreme Court justice who wrote the opinion overturning Roe), but it follows standard medical procedures, employs experienced \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  nurses and is listed by a national clearinghouse of legitimate medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 One patient, Mikayla, 28, who asked to be identified by her first name to protect her privacy, drove from Texas to an Albuquerque airport hotel to use the service , and allowed \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  to observe. During video medical consultations, a nurse practitioner and patient care coordinator discussed effects like cramping and bleeding and urged her to call their 24-hour nurse hotline with questions or concerns.\par \par \uc2 After she received the medication, the process took a different turn. Via Zoom, a minister prompted Mikayla to look in a mirror to reflect on self-empowerment and recite: ''One's body is inviolable, subject to one's own will alone.'' After swallowing the first pill in the two-drug regimen, Mikayla recited a tenet about prioritizing science. The minister advised that after the pregnancy tissue was eventually expelled, Mikayla could recite: ''By my body, my blood. By my will, it is done.''\par \par \uc2 Legal experts said some religious freedom lawsuits seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights might succeed, given recent Supreme Court decisions that ''supported religious exemptions even in cases where there are really strong health and safety issues,'' said Elizabeth Reiner Platt, director of the Law, Rights and Religion Project at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 . Arguments for exemptions might also be persuasive because most \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have some exceptions, like rape, experts said.\par \par \uc2 ''These should be very strong, compelling cases, but I also acknowledge that this is a highly political issue,'' Ms. Platt said.\par \par \uc2 Josh Blackman, a professor at \uc2 South Texas College of Law Houston\uc2  who has criticized the lawsuits, questioned the plaintiffs' legal standing, saying, ''A lot of these women are sort of making prospective claims that, One day, I might be pregnant, and one day, I might have this problem and that might require me to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 He said some plaintiffs could have religiously sincere ''extenuating individual circumstances,'' but that allowing widespread exemptions could undermine the law's larger purpose.\par \par \uc2 Whichever way courts rule could be groundbreaking.\par \par \uc2 ''We're in a completely new landscape,'' Ms. Platt said.\par \par \uc2 Adria Malcolm contributed reporting from Albuquerque.\par \par \uc2 Adria Malcolm contributed reporting from Albuquerque.\par \par \uc2 Mikayla drove from Texas to a hotel in Albuquerque to use the first telemedicine \uc2  service operated by a religion, where she was guided by a Satanic Temple minister, top, and took the medication. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ADRIA MALCOLM FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); The Rev. Dr. Laurie Hafner is suing Florida over its \uc2  bans, saying that according to her faith, ''women have the ability and wherewithal to make the decision that's right for them.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY SYDNEY WALSH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); ''The Torah teaches us that the fetus does not have the same personhood status as the mother until its first breath,'' said Sarah Baron, a Kentucky plaintiff and synagogue board member. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ANDREW CENCI FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A15) This article appeared in print on page A1, A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230703ej7300045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
