\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Indiana Supreme Court Rules a Near-Total Ban on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Is Constitutional\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,045 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The court said the state Constitution provides a limited right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but not a ''fundamental'' one.\par \par \uc2 A ruling by Indiana's highest court on Friday cleared the way for a ban in the state on most abortions from conception.\par \par \uc2 The court said that the state Constitution guarantees a limited right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but not a fundamental one -- that means allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  only when it is necessary to save a woman's life or protect her from a serious health risk.\par \par \uc2 The court's decision removes the temporary injunction on a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the state's Republican-controlled legislature passed last August, after the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade and the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the U.S. Constitution.\par \par \uc2 The Indiana case resembles those in other states, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups have challenged bans passed after Roe's reversal by arguing that varying provisions in state constitutions protect a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Friday, a federal judge ruled that the bulk of a North Carolina \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after 12 weeks of pregnancy could move forward.\par \par \uc2 The Indiana high court said that it was trying to resolve what its opinion called the conflict between ''two irreconcilable interests'': a woman's decision to end a pregnancy, and the state's desire to protect potential life.\par \par \uc2 By saying there was a limited but not a ''fundamental'' right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the justices left open a door for future lawsuits, but only in cases where a woman's life or health was at risk: ''The General Assembly otherwise retains broad legislative discretion for determining whether and the extent to which to prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 ''We celebrate this day -- one long in coming, but morally justified,'' the state's Republican Attorney General, Todd Rokita, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The coalition of providers and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups that had brought the suit, which included \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 , said it was ''devastated'' by the ruling, arguing that it would deny ''essential health care'' to 1.5 million people in Indiana, and especially the poor and others who already face hurdles seeking medical care.\par \par \uc2 ''Now, patients will be forced either to flee the state to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they have the means, seek \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside of the health care system, or carry pregnancies against their will with profound medical risk and life-altering consequences,'' the plaintiffs said.\par \par \uc2 Indiana was the first state to pass a ban after the Supreme Court's ruling striking down Roe. The emotional two-week legislative debate over the measure displayed the divisions -- in Indiana and other states -- among even Republicans who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Some argued that the measure went too far, while others objected that it allowed too many exceptions.\par \par \uc2 The law bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except in cases of rape or incest, as long as the procedure is not done more than 10 weeks after fertilization; cases of fatal fetal anomalies; and when the mother faces risk of death.\par \par \uc2 It also requires abortions to be done in certain hospitals, eliminating the licenses for clinics that specialize in the procedure. Doctors who perform illegal abortions face up to $10,000 in fines and six years in prison.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups had argued that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was protected in the first section of the state Constitution, which endows its citizens with ''certain inalienable rights'' among them ''life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness.''\par \par \uc2 As long as the ban was blocked, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained legal in the state until 22 weeks of pregnancy, making Indiana critical to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for women in nearby states that enacted near-total bans. The state became the center of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate last summer after a 10-year-old rape victim from Ohio, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was banned, sought an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Indiana.\par \par \uc2 The Indiana ban is scheduled to take effect on Aug. 1, but the court's decision left a small opening: A separate lawsuit argues that the ban violates a religious freedom act passed in 2015, because some religions permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A judge in that case put a temporary injunction on the ban in December, and the case is to be heard in September. Until then, those who say that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban violates their religious liberty can still seek abortions.\par \par \uc2 The four out of five justices who agreed on Friday to allow the ban to take effect said, essentially, that the Indiana legislature had the right to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it had a long tradition of doing so. That tradition was interrupted only ''under protest'' in 1973 when Roe v. Wade established that states could not ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the third trimester, and then only in limited circumstances.\par \par \uc2 ''Our laws have long reflected that Hoosiers, through their elected representatives, may collectively conclude that legal protections inherent in personhood commence before birth,'' the opinion said, referring to Indiana residents, ''so the state's broad authority to protect the public's health, welfare and safety extends to protecting prenatal life.''\par \par \uc2 One justice, Christopher M. Goff, disagreed that the state Constitution did not protect a more fundamental right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying that within its ''bundle of liberty rights'' was ''the fundamental 'right to be let alone.''' He wrote that ''many of the liberties Hoosiers take for granted -- the right to vote, to travel, to marry, to educate one's children as one sees fit, or to refuse medical treatment -- stand on federal precedents that are also now vulnerable to reversal.''\par \par \uc2 In his dissent, Justice Goff argued that Indiana residents should be allowed to vote on whether they want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be legal, and urged the legislature to put the question on a ballot measure.\par \par \uc2 A Public Religion Research Institute poll in December found that 61 percent of Indiana residents believed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, and 10 percent believed it should be illegal in all cases, which is in line with public opinion nationwide.\par \par \uc2 An anti-\uc2  rally at the Indiana Statehouse. The state was the first to pass a ban after the U.S. Supreme Court overturned Roe. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KAITI SULLIVAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230701ej710003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
