\par\par \uc2 TIMES INSIDER\par \uc2 The Arts/Cultural Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Finding the Clinics That Closed After Dobbs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Bahr \par \uc2 986 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A current list of clinics that had closed, altered services or relocated didn't exist -- so two Times reporters made one.\par \par \uc2 Amy Schoenfeld Walker, a reporter and graphics editor who covers \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , spotted an article in March by Shalina Chatlani, a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  local investigative fellow.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Chatlani reported that the site of Jackson Women's Health Organization, the clinic in Jackson, Miss., at the center of the Supreme Court ruling that overturned Roe v. Wade in June 2022, was now a luxury consignment shop. And the building -- once pale pink -- had been painted white.\par \par \uc2 She sent a photo of the building to Heather Casey, a photo editor on The Times's National desk whom she had worked with on an article about exceptions to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Ms. Casey suggested there might be a broader story about what had happened to medical clinics and the surrounding communities where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was banned or restricted after the Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 But gathering that data turned out to be more complicated than either of them had anticipated. No one organization had compiled a single, up-to-date list of what had happened to those providers, Ms. Casey said. So they had to create their own.\par \par \uc2 Over the next three months, after examining hundreds of emails, phone calls and sleuthing through surveys and property records, Ms. Walker and Allison McCann, a reporter and graphics editor who covers civil rights, compiled a list of 61 providers that were no longer offering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in 20 states that had banned or restricted the procedure. They also logged where clinics had changed locations or resisted efforts to close, along with clinics that had opened since Roe fell. Their findings were published online last week and appeared in Saturday's newspaper, the first anniversary of the ruling.\par \par \uc2 ''It was a lot of legwork,'' Ms. McCann said. ''But we wanted to know how people were coming to the decisions they did, especially those that stayed.''\par \par \uc2 To build their database, Ms. Walker and Ms. McCann started by contacting organizations like the ANSIRH \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Facility Database and ineedana.com, which shared an initial list of providers sourced from past surveys they had conducted. In May and June, the reporters called and emailed providers to determine the status of each facility: whether it had closed, relocated to a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legal or remained open for other health care services.\par \par \uc2 Often, Ms. Walker said, the contact information for a clinic that had closed or relocated was not current, and its website was no longer active. So they turned to property records to determine whether the space had been sold to a new owner.\par \par \uc2 ''And then we had to figure out if the closure was permanent, or if it had reopened in another state,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 They scanned local newspapers for articles about clinic closures or openings in new locations. It was an ad hoc process, sometimes aided by phone calls from sources who'd heard about the status of other clinics.\par \par \uc2 ''Things were constantly in flux,'' Ms. McCann said. The team had to issue a correction after the article was published, she said, after \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  informed them that one of its facilities in Indiana that had stopped providing abortions in December had resumed offering them -- the week before the findings were published online.\par \par \uc2 As Ms. Walker and Ms. McCann continued their reporting, Ms. Casey began thinking about how to tell the story visually. At first, she said, the goal was to dispatch photographers to take pictures of every clinic -- but that was before Ms. Walker and Ms. McCann uncovered just how many there were.\par \par \uc2 In cases where they could not photograph the clinics, they opted for street views of the exteriors of the buildings. Shannon Lin and Gray Beltran, editors on the Digital News Design team, arranged the images in a grid at the top of the article, with 30 animated tiles that rotated through images of all 61 clinics.\par \par \uc2 ''We had to think how we were going to make the story of 61 gray and brown buildings visually interesting,'' Ms. Lin said. ''We arrived at the grid at the top with the flippy animated squares, because it makes it seem like there's a lot going on without bogging it down.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Walker and Ms. McCann also visited several clinics. Ms. Walker went to three in Alabama and Ms. McCann visited four in Minnesota, North Dakota, Tennessee and Virginia. (Photographers accompanied them at each location.)\par \par \uc2 They were struck, Ms. Walker said, by the haste with which normal operations had been abandoned: At a clinic in Montgomery, Ala., that closed a year ago, wall calendars were open to June 2022, and surgical instruments still lay in a sterilizer.\par \par \uc2 ''A lot of these places just shut down,'' she said. ''It was such a chaotic moment.''\par \par \uc2 It remains to be seen, Ms. McCann said, what the long-term effect on communities will be. Legal abortions fell by six percent nationally in the six months after the ruling, after years of increasing in every region of the country. And a federal lawsuit that challenges the F.D.A.'s approval of a common \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill and its decision allowing patients to receive pills by mail could soon once again upend the landscape.\par \par \uc2 ''It's fascinating that, in the legal chaos of this, a medical procedure is being administered differently depending on where you live and how fast you're able to drive to a state and drive home,'' Ms. McCann said.\par \par \uc2  Services opened as an \uc2  clinic in Montgomery, Ala., in 1978. It closed its doors on June 24, 2022, the day the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY KENDRICK BRINSON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230630ej6u00070\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
