\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Democrats to Use Equal Rights Push to Raise Turnout\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dana Rubinstein \par \uc2 904 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Numerous left-leaning groups are behind a statewide effort to focus attention on a 2024 equal-rights referendum, hoping to increase voter turnout.\par \par \uc2 New York Democrats' substandard performance in the midterm elections last year helped their party lose control of the House of Representatives, threatened its national agenda, and angered national Democrats.\par \par \uc2 In an effort to avoid repeating the same mistake, New York Democrats on Thursday will announce support for a statewide effort to pass a women's rights amendment that they hope will also supercharge turnout in 2024, when President Biden and House members will be up for re-election.\par \par \uc2 Their strategy: Get Democrats to the polls by focusing attention on a 2024 statewide referendum, the New York Equal Rights Amendment, that will explicitly bar New York from using its power and resources to penalize those who have abortions.\par \par \uc2 The campaign, backed by Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  and House Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , among others, plans to raise at least $20 million to spend on television ads, direct mail and organizing in support of the initiative. The effort is designed to complement the House Democrats' main super PAC's $45 million bid to win six New York swing districts next year, including four that just flipped Republican.\par \par \uc2 The campaign is launching a year after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, eliminating the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and ushering in near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in 14 states. It is in step with a national Democratic strategy highlighting the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  record of the Republican Party -- a game plan that Gov. Hochul embraced last year with mixed results, beating her Republican opponent, \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , by only six points.\par \par \uc2 In an interview on Monday, Ms. Hochul argued that the threat to women's \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  represents ''a highly mobilizing force'' that is a proven electoral strategy in New York, her own history notwithstanding. She pointed to the victory last year of Representative Pat Ryan, a Hudson Valley Democrat, over \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 , a Republican who favored giving states the discretion to govern the legality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The New York Equal Rights Amendment campaign is being supported by numerous left-leaning groups, including \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , the \uc2 New York Immigration Coalition\uc2 , the National Institute for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 New York Civil Liberties Union\uc2 , N.A.A.C.P. New York and \uc2 Make the Road New York\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hochul added that the campaign chose to bring the amendment to a statewide vote in 2024, rather than this year as the state is legally entitled, to create space for its message to penetrate. The timing, during a presidential election year, should maximize the campaign's efforts\par \par \uc2 ''Having a ballot initiative in our state is going to drive voter turnout overall, which will definitely help Democrats,'' said Senator Kirsten Gillibrand of New York. ''The biggest reason we lost House seats was because of voter turnout.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jeffries, the House minority leader, took a slightly different tack. ''This has nothing to do with voter turnout and everything to do with ensuring that a woman's freedom to make her own \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care decisions is protected in New York State,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, New York passed the \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act, which protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in New York State. \uc2 Andrew M. Cuomo\uc2 , the governor at the time, regarded the law as necessary in case a more conservative Supreme Court might overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 That act and others render the ballot amendment ''largely gratuitous and symbolic,'' said Dennis Poust, the executive director of the New York State Catholic Conference.\par \par \uc2 ''The reality is, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already widely available and accessible in New York,'' Mr. Poust said. He urged New York to put ''at least as much effort into helping to empower women who might seek to keep their baby if only they had the necessary resources and support.''\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Hochul argues that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act is no longer enough.\par \par \uc2 ''Laws can be repealed,'' she said. ''There's a much higher threshold to change the Constitution.''\par \par \uc2 Voter sentiments about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have begun to shift nationally, in step with a drumbeat of stories about pregnant women being denied medical care and facing near-death experiences. Polls have found that pro-choice Democratic voters are more motivated to vote on the issue, and Republicans less so. Democratic leaders have taken notice.\par \par \uc2 ''Let's be honest,'' said Letitia James, the state attorney general. ''As I travel, \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an issue which comes up over and over again.''\par \par \uc2 Electoral strategy aside, the campaign's supporters also back the initiative on the merits. Other states have passed their own versions of an equal rights amendment, but many generally ban sex discrimination alone, the organizers said. New York's ballot initiative would go further.\par \par \uc2 Not only would it prohibit discrimination on the basis of sex, but also on the basis of ''pregnancy, pregnancy outcomes, \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and autonomy.'' It would ban government discrimination based on age, ethnicity, national origin, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity.\par \par \uc2 Sasha Neha Ahuja, the former national director for strategic partnerships at \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2 , who is spearheading the new campaign, said the amendment would mean that ''for the first time, discrimination of folks on the basis of their \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisions will be categorized as explicitly sex discrimination.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230630ej6u00068\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
