\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump Burnishes Judicial Record at Evangelical Conference: 'This Guy Ended Roe'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neil Vigdor \par \uc2 922 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  told an evangelical gathering that no president had done more for Christians than he did.\par \par \uc2 One year after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  reminded a gathering of evangelical activists in the nation's capital how he had shaped the court's conservative supermajority that ended nearly 50 years of constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Appearing at a Faith & Freedom Coalition gala in Washington on Saturday night, he cited his appointment of three of the six justices who voted to strike down the law as a capstone of his presidency. And he cast himself as an unflinching crusader for the Christian right in a meandering speech that lasted nearly 90 minutes.\par \par \uc2 ''No president has ever fought for Christians as hard as I have,'' he said, adding, ''I got it done, and nobody thought it was even a possibility.''\par \par \uc2 It was the eighth appearance by Mr. Trump in front of the group, whose support he is seeking to consolidate in a crowded G.O.P. competition for the 2024 nomination, though he is the front-runner in the field. He said that Republican voters were skeptical of claims by some of his rivals that they were stronger opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and suggested that the skepticism had arisen on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 ''A woman stood up and said, 'This guy ended Roe v. Wade. How the hell can you go against him?''' Mr. Trump said.\par \par \uc2 A few thousand activists gave Mr. Trump an ovation when he mentioned the ruling, which he said gave conservatives leverage in the ongoing battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Several hundred more filled an overflow room.\par \par \uc2 ''You have power for the first time,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Virtually all of Mr. Trump's rivals in the crowded G.O.P. field appeared during the group's three-day Road to Majority conference at the Washington Hilton. The lineup included Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, Mr. Trump's chief rival, and former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , Mr. Trump's onetime running mate.\par \par \uc2 At a rally in front of the Lincoln Memorial earlier on Saturday commemorating the court's ruling, Mr. Pence urged anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists to continue fighting to place further restrictions on the procedure at the state level.\par \par \uc2 ''Save the babies, and we will save America,'' he said, adding, ''As the old book says, that many more are with us than are with them.''\par \par \uc2 In a speech at the gathering a day earlier, Mr. Pence called on the entire 2024 Republican presidential field to pledge support for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban at 15 weeks -- a ban more extreme than what Mr. Trump has backed so far.\par \par \uc2 David Porter, 64, a Republican from Newport News, Va., who wore a ''Walk With Jesus'' hat to the rally, commended Mr. Trump for his imprint on the judiciary.\par \par \uc2 ''He's my guy right now,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Several times in his speech on Saturday night, Mr. Trump sought to align himself with the faith community and said that it was under attack, much like he was.\par \par \uc2 ''Together, we're warriors in a righteous crusade to stop the arsonists, the atheists, globalists and the Marxists,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Each indictment, he added, was a ''great badge of courage.''\par \par \uc2 ''I'm being indicted for you,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's alliance with the Christian right is a study in political opportunism, one that has yielded prodigious dividends for both.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, evangelical voters helped propel Mr. Trump to successive Republican primary victories in South Carolina and other key states, giving him a pathway to the nomination and ultimately the presidency.\par \par \uc2 The influential electoral bloc demonstrated its willingness to look beyond the impieties of the twice-divorced Mr. Trump, whose extramarital affairs had long been tabloid fodder and who came with a history of supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the 1990s. Evangelical voters bought into Mr. Trump's populist narrative, as well as his pledges to carry out a hard-line reset of the nation's immigration and trade policies and to appoint ''pro-life'' justices.\par \par \uc2 The group collected its returns during Mr. Trump's presidency when he cemented a supermajority on the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has heralded his remake of the nation's highest court as he once again seeks the support of evangelical voters, this time beset by a cascade of indictments, including one in a hush-money case involving a porn star.\par \par \uc2 But even as Mr. Trump has highlighted his role in the right's fight to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, he has repeatedly sidestepped questions about whether he would sign a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban if Republicans managed to steer one through the divided Congress.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Porter, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist from Virginia, said Mr. Trump's evasiveness was concerning.\par \par \uc2 ''Either you stand for what you believe in or you don't,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis, who spoke on Friday at the evangelical conclave, has sought to stake out the right flank against Mr. Trump on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. He criticized the former president for suggesting that a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that Mr. DeSantis signed in Florida was ''too harsh.''\par \par \uc2 Susan Migliore, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist from Falls Church, Va., who said she was religious but not evangelical, said at the Lincoln Memorial rally that she was grateful for Mr. Trump's court picks, but had not decided which candidate she would support in 2024.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230626ej6q00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
