\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Arizona Governor Prevents Local Prosecution of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Providers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Healy \par \uc2 740 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , a Democrat, says she is doing what she can to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in a state with a 15-week ban on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  of Arizona on Friday stripped local prosecutors of the power to criminally charge \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, a move aimed at protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in a narrowly divided political battleground.\par \par \uc2 An executive order signed by Ms. Hobbs, a Democrat, would take authority away from elected county attorneys, a largely Republican group, and transfer it to Arizona's attorney general, a Democrat who has vowed not to prosecute \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups applauded the governor's move as a ''promising and welcome'' measure in a state with a law banning most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood of Arizona said the governor's action would help ease fears and uncertainty among \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the state. Clinics have closed, reopened, curtailed services and lost staff in the uncertain legal landscape since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade a year ago.\par \par \uc2 But some Republican prosecutors said they were blindsided by the governor's move, and called it a power grab that unconstitutionally swept away their authority to prosecute crimes. Local prosecutors said they were still reviewing the order, but raised the possibility of suing to block it.\par \par \uc2 ''Is she going to say I can't prosecute drugs, or homicide cases?'' said Kent Volkmer, the elected prosecutor in Pinal County, a fast-growing area just south of Phoenix. ''For the governor to take that authority from me and say, 'We're going to outsource that to the big city' -- that's highly problematic.''\par \par \uc2 Rachel Mitchell\uc2 , the county attorney in the Phoenix area, called the order an ''outrageous'' effort to undermine local prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 The reaction highlighted the political divide between Arizona's new Democratic governor and attorney general, and elected Republicans in more conservative areas.\par \par \uc2 A similar dynamic has played out in Republican-led states where officials have removed powers from Democrats in blue cities, creating bitter rifts over local control in Florida, Tennessee, Wisconsin and other states.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Volkmer, a Republican, said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases were a low priority, and that he generally did not want to come between women and their doctors. But he said most criminal cases belonged with local prosecutors, and said the attorney general's office was ''certainly not equipped'' to handle \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related cases.\par \par \uc2 The effects of Ms. Hobbs's move may be mostly symbolic. There have not been any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prosecutions in Arizona since Roe was struck down, legal experts said, and most counties in Arizona do not even have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics. The state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers are clustered around Phoenix and Tucson.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hobbs won her campaign for governor last year in part by promising to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and reproductive freedom, but the state's Republican-controlled Legislature has limited what she and other Democrats can do.\par \par \uc2 Unlike the Democratic governors of Minnesota and California, who have passed new laws strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in their solidly blue states, Ms. Hobbs has been mostly limited to vetoing Republican anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts and signing executive actions like the one on Friday.\par \par \uc2 A near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that was passed in the 1800s still sits on the books, despite Ms. Hobbs's promises to call a special legislative session to repeal it. Enforcement of that law has been halted by the courts, but a state law making it a felony for doctors to perform abortions after the 15th week of pregnancy is still in effect. Doctors convicted under the law could have their medical license revoked. Pregnant women are exempted under that law.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers say they are also subject to restrictions including a 24-hour waiting period and limits on prescribing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 The order signed by Ms. Hobbs on Friday also said Arizona would not help other states seeking assistance in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prosecutions, and would oppose efforts to extradite people to face \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related charges in other states.\par \par \uc2 ''I will not allow extreme and out of touch politicians to get in the way of the fundamental right Arizonans have to make decisions about their own bodies and futures,'' Ms. Hobbs said in a statement. ''I will continue to fight to expand access to safe and legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in any way that I can.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230624ej6o0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
