\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Biden Slams  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans; Warns That Privacy Is Next\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Baker \par \uc2 939 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The president sought to galvanize supporters a year after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade as Democrats hope the issue helps them win next year's elections.\par \par \uc2 President Biden denounced on Friday new restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  imposed in Republican-led states in the year since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade and warned that the right to privacy, which has been the foundation for other rights like same-sex marriage and access to birth control, could be at risk next if Democrats do not win next year's elections.\par \par \uc2 Marking Saturday's anniversary of the Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization decision eliminating a national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for women, Mr. Biden decried its ''devastating effects,'' telling an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights rally that women had been deprived of basic health care and noting that some leading Republicans, not content to leave the issue to the states as they had long advocated, are now seeking a national ban on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ''They're not stopping here,'' said Mr. Biden, who was joined at the rally by his wife, Jill Biden, as well as Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and her husband, Doug Emhoff. ''Make no mistake, this election is about freedom on the ballot.''\par \par \uc2 The president collected the endorsement of the nation's leading \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups, Emily's List, \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2  and \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 . While the endorsement was hardly a surprise, the early timing underscored the role that Democrats believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will play in next year's election.\par \par \uc2 Polls show that support for legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen since the Dobbs decision. Democrats argue that it helped them avoid a Republican wave during last year's midterm elections -- ''you all showed up and beat the hell out of them,'' as Mr. Biden put it -- and could be critical to retaining the White House and recapturing the House next year. Republicans are at odds with each other over how much to emphasize the issue, with some worried that it will only hurt them in a general election. But some progressive activists have privately expressed frustration that Mr. Biden has not made it more of a public priority until now.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long been an uncomfortable issue for Mr. Biden, who has cited his Catholic faith as his views have shifted over the years. While a young senator, he declared that the Supreme Court had gone ''too far'' in the Roe decision and later voted for a constitutional amendment allowing states to individually overturn the ruling before reversing himself. He supported the so-called Hyde amendment prohibiting the use of federal funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including through Medicaid, until the 2020 campaign, when he changed his mind under pressure from liberals in his party.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, Ms. Harris has unabashedly joined the battle for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since Roe was reversed, becoming by all accounts the administration's most passionate and effective voice on the issue. At Friday's event, Laphonza Butler, president of Emily's List, praised Mr. Biden's team as ''the most pro-choice administration we've ever seen'' but reserved her most effusive words for Ms. Harris.\par \par \uc2 The rally on Friday, organized with the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 , was part of a series of messaging efforts by the Biden team around the anniversary of the Dobbs ruling. Earlier this week, Dr. Biden hosted a session with women from states that have imposed limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to highlight the consequences even for those not seeking to end a pregnancy. On Saturday, Ms. Harris will deliver an address on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Charlotte, N.C.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's allies on Capitol Hill on Friday also called attention to the issue. House Democrats led by Representative \uc2 Ayanna S. Pressley\uc2  of Massachusetts introduced legislation to require insurance coverage to include \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, shield patients and providers from criminal charges, and affirm a legal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and miscarriage care. The bill has no chance of passing the Republican-controlled House but was meant as a signal to supporters.\par \par \uc2 As he has over the last year, Mr. Biden sought to expand the debate to other privacy-related concerns, ideological ground where he is more comfortable, as he cast Republicans as extremists beyond the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The White House announced Friday that in his third executive action in response to the Dobbs decision, he was ordering federal agencies to look for ways to ensure and expand access to birth control.\par \par \uc2 ''The idea that I had to do that -- I mean, no, really, think about it, think about it,'' he told supporters. ''I know I'm 198 years old but all kidding aside, think about that. I never, ever thought I'd be signing an executive order protecting the right to contraceptives.''\par \par \uc2 He boasted that he had done more to put women in positions of power than any of his predecessors. In addition to making Ms. Harris the first woman to serve as vice president, he noted that he is the first president to have a majority-woman cabinet, pointed to his appointment of \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  as the first Black woman on the Supreme Court and said that he had installed more Black women to federal appeals courts than all of the previous presidents combined.\par \par \uc2 ''Look, we made so much progress,'' Mr. Biden said. ''We can't let them take us backwards.''\par \par \uc2 ''Make no mistake, this election is about freedom on the ballot,'' President Biden said on Friday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY TOM BRENNER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230624ej6o0003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
