\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 One Year and 61 Clinics: The Aftermath of Dobbs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amy Schoenfeld Walker and Allison McCann \par \uc2 2,073 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dozens of clinics have closed or halted abortions since the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. We set out to see what happened to them and the surrounding communities.\par \par \uc2 In the year since Roe fell, 20 states enacted laws banning or restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , forcing a rapid shift in the country's patchwork of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Clinic owners scrambled to adjust, canceling appointments and helping patients travel elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Some clinics relocated, while others stayed open to provide the services they still could. Many simply closed, leaving behind empty buildings.\par \par \uc2 In Milwaukee, a former clinic is for sale for $1 million. The real estate agent says he's had a hard time finding buyers.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, patients still show up, knocking on closed doors. CeeJ, 20, who asked to be identified only by her first name, recently stopped by a shuttered Montgomery, Ala., clinic because she could not afford $50 for emergency contraception at \uc2 Walmart\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Protesters come by, too. The same people who used to picket a Bristol, Tenn., \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider now stand outside a new clinic, less than a mile away in Bristol, Va.\par \par \uc2 At least 61 clinics, Planned Parenthood facilities and doctors' offices stopped offering abortions in the last year.\par \par \uc2 Most were in the 14 states that banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outright. But the uncertainty surrounding laws in several other states also caused providers there to shut down.\par \par \uc2 Physicians said the laws in some states were unclear. Others pointed to the possibility of criminal penalties, including prison time, making the prospect of offering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services risky.\par \par \uc2 About half of the clinics have shifted their focus to other services, such as birth control and prenatal care. Some see patients who have gotten abortions elsewhere for follow-ups. At least a dozen providers moved and opened new clinics in friendlier states.\par \par \uc2 A handful of the buildings, including the clinic at the center of Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, the case that overturned Roe, were sold and turned into something else.\par \par \uc2 A year ago, the Jackson clinic was the last one standing in Mississippi. Today, it is a luxury consignment store offering $1,750 toile drapes.\par \par \uc2 David Carpenter bought the building last July. He spent months renovating, removing the exam rooms to open up the space inside. ''I wanted a clean, fresh start,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Carpenter wasn't interested in talking about what the building once was. Only a few people have turned up looking for the clinic, he said. ''We just explain that it's closed.''\par \par \uc2 Many clinic owners said they couldn't afford to stay open without providing abortions. Instead, they decided to move.\par \par \uc2 Most of the movers were independent clinics, which typically have performed more than half of the country's abortions. They usually offer financial assistance, don't require insurance and won't turn away patients who can't afford the cost of the procedure -- about $500 in the first trimester and $2,000 or more in the second trimester.\par \par \uc2 A clinic in Shreveport, La., closed and moved to western Florida. The director, Kathaleen Pittman, plans to open there once her license is approved, despite the state's recent passage of a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 ''It was a calculated move on our part to help as many people as possible, particularly those from Louisiana,'' Ms. Pittman said.\par \par \uc2 Diane Derzis has helped set up three new clinics in the last year, including one in Bristol, Va., not far from where a Tennessee doctor had provided abortions for 30 years.\par \par \uc2 She did not foresee resistance to the clinic in Virginia, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal. An anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group helped draft a zoning code change that would prohibit local land from being used to end ''pre-born human life.''\par \par \uc2 The proposed change has not passed. But the clinic's landlords are also suing it, claiming they were not told that the facility would offer abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''If this were easy, everybody would do it. This all just goes along with being an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider,'' Ms. Derzis said.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates said they had celebrated clinic closures in their communities, but some said that their work was far from over.\par \par \uc2 ''I was hoping they'd tear it down,'' Doug Lane, 71, a pastor and longtime protester, said of the clinic in Jackson. ''It's still a reminder of what they did there.''\par \par \uc2 In Huntsville, Ala., community members still gather every Thursday morning outside the Alabama Women's Center to pray. The center hasn't performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last June.\par \par \uc2 ''We will come until the building is torn down or someone buys it,'' said Josefina Montoya, 30. ''We are trying to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  everywhere.''\par \par \uc2 Rather than reduce abortions, the bans enacted since Roe was struck down appear largely to have pushed patients across state lines or to find pills online. Illinois, Florida and North Carolina have reported thousands more abortions compared with the months before the Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 Nationally, average monthly abortions fell by about 3 percent in the nine months after the ruling.\par \par \uc2 Even though they can no longer perform abortions, many providers have stayed put, continuing to offer other services.\par \par \uc2 Most providers that have remained open are Planned Parenthood health centers, which have the backing of the national organization and already offered a range of other services.\par \par \uc2 Others, like Dr. Darin Weyhrich, an OB-GYN in Boise, Idaho, who had performed abortions at his private practice since 2002, have also stayed open. Dr. Weyhrich now keeps his stock of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills locked in a filing cabinet.\par \par \uc2 ''It's really hard to feel that you're not able to provide optimal care and all the services you were trained to do,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 In many communities, clinics had offered the only free or low-cost access to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. It's one reason why some are trying to keep their doors open.\par \par \uc2 Terreisha Rancher, 26, recently sat in an exam room at the West Alabama Women's Center in Tuscaloosa, pregnant and uninsured.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rancher was stuck: A local OB-GYN office had said she needed insurance before she could come in. But to apply for Medicaid, she needed proof of her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 At the clinic, Dr. Leah Torres gave her the paperwork she needed to apply for insurance and connected her with a dentist to help her with gum pain.\par \par \uc2 ''If this person wasn't here,'' Ms. Rancher said, ''I don't know what I'd be doing right now.''\par \par \uc2 Across states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, legislators and health care providers are preparing for more pregnancies and births.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Yashica Robinson and Dalton Johnson want to transform their former \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Huntsville into one of Alabama's first birth centers, for patients who want to deliver outside of a hospital. They are waiting for regulators to finalize licensing requirements.\par \par \uc2 They stopped performing abortions last year but continue to run two OB-GYN offices. They have seen deliveries rise 30 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 Many of their patients have little or no insurance and face high-risk pregnancies. ''They can't travel to a state to exercise other options,'' Dr. Robinson said. ''Instead, they start their prenatal care reluctantly.''\par \par \uc2 Some states that restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have at the same time promised to help pregnant women and new parents.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, clinics started closing even before Roe was overturned, after the state passed a restrictive six-week ban in 2021. The state has since extended Medicaid coverage to one year after birth, and gave $100 million to ''alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' like crisis pregnancy centers, which are largely operated by faith-based groups without medical training.\par \par \uc2 Last fall, the owners of a Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic sold the building to a group of local doctors. One was on the board of the McAllen Pregnancy Center, which bought the space days later.\par \par \uc2 The clinic's colorful mural has been painted over. Yolanda Chapa, the center's founder, had rites of exorcism performed inside. She will offer baby supplies, counseling and prayer.\par \par \uc2 ''We are delighted that a place primarily devoted to ending life is no longer open in our community,'' said the Rev. Derlis Garcia, who is on the center's board.\par \par \uc2 Many clinics had been in their communities for decades. For their supporters, the loss is greater than a building.\par \par \uc2 Bekki Vaden, 38, was a surgical assistant at a shuttered Knoxville, Tenn., clinic that had been open since 1975. She took the job after having two abortions there. ''They were so good to me when I was so alone,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 She now works as a truck driver and spends some of her long hours on the road crying in grief and anger, thinking about the patients she can no longer help.\par \par \uc2 ''I've realized how quickly something can get pulled out from underneath you,'' Ms. Vaden said.\par \par \uc2 In at least one case, the people who took over a former clinic said they were trying to honor the building's history.\par \par \uc2 The day before the Dobbs decision, Tammi Kromenaker, the director of Red River Women's Clinic in Fargo, N.D., closed on the purchase of a building two miles away in Moorhead, Minn.\par \par \uc2 The new clinic is larger and has a private parking lot that protesters can't enter. She started seeing patients there last August. ''Our patients are still getting the same services they need,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Still, it's been hard for Ms. Kromenaker to shake the feeling that she lost. Red River had been the only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in North Dakota for more than 20 years.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Kromenaker rents the old clinic building to an artists collective. They've repainted, converted the former exam rooms into studio spaces and adorned the walls with artwork for sale.\par \par \uc2 They added a plaque commemorating the clinic in the lobby, and kept the old sign outside. ''People need to know that it existed,'' said Anj Karna, who runs the collective.\par \par \uc2 Allison McCann reported from Fargo, N.D., and Bristol, Va. Amy Schoenfeld Walker reported from Huntsville, Montgomery and Tuscaloosa, Ala.\par \par \uc2 About the data: There is no single, current list of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and their status. The Times consulted multiple sources and provider surveys, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Facility Database, ineedana.com and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Care Network to compile a list of providers that were no longer offering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in states that restricted the procedure after the Dobbs decision on June 24, 2022. (In Texas, clinics that closed after a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban took effect in September 2021 were also included.)\par \par \uc2 We contacted the providers in May and June 2023 to determine the status of each facility: whether it had closed, relocated or remained open for other services. Data on newly opened \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics is based on Times reporting and local news reports.\par \par \uc2 About the data: There is no single, current list of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and their status. The Times consulted multiple sources and provider surveys, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Facility Database, ineedana.com and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Care Network to compile a list of providers that were no longer offering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in states that restricted the procedure after the Dobbs decision on June 24, 2022. (In Texas, clinics that closed after a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban took effect in September 2021 were also included.) We contacted the providers in May and June 2023 to determine the status of each facility: whether it had closed, relocated or remained open for other services. Data on newly opened \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics is based on Times reporting and local news reports.\par \par \uc2 In Jackson, Miss., what was a clinic is now a luxury consignment store. In Fargo, N.D., an artists' collective took over a clinic space. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY EMILY KASK FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; JENN ACKERMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A14);  (PHOTOGRAPH BY GOOGLE MAPS); Dr. Leah Torres at the West Alabama Women's Center in Tuscaloosa. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENDRICK BRINSON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A14- A15); Terreisha Rancher, who needed help getting Medicaid, in Tuscaloosa. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENDRICK BRINSON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Sammi Cooper, who protests outside an \uc2  clinic in Bristol, Va. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MADELEINE HORDINSKI FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Dr. Darin Weyhrich, at his practice in Boise, Idaho, is frustrated by the ban. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SARAH ANNE MILLER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A15) This article appeared in print on page A14, A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230624ej6o0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
