\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Wyoming Judge Temporarily Blocks Ban On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills, a Week Before Its Start\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 761 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The law was to take effect on July 1. It is the only state law that specifically outlaws the most common \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  method in the country.\par \par \uc2 A Wyoming judge on Thursday temporarily blocked the first state law specifically banning the use of pills for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the most common method in the country.\par \par \uc2 Just over a week before the ban was scheduled to take effect, Judge Melissa Owens of Teton County District Court granted a temporary restraining order, putting the law on hold pending further court proceedings.\par \par \uc2 Ruling from the bench after a hearing that lasted about two hours, Judge Owens said that the plaintiffs, who include four health care providers, ''have clearly shown probable success on the merits and that at least some of the plaintiffs will suffer possible irreparable injury'' if the ban were to take effect.\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already outlawed in states that have near-total bans, since those bans prohibit all forms of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But Wyoming became the first state to outlaw the use of pills for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  separate from an overall ban. The law was scheduled to take effect July 1.\par \par \uc2 The ban, passed by the Legislature and signed by Gov. Mark Gordon in March, makes it illegal to ''prescribe, dispense, distribute, sell or use any drug for the purpose of procuring or performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Doctors or anyone else found guilty of violating this law would be charged with a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in prison and a $9,000 fine. The law explicitly says that pregnant women would be exempt from charges and penalties.\par \par \uc2 In the year since the Supreme Court overturned the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Wyoming's Republican-controlled Legislature has been trying to ban abortions in the state.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Judge Owens temporarily enjoined a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which she said appeared to contradict an amendment to Wyoming's Constitution that guarantees adults the right to make their own health care decisions. An overwhelming majority of Wyoming citizens voted for that amendment in 2012.\par \par \uc2 In March, the Legislature passed another near-total ban on abortions that went into effect without Governor Gordon's signature. That law tried to circumvent the constitutional amendment by declaring that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not health care. Judge Owens temporarily blocked that law soon after it was signed, saying she questioned the state's contention that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not health care.\par \par \uc2 The issue of whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is health care was also a significant aspect of Thursday's hearing on the medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Jay Jerde, a special assistant attorney general for Wyoming, argued that even though doctors and other health providers must be involved in abortions, there are many instances when ''getting the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't implicate health care because it's not restoring the woman's body from pain, physical disease or sickness.''\par \par \uc2 Judge Owens questioned Mr. Jerde's argument. ''Essentially the government under this law is making the decision for a woman,'' she said, ''rather than the woman making her own health care choice, which is what the overwhelming majority in Wyoming decided that we should get to do.''\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs in the case, who are challenging all of the bans in various lawsuits, include the only two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Wyoming; an obstetrician-gynecologist who often treats high-risk pregnancies; an emergency room nurse; a fund that gives financing to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients; and a woman who said her Jewish faith requires access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if a pregnant woman's physical or mental health or life is in danger.\par \par \uc2 A ban on medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have a substantial impact because pills have been the method used in almost all recent abortions in the state, a lawyer for the plaintiffs, Marci Bramlet, told the court. Nationally, pills are now used in over half of abortions. Only one of Wyoming's providers offers the other method, surgical abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''The ban seeks to only ban medication abortions, not all abortions, completely undermining the state's stated goal of preserving prenatal life, and allows surgical abortions which are more invasive physically, financially and logistically,'' Ms. Bramlet told the court. ''The statute tells women, 'You can have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Wyoming but not using the safe, effective, F.D.A.-approved medication available.'''\par \par \uc2 Judge Melissa Owens said if the ban were to take effect, it could cause ''possible irreparable injury'' to some of the plaintiffs. (POOL  BY BRADLY J. BONER) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230624ej6n0000f\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brd